91\t   \"R04 O/codex v29\": \"yes\",\n   492\t   \"R05 P-v4/codex v4\": \"yes\",\n   493\t   \"R06 O/claude v29\": \"split\",\n   494\t   \"R07 S/codex v6\": \"no\",\n   495\t   \"R08 P-v8/codex v8\": \"yes\",\n   496\t   \"R09 P-v4/claude v4\": \"yes\",\n   497\t   \"R10 P-v8/claude v8\": \"yes\",\n   498\t   \"R11 P/claude v4\": \"yes\",\n   499\t   \"R11 P/claude v8\": \"yes\",\n   500\t   \"R12 E/claude v41\": \"yes\"\n   501\t  }\n   502\t }\n   503\t}     1\t{\n     2\t \"R01\": {\n     3\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n     4\t  \"writer\": \"claude\"\n     5\t },\n     6\t \"R02\": {\n     7\t  \"case\": \"P\",\n     8\t  \"writer\": \"codex\"\n     9\t },\n    10\t \"R03\": {\n    11\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    12\t  \"writer\": \"codex\"\n    13\t },\n    14\t \"R04\": {\n    15\t  \"case\": \"O\",\n    16\t  \"writer\": \"codex\"\n    17\t },\n    18\t \"R05\": {\n    19\t  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n    20\t  \"writer\": \"codex\"\n    21\t },\n    22\t \"R06\": {\n    23\t  \"case\": \"O\",\n    24\t  \"writer\": \"claude\"\n    25\t },\n    26\t \"R07\": {\n    27\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    28\t  \"writer\": \"codex\"\n    29\t },\n    30\t \"R08\": {\n    31\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    32\t  \"writer\": \"codex\"\n    33\t },\n    34\t \"R09\": {\n    35\t  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n    36\t  \"writer\": \"claude\"\n    37\t },\n    38\t \"R10\": {\n    39\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    40\t  \"writer\": \"claude\"\n    41\t },\n    42\t \"R11\": {\n    43\t  \"case\": \"P\",\n    44\t  \"writer\": \"claude\"\n    45\t },\n    46\t \"R12\": {\n    47\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    48\t  \"writer\": \"claude\"\n    49\t }\n    50\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && for f in reader-packets/R*.json adjudication/R*.json; do echo \"$f\"; cat \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && for f in reader-packets/R*.json adjudication/R*.json; do echo \"$f\"; cat \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"reader-packets/R01.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Sourced guidance: Alphabet $195-205bn (e1254, F1), Meta $125-145bn (e1549, F2) and Amazon $220bn (e1649, F3). The writer's own Microsoft estimate of $175bn (F6) rests on e922 ($30.88bn for Jan-Mar 2026, up 84%), the e4405 backlog, and the outside four-company tallies less the other three companies. These sum to $715-745bn (F7; the arithmetic checks). A stated 95-100% data-center share (F13), which leans on e1416, e4082, e4766 and e5376 calling the ~$750-800bn totals data-center spending, gives $679.25-745bn (F14; the arithmetic checks). The midpoint is about $712bn, in rung 4. The outside tallies of the four (e5014 $720-745bn, e1856 ~$745bn, e2458 $760bn) and NVIDIA's ~$800bn for five companies (e5376) also point to rung 4 if data-center spending is close to total capital expenditure. Caveats: the Microsoft estimate is largely backed out of those same four-company tallies (see defects). The low end of F14 is in rung 3. The reference F8 is total capital expenditure, set against the data-center quantity without the share deduction that the outcome applies.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\", \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"], \"detail\": \"F7 forecasts the four companies' combined CY2026 capital expenditure at $715-745bn. On F7's path, F6 (Microsoft) rests on F8 ($720-745bn), F9 (~$745bn) and F10 ($760bn), three other dollar values of that same quantity and period. F6 subtracts the other three companies' guidance from them (basis_text: '$720-760bn less ... $540-570bn imply roughly $175-190bn ... I take $175bn'). So the combined total feeds back into its own build, and F7 roughly equals F8 by construction. These are dollar totals used in a subtraction, not a growth rate used only as a benchmark, so the ruling's exemption does not apply. F8, the figure marked as v6's reference, is also an input to the forecast, so their agreement is not independent evidence. The basis also calls $175bn 'between' the ~$155bn run-rate build and the implied $175-190bn, but it is the low end of the implied range.\"}, {\"type\": \"broken structure\", \"figures\": [\"F14\"], \"detail\": \"The outcome F14 is $679.25-745bn. The chosen rung 4 holds 'at least $700bn and below $800bn', so its band does not hold the whole outcome: $679.25-700bn, about a third of the range, falls in rung 3. The rung is chosen on the midpoint (~$712bn). The low end comes from the writer's own 95% lower share bound (F13), although the reasons say that reaching rung 3 'would need a non-data-center share no source indicates'.\"}]}\nreader-packets/R01.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"e1254, e1549 and e1649 supply three company capex figures. The answer adds its $175 billion Microsoft estimate and assumes that 95–100% of combined capex goes to data centers. That yields $679.25–745 billion, with a midpoint of $712.125 billion.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"The midpoint is in rung 4, but the stated forecast extends into rung 3. The answer's own assumptions allow that lower result. e5014 gives total capex, and the cited data-center estimates cover different or unspecified groups, so they do not establish the assumed 95–100% share for these four companies.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"broken structure\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F14\"],\n      \"detail\": \"The answer selects rung 4, which starts at $700 billion, but the linked outcome is a range starting at $679.25 billion.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}reader-packets/R02.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 reported $177.84bn = F1 $81.62bn (e111) + F2 $96.22bn (e5078); Q3 $112bn = F2 x (1 + 16.4%), the F6 stated assumption of a beat smaller than the outside estimate, placed between the guidance midpoint $108bn (e2652) and Pradhan's $113.6bn (e5056), citing the Q2 beat (e3200), Vera Rubin shipments (e2693), ACIE-led growth (e5061) and supply limits (e5067); Q4 $131.04bn = $112bn x 1.17, the F8 stated assumption of a modest pickup as Vera Rubin supply reaches hyperscalers (e2693, e5138, e5061, e2759) limited by memory and lead times (e5067, e5205); total $420.88bn sits in rung 2 [$392bn, $431.2bn), as does the $411bn consensus (e5204). Rung 2 holds even with Q4 flat at Q3 or both quarters at the $108bn guidance (about $394bn-$402bn); the 17% Q4 step (about 93% year over year on e2452's $68bn) is aggressive but stated and checkable.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}, \"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 Data Center $164.27bn = F12 $75.25bn (e109) + F13 $89.02bn (e5079), matching e5309's $164bn; Q3 $104.16bn = 93% (F16) x company Q3 $112bn (F7) and Q4 $122.52bn = 93.5% (F17) x company Q4 $131.04bn (F9), the shares being stated assumptions extending e3399's rising mix (87.9% to 92.5% in Q2, F15) with Data Center leading growth (e5223, e5061); total $390.95bn sits in rung 2 [$350bn, $400bn), as does Morningstar's $361bn (e2295). The forecast is about $9bn below the rung 1 line and inherits v4's 16.4% and 17% sequential assumptions, so the band depends on them, but every step is stated and checkable.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R02.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v4\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 revenue totals $177.84 billion (e111, e5078). The stated 16.4% sequential growth assumption gives $112 billion for Q3, between the guidance midpoint and the outside estimate (e2652, e5056), with the prior guidance beat and Vera Rubin shipments supporting the increase (e3200, e2693). The stated 17% growth assumption gives $131.04 billion for Q4 as production expands and hyperscaler demand picks up (e5138, e5061). The annual sum is $420.88 billion, in rung 2.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    },\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"Reported first-half Data Center revenue totals $164.27 billion (e109, e5079). The forecast raises Data Center's share from 92.5% in Q2 (e3399) to 93% in Q3 and 93.5% in Q4, based on Data Center driving growth and the expected pickup in hyperscaler demand (e5223, e5061). Applying those shares to the shared company forecasts gives $104.16 billion and $122.52 billion. The annual sum is $390.95 billion, in rung 2.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F4\"],\n      \"detail\": \"F4 quotes e2652's $108 billion plus or minus 2% guidance but records the qualifier as none and leaves both bounds empty. The forecast reasoning correctly uses $108 billion as the midpoint, so the omitted qualifier does not change either forecast.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}reader-packets/R03.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F7 = F5 x F6 = 17 x 2 = 34 (USD bn), which falls in rung 3 ($30bn to under $40bn). F5 = F1 x F4 = 20 x 85% = 17, where F1 is the $20bn FY2027 CPU revenue outlook (e3660, quoted correctly) and F4 is the writer's stated assumption that Vera captures 85% of that outlook (rests on F1, e4689, e5044). F6 is the writer's stated assumption that revenue exactly doubles, benchmarked on management's doubling language (F2/e4856, e4691), with competition noted from e5400 and e5403. The reference F3 = F1 x F2 = 20 x 2 = 40 is built only from guidance (e3660, e4856), and the reasons correctly describe it as a floor ('more than double', at the lower edge of rung 2). The rung 3 placement depends entirely on the stated 85% haircut in F4. Without it, 20 x 2 = 40 would land in rung 2. The haircut is the writer's own judgement: the passages give no shortfall against the $20bn outlook and only the absence of volume data. Even so, it is stated openly, and every step can be checked against the passages and the stated assumptions.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R03.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"The answer assumes FY2027 Vera CPU revenue is 85% of NVIDIA’s $20 billion CPU outlook (e3660), yielding $17 billion, and uses management’s doubling outlook as a growth benchmark (e4691, e4856). Doubling $17 billion gives $34 billion, within rung 3.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reader-packets/R04.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v29\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F1 = $25bn of equity investment commitments at 2026-07-26 (e2620; also e3917, e4053). F2 = $18bn committed for the rest of the fiscal year (e2889). F8 is the stated assumption that this $18bn is the part of the July $25bn balance due in FY2027 H2 and that about $17bn of it is paid before year-end (backed by the H1 pace in e4729), so F9 = 25 - 17 = $8bn of the July balance is still open. F10 is the stated assumption that about $8bn of commitments added after July stay unpaid at year-end. It discounts the August deals (Lancium up to $3bn e1919, SB Energy $1.5bn e1957/e2404, Poolside $1bn equity e2207) and the talks with Anthropic (up to $10bn, e4648), Thinking Machines (~$2.5bn, e3934) and Perplexity (e2305, e3455) for uncertain signing and payment dates. F11 = 8 + 8 = $16bn, which is below the $25bn line, so rung 1. Each step can be checked against the cited passages and the stated assumptions. The rung depends on F10 staying below about $17bn. Even if every named deal were signed and none paid, the total (~$19.5bn plus Perplexity) only just passes that line.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R04.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v29\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"e2620 gives a $25 billion July balance. The answer assumes the $18 billion in e2889 is part of that balance and that $17 billion will be funded by year-end, leaving $8 billion. It assumes another $8 billion of later commitments will remain outstanding, based on the announced and potential deals in e1919, e1957, e2207, e2404, e4648 and e3934. Those assumptions yield $16 billion, below the $25 billion cutoff for rung 1.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reader-packets/R05.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Reported Q1 $81.62bn (e111, F2) and Q2 $96.22bn (e5078, F3); Q3 estimate $112.5bn (F6), a stated beat above the $108.0bn +/-2% guide (e2652, F4; high end $110.16bn) and below the $113.6bn trend-repeat figure (e5056, F5), resting on the prior-quarter beat (e3200), Rubin shipments (e2693, e5118) and lead-time drag (e5205); Q4 = F6 x 1.12 = $126bn (F7, F8), a stated 12% sequential assumption benchmarked on the guide's implied pace (108/96.22 = 12.2%) with hyperscale reacceleration (e3406) and supply limits (e5067). Sum 81.62 + 96.22 + 112.5 + 126 = 416.34, inside rung 2 ($392bn to below $431.2bn). Market reference $411bn consensus (e5204, F1) also in rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R05.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v4\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 revenue is $81.62 billion and $96.22 billion (e111, e5078). The $112.5 billion Q3 estimate assumes a beat over the $108 billion guidance midpoint (e2652), tempered below the roughly $113.6 billion historical-pattern estimate by long lead times (e3200, e5056, e2693, e5118, e5205). The stated 12% Q4 growth assumption uses guidance-implied growth as a benchmark and applies it to $112.5 billion, yielding $126 billion (e3406, e2686, e5067, e5205). The four quarters total $416.34 billion, within rung 2. The $411 billion market reference comes from e5204.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reader-packets/R06.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v29\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e2620 (F1: $25 billion of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026) and e2889 (F2: $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of the fiscal year, read as the part of the July book to be funded by year end) give F3 = 25 - 18 = $7 billion of the July book still owed at year end. The stated own estimate F4 ($2-13 billion of commitments signed after the quarter and still unfunded at year end) rests on e1970 (SB Energy second $1.5 billion tranche), e1919 (Lancium milestone-linked $1 billion) and e4648 (Anthropic IPO anchor up to $10 billion): high = 1.5 + 1 + 10 = 12.5 rounded to 13, low = Lancium $1 billion plus about $1 billion other. It is bounded by the stated assumption that deals such as Poolside (e2207), MediaTek (e3474) and SB Energy's first tranche (e1957) fund at closing, and by Huang's statements (e2643, e4487). F5 = F3 + F4 = $9-20 billion, wholly below $25 billion, so rung 1.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R06.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v29\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"e2620 gives the $25 billion July 26 balance. The forecast subtracts the $18 billion in e2889 and adds an estimated $2–13 billion of new commitments still unfunded at year-end. The resulting $9–20 billion range fits rung 1.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"e2889 does not establish that the $18 billion is part of the July 26 balance or that all of it will be paid by year-end. The answer treats both as company guidance, so its $7 billion starting point for the year-end estimate is unsupported.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F2\", \"F3\", \"F5\"],\n      \"detail\": \"e2889 reports $18 billion committed for the rest of the fiscal year. The answer recasts that amount as scheduled payments against the July 26 balance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F10\"],\n      \"detail\": \"e1919 describes an additional investment of up to $1 billion tied to milestones. F10 omits the upper-limit qualifier.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}reader-packets/R07.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"F13 = F11 x F12 = 745 x 90% = 670.5 (rung 3, $600-700bn). F11 ($745bn total capex) is the high end of the Zacks range e5014 ($720-745bn) and sits below e2458's $760bn. F12 (90% data-center share) is the writer's own stated assumption, and it alone decides the band.\", \"why_not\": \"The band depends on the 90% share assumption. Rung 3 needs a share below about 94% (700 / 745). The answer's own cited comparison points the other way: Meta's AI capex of $130bn (e3686) against its $125-145bn total guidance (e1549, midpoint $135bn) is about 96%. Applied to $745bn, that gives about $717bn, which is rung 4. The cut to 90% 'to allow for other capital spending' has no passage behind it, and e4404 (AWS growth) says nothing about how spending splits. Other passages in view treat data-center spending as roughly equal to total capex: e1416 and e4082 put 2026 data-center / AI data-center spending at about $750bn, and NVIDIA's big-five data-center capex of nearly $800bn (e5376) matches BofA's five-company total capex of about $795bn (e5055). Those passages point to a share near 100%, which means rung 4. A reader can check the arithmetic, but not the assumption that puts the forecast below $700bn.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R07.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"The forecast uses $745 billion in total capex, the high end of the estimate in e5014, and assumes that 90% goes to data centers. The answer bases that share on e5014's description of AI infrastructure spending and the comparison between Meta's AI capex and total capex in e3686 and e1549. Multiplying $745 billion by 90% gives $670.5 billion, within rung 3.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reader-packets/R08.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Reported first half Data Center revenue F4 = F2 75.25 (e109) + F3 89.02 (e5079) = 164.27, which matches e5309's $164 billion. Q3: F9 = F5 96.22 (e5078) x (1 + F8 16%) = 111.62. F8 is a stated assumption: a beat above the $108.0bn +/-2% guidance (e2652, and the prior beat in e3200) but below Pradhan's $113.6bn (e5056), because supply is tight (e5067). F14 = 111.62 x F12 93% = 103.8. The 93% assumes a modest rise from the reported 92.5% Q2 mix, which has risen every quarter (e3399), with Data Center leading growth (e5223). Q4: F11 = 111.62 x (1 + F10 15%) = 128.36. F10 rests on hyperscale reacceleration as Rubin supply builds (e3406, e5118), limited by e5067 and e5205. F15 = 128.36 x F13 93.5% = 120.01. Second half F16 = 223.81. Total F17 = 388.08, in rung 2 ($350bn to under $400bn). Every operation checks. The rung holds under weaker assumptions too. With Q3 at the guidance midpoint and the 92.5% mix (99.9, the figure in e3401) and Q4 flat, the year is about $364bn, still rung 2. Morningstar's own $361bn estimate (e2295, F1), the reference, is also in rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R08.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"e109 and e5079 establish $164.27 billion in reported first-half Data Center revenue. Starting from Q2 company revenue in e5078, the answer assumes 16% sequential growth in Q3 and 15% in Q4, with Data Center shares of 93% and 93.5%. The guidance and outside estimate in e2652 and e5056 serve as Q3 benchmarks. e2693, e3200, e5061, e3406, e5118, e5067, and e5205 support the growth assumptions, while e3399, e5059, and e5223 support the mix assumptions. The resulting forecast of about $388 billion falls in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion estimate in e2295 is the separate outside reference.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F14\", \"F15\"],\n      \"detail\": \"Using the stated inputs, F14 is 111.62 × 93% = 103.8066, which rounds to 103.81, and F15 is 128.36 × 93.5% = 120.0166, which rounds to 120.02. The listed results can be reproduced by carrying unrounded company totals, but the answer does not state that convention and the results differ from the products of the listed inputs. The differences leave the forecast in rung 2.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}reader-packets/R09.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F15 = F3 + F12 + F14 = 177.82 + 112.86 + 128.66 = 419.34, in rung 2 ($392bn to below $431.2bn). F3 = reported Q1 $81.62bn (e111) + Q2 $96.2bn (e2649). F12 = Q3 guidance midpoint $108.0bn (e2652) x 1.045, where the 4.5% beat (F11) is a stated assumption resting on the computed Q2 beat of 5.71% (e3200 $91.0bn midpoint vs e2649) and Q4 FY2026 beat of 4.62% (e2452 $65bn vs $68bn), the 12-quarter beat record and 3.1% average above the high end (e5054, e5055), trimmed for sold-out capacity and supply at about 70% of demand (e4911, e3733). F14 = F12 x 1.14, where the 14% sequential pace (F13) is a stated assumption benchmarked below the computed 17.86% Q2 sequential growth (F4) and above the 12% implied by Q3 guidance, resting on Q4 hyperscale reacceleration (e3406), Vera Rubin shipments and orders (e2693), supply commitments rising to $279bn (e2759), and supply constrained through FY2028 (e5067, e3733). All arithmetic checks; the reasons' boundary checks (rung 1 needs Q4 above ~$140.5bn, a 24.5% jump; rung 3 needs Q3+Q4 below ~$214.2bn) also check. Reference F16 = $411bn Wall Street consensus (e4333), rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R09.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v4\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 revenue totals $177.82 billion (e111, e2649). The stated 4.5% beat on the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint gives $112.86 billion, drawing on earlier beats and supply constraints (e2652, e3200, e2452, e5054, e5055, e4911, e3733). The stated 14% sequential growth in Q4 gives $128.66 billion, based on the Vera Rubin ramp and continued supply constraints (e3406, e2693, e2759, e5067). The total is $419.34 billion, within rung 2.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F5\"],\n      \"detail\": \"e2652 gives $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%. F5 preserves that range in its quotation but records no qualifier or bounds in the numeric fields. The forecast correctly uses $108.0 billion as the midpoint.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}reader-packets/R10.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 Data Center revenue from reported quarters e109 ($75.25bn) and e2650 ($89.0bn), summed to $164.25bn (F3, consistent with e5309's $164bn). Q3 company revenue = the $108.0bn guidance midpoint (e2652) times a stated 5% beat assumption (F11, resting on the computed Q2 beat of 5.71% over the $91.0bn midpoint from e3200/e2649, the Q4 FY2026 beat of 4.62% from e2452, and e5054/e5055's 3.1% above the high end, which is about 5% above the midpoint), giving $113.4bn. Q4 = $113.4bn times a stated 12% sequential pace benchmarked on the guided 12% (e5005), giving $127.01bn. H2 company revenue is $240.41bn; a stated H2 Data Center share of 92.8% (F16, from Q2's 92.5% = 89.0/96.2 and the rising share series in e3399) gives $223.1bn; the FY2027 total is $387.35bn, inside rung 2 ($350bn to $400bn). The arithmetic checks, and the rung holds with margin: falling below $350bn would need H2 Data Center revenue under $185.75bn, which is below the guided Q3 pace, and reaching $400bn would need about $235.75bn.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R10.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"Reported Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion in Q1 (e109) and $89.0 billion in Q2 (e2650) gives $164.25 billion for H1. The stated 5% beat on the $108.0 billion Q3 company guidance midpoint (e2652, e3200, e2452, e5054, e5055) gives $113.4 billion. Applying the assumed 12% sequential growth rate, benchmarked to e5005, gives $127.01 billion in Q4. Applying the assumed 92.8% Data Center share (e3399, e5223) to those two quarters gives $223.10 billion in H2 and $387.35 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reader-packets/R11.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 reported: F1 $81.62bn (e111) + F2 $96.2bn (e2649) = F3 $177.82bn. Q3: F9 $112.5bn, an estimate stated in its basis text from guidance F7 (e2652, $105.84-110.16bn) plus part of the beat pattern (e5054 twelve beats of the high end; e5055 3.1% average, i.e. about $113.6bn; e3200 $5.2bn over the Q2 midpoint; e2452 $68bn vs $65bn), trimmed for supply caps (e3115, e4911). Q4: F12 = F9 x 1.12 = $126.0bn, with F11's 12% the assumed pace guided for Q3 (F10, e4899), backed by hyperscale reacceleration (e3406, e4283), commitments of $279bn (e2759) and supply limits (e5067). FY2027 = 177.82 + 238.5 = $416.32bn (F14), inside rung 2 ($392-431.2bn). Every computed figure checks. The band holds even under the answer's own downside: guidance midpoint and 5% Q4 growth gives about $399bn (still rung 2). Reference F19 $411bn (e4333) is quoted consensus, also in rung 2. Reading the '70% full-year growth' reports as FY2028 is argued from e2691 and the arithmetic ($367bn would need Q4 near $81bn). Two small prose slips do not move the band: F11's 'actual sequential growth has run 17-20%' is really about 17-18% (96.2/81.62 = 17.9%; 112.5/96.2 = 16.9%), and the rung-1 example 'Q4 near $138bn (+20% sequential)' falls short of the H2 of about $253.4bn that rung 1 needs from a $112.5bn Q3 (it would need about $140.9bn, +25%).\", \"why_not\": \"\"}, \"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 reported: F4 $75.25bn (e109) + F5 $89.0bn (e2650) = F6 $164.25bn (in line with e5309's $164bn). H2: F17 = F13 company H2 $238.5bn x F16 93.0%, the stated share assumption, = $221.81bn. F16 rests on the share rising every quarter to 92.5% (F15, e3399; 89.0/96.2 = 92.5%), on growth led by the data center business (e5223, e4283, e2721), and on the remainder (Edge Computing, $7.2bn = 96.2 - 89.0) growing a slower 13% (e5059). FY2027 = 164.25 + 221.81 = $386.06bn (F18), inside rung 2 ($350-400bn). This is below the whole's $416.32bn, and the H2 part is a share of the H2 total, so the part cannot exceed the whole in Q3 or Q4. At Q2's 92.5% share the result would be about $384.9bn, still rung 2. The answer's downside (guidance midpoint, 5% Q4 growth, 92.5% share) gives about $369bn, also rung 2. Reference F20 $361bn is Morningstar's own estimate (e2295), not the writer's, and sits in rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R11.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v4\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"e111 and e2649 give $177.82 billion in first-half revenue. The answer assumes $112.5 billion for Q3, a smaller beat than the recent pattern in e5054 and e5055 would imply from e2652's guidance, given the supply constraints in e3115 and e4911. It uses the 12% pace in e4899 as a benchmark for Q4, supported by the expected pickup in hyperscale growth in e4283. That gives $126 billion for Q4 and $416.32 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    },\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"e109 and e2650 give $164.25 billion in first-half Data Center revenue. The answer assumes a 93% second-half share based on the rising share in e3399 and the expected Data Center-led growth in e5223 and e4283. Applying 93% to the same $238.5 billion second-half company forecast gives $221.81 billion for Data Center and $386.06 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reader-packets/R12.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F8 = F1 x (1 + F7) = 20 x 1.85 = 37, inside rung 3 (at least $30bn, below $40bn). F1 is the $20bn FY2027 CPU revenue guidance (e3660), tied to Vera CPU by e4856 and e4691. F7 is the stated 85% growth assumption: management's 'more than double' (F2, e4856) is the benchmark, discounted for time and execution toward the ~50% a year CPU-market pace (F4 from e4687; F5 to F6 from e4857, about 48% a year) and AMD's competing benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404), weighed against broad announced adoption (e4689, e5044, e4692). The reference F3 = 20 x 2 = 40 comes from guidance alone (F1, F2) and sits at the bottom of rung 2; F7 uses the doubling only as a benchmark, so the outcome path carries no second dollar value for FY2028.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R12.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"The FY2027 base is $20 billion (e3660, e4856). The writer assumes 85% FY2028 growth, discounting NVIDIA’s more-than-double outlook for execution risk and competition while allowing for broad Vera adoption (e4856, e4687, e4857, e4689, e5044, e5399–e5404). Applying 85% growth to $20 billion gives $37 billion, within rung 3.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}adjudication/R01.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The build follows from the passages and the answer's stated assumptions. The sourced inputs are read correctly: Alphabet '$195 billion to $205 billion' (e1254), Meta '$125 billion to $145 billion' (e1549), Amazon '$220 billion' (e1649) and Microsoft '$30.88 billion, up 84%' (e922). The arithmetic checks: F7 = 195+125+220+175 = 715 and 205+145+220+175 = 745. F14 = share(F7, F13) gives 715 x 0.95 = 679.25 and 745 x 1.00 = 745, midpoint about 712. Microsoft's $175bn (F6) is stated as the writer's own estimate. It sits between a run-rate build (about $154bn at 15% sequential growth from $30.88bn) and the four-company tallies less the other three companies' guidance (e5014 '$720 billion to $745 billion', e1856 '$745 billion', e2458 '$760 billion' and e2079 'guided roughly $720 billion ... to $745 billion', less $540-570bn). The system text permits this: 'a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it.' The data-center share is written as its own figure (F13, 95-100%), as the format rule requires ('write the deduction or share as its own figure'). It rests on passages that give the same top-five total both as capital expenditures (e5081 'nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures') and as data-center capital expenditures (e5376 'nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures'). Further support: e4082 calls ~$750bn 'AI data center infrastructure spending', e5014 says 'a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure', and e1649 attributes Amazon's increase to 'higher memory chip costs'. So the high share is a grounded assumption, not a relabelled total. The range $679.25-745bn overlaps rung 4 for about two thirds of its width and has its midpoint in rung 4. The reasons say why rung 4 was chosen, which the format rule allows for a range outcome. Caveat on weight: the Microsoft estimate draws on the same tallies that serve as the reference F8, so the agreement between forecast and reference is not independent. With Microsoft at the run-rate figure alone, the midpoint would fall near $693bn, in rung 3. Rung 4 therefore depends on the tally-derived estimate, which the passages support.\"}},\n \"defects\": [\n  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"broken structure\", \"figures\": [\"F14\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The format rule for answers/rung reads: 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.' F14 is a range outcome ($679.25-745bn) and overlaps rung 4's band ('at least $700 billion and below $800 billion'). The reasons give the ground: 'midpoint about $712bn ... the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4.' A rung chosen as an overlapped band, with its reason stated, is what the rule permits. It is not a rung whose band fails to hold the outcome.\"},\n  {\"reader\": \"Reader 2\", \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\", \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"F7 is the only forecast figure of the four companies' combined CY2026 capital expenditure. F8, F9 and F10 are source figures with basis third_party (e5014, e1856, e2458), not forecasts. The format rule reads: 'Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period ... (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine).' They sit beneath F7 only through F6's rests_on. There they serve as one of two benchmarks for an estimate of a different quantity, Microsoft's capital expenditure. The value of the combined quantity in the calculation comes from F7 alone (sum of F1, F2, F3, F6). This is the same pattern as the ruling's benchmark exemption, and the system text endorses deriving an unstated figure 'from the accounting identities in the evidence'. No figure depends on itself: F7 is not in F6's rests_on. The point that the reference F8 also feeds the forecast, so the two are not independent, is about how much weight the answer deserves. It is not one of the listed defect types. Calling $175bn 'between' $155bn and $175-190bn is wording at an inclusive edge, not a defect.\"},\n  {\"reader\": \"Reader 2\", \"type\": \"broken structure\", \"figures\": [\"F14\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The format rule for answers/rung reads: 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.' The band need not hold the whole range. F14 ($679.25-745bn) overlaps rung 4 ($700-800bn), and the reasons state why that band was chosen (midpoint about $712bn; most of the range in rung 4). The low end resting on the writer's own 95% bound affects weight, not structure. The share is written as its own figure (F13), as the format rule requires.\"}\n ]}\nadjudication/R01.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"why\": \"The Alphabet, Meta and Amazon guidance in e1254, e1549 and e1649 sums to $540–570 billion. Adding the stated $175 billion Microsoft estimate gives $715–745 billion in total capex. Applying the stated 95–100% data-center share gives $679.25–745 billion. The midpoint of $712.125 billion and most of the range fall in rung 4. The exact share remains an assumption. e5014 says only that a 'large portion' goes to AI infrastructure, and e5376 covers five companies.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n      \"type\": \"broken structure\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F14\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"The format permits 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.' F14 overlaps rung 4 from $700 billion to $745 billion, and the answer explains its choice using the midpoint and the portion of the range in that band.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"The format says 'outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine.' F8, F9 and F10 are outside totals used to estimate Microsoft's contribution in F6 alongside its spending run-rate. F7 is the only forecast of the combined total that supplies F14. Because F8 helps inform F6, its agreement with F7 carries no independent weight.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n      \"type\": \"broken structure\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F14\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"The format requires 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.' The stated 95% share does allow an outcome in rung 3, and the answer acknowledges that overlap. Its stated reason for choosing rung 4 is that the midpoint and most of the range fall there.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}adjudication/R02.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"F3 = F1 $81.62bn (e111 'First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion') + F2 $96.22bn (e5078 'Q2 FY2027 revenue landed at $96.22 billion') = $177.84bn. F7 = apply_growth(96.22, 16.4) = 112.0, under the stated F6 assumption of a beat above the guidance midpoint ($108.0 billion, e2652) but smaller than the outside estimate (roughly $113.6 billion, e5056), backed by the prior beat (e3200 'exceeded that midpoint by $5.2 billion'), Vera Rubin production shipments (e2693) and ACIE-led growth (e5061), limited by supply (e5067). F9 = apply_growth(112, 17) = 131.04, under the stated F8 assumption that hyperscaler demand picks up in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e5061 'hyperscaler demand could pick up more strongly in the fourth quarter'; e5138; e2759), a pace close to Q2's 18% sequential rise (e2649). F10 = H1 + Q3 + Q4 = 177.84 + 112 + 131.04 = 420.88, in rung 2 [$392bn, $431.2bn), as is the $411bn consensus reference (e5204). The rung holds under weaker inputs: Q3 and Q4 both at the $108bn midpoint give about $393.8bn, and Q4 at the analysts' 65% year-over-year growth on $68bn (e5162, e2452, about $112.2bn) gives about $402bn, both still rung 2. Every step is a stated assumption or correct arithmetic; one forecast figure (F7, F9) supplies each shared quarter.\"}, \"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"F14 = F12 $75.25bn (e109 'the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion') + F13 $89.02bn (e5079 'Data Center alone at $89.02 billion') = $164.27bn, matching e5309's '$164 billion' for the first six months. F18 = share(F7 112, F16 93%) = 104.16 and F19 = share(F9 131.04, F17 93.5%) = 122.52; the shares are stated estimates extending the rising mix in e3399 ('87.9% ... 89.8% ... 91.5% ... 92.2% ... then 92.5% in the quarter just reported') with growth 'driven primarily by continued strength in the AI data center business' (e5223) and ACIE and hyperscaler demand (e5061). F20 = 164.27 + 104.16 + 122.52 = 390.95, in rung 2 [$350bn, $400bn), as is Morningstar's $361bn reference (e2295). The part stays below the whole in Q3 (104.16 < 112), Q4 (122.52 < 131.04) and the year (390.95 < 420.88), and it uses the family's single company forecasts for Q3 and Q4. The forecast inherits v4's sequential assumptions, but the rung is robust: both company quarters at the $108bn midpoint with a 92.5% share give about $364bn, still rung 2, and rung 1 would need second-half Data Center revenue of at least about $235.7bn.\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F4\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The quote_number rule permits exactly what F4 does: \\\"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value.\\\" F4 copies e2652's expression in full ('$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%') and gives its midpoint, 108, as value; low and high are correctly null because the value rule says 'Give value, or low and high, never both.' The qualifier rule asks for 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none'. e2652's quote, 'Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%', writes no qualifier before the number, and the 'plus or minus 2%' belongs to the quoted number expression itself, so 'none' is right. The defect type excludes differences 'allowing what the format rules permit', so no number, scale, sign or qualifier is misread.\"}]}\nadjudication/R02.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v4\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"why\": \"Reported first-half revenue was $177.84 billion (e111, e5078). The prior guidance beat and early Vera Rubin shipments support the stated $112 billion Q3 forecast, between NVIDIA’s $108 billion guidance midpoint and Pradhan’s roughly $113.6 billion estimate (e3200, e2693, e2652, e5056). Full production and a possible pickup in hyperscaler demand support the assumed 17% sequential growth to $131.04 billion in Q4, with supply constraints limiting upside (e5138, e5061, e5067, e5205). The annual total of $420.88 billion falls in rung 2.\"\n    },\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"why\": \"Reported first-half Data Center revenue was $164.27 billion (e109, e5079). The segment’s rising share reached 92.5% in Q2 (e3399), and its expected role as the main growth driver supports the stated assumptions of 93% in Q3 and 93.5% in Q4 (e5223, e5061, e5138). Applying those shares to the same company-wide forecasts gives $104.16 billion and $122.52 billion. The annual total of $390.95 billion falls in rung 2.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F4\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"e2652 says, “Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.” F4 copies the full number expression, and the format explicitly allows “low and high or its midpoint as value.” The qualifier field covers words immediately before the number or “or more” or “or less” immediately after it. The quoted expression has no separate qualifier of that kind, so the midpoint value of 108, null bounds, and qualifier of “none” comply.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}adjudication/R06.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v29\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"e2620 (NVIDIA, 2026-08-26 filing) gives 'equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026'. e2889 is NVIDIA's own statement on the same day: 'it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year'. 'Has ... committed' means money already in its commitment book, and 'for the rest of its fiscal year' gives when it is due. So reading the $18 billion as the part of the July book scheduled to be funded in Q3-Q4 is the natural reading of the passage words, not an invented one. The answer states it as its assumption: 'NVIDIA said $18 billion (F2) of its commitments is to be funded in the rest of FY2027 ... it leaves about $7 billion (F3) of the July book still owed at year end'. It also names the risk that would move it ('a large part of the $18 billion slipping into next year'). The system text calls current-period guidance 'the strongest forecast input'. Forecasting that a stated schedule holds is the judgement the writer is asked to make, and a passage that proves it in advance is not required. F4 ($2-13 billion) is an estimate marked as the writer's own. Its basis_text sizes it from named staged or unclosed deals: SB Energy's second $1.5 billion (e1970), Lancium's $1 billion milestone tranche (e1919) and the 'as much as $10 billion' Anthropic anchor (e4648). It argues that closed deals such as Poolside (e2207), MediaTek (e3474) and SB Energy's first tranche (e1957) fund at closing, and it cites Huang's statements (e2643, e4487). The arithmetic holds: 25 - 18 = 7, then 7 + 2 = 9 and 7 + 13 = 20. The $9-20 billion range lies wholly below $25 billion, inside rung 1's band ('below $25 billion'). The forecast follows from the passages and the stated assumptions.\"}},\n \"defects\": [\n  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F2\", \"F3\", \"F5\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"Number misread covers 'a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier'. F2's quote_number is '$18 billion' with qualifier none, and e2889's quote writes '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year'. Number, scale and sign match, and no qualifier comes before the number. The period FY2027 H2 matches 'the rest of its fiscal year' as said after the Q2 close of July 26. Reading the $18 billion as funding due against the July book is an interpretation of the measure, already settled under the member's support. It is not a misread number. F3 and F5 are computed figures, not source figures, so this defect type cannot apply to them. F3 = difference(25, 18) = 7 and F5 = sum(7, 2-13) = 9-20 are both correct.\"},\n  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F10\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The format rule says the qualifier is 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number'. e1919's quote writes 'NVIDIA will spend up to $3 billion in Lancium ... followed by an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting particular go'. 'Up to' qualifies the $3 billion total. The $1 billion is preceded only by 'an additional', which is not a qualifier. Only the passage summary (not the quote) says 'up to $1 billion'. F10's value 1, quote_number '$1 billion' and qualifier 'none' match what the quote writes.\"}\n ]}\nadjudication/R06.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v29\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"why\": \"The $9–20 billion range fits rung 1, but it relies on subtracting $18 billion from the July 26 balance. e2620 reports $25 billion of commitments as of July 26. e2889 says NVIDIA has '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.' Neither passage says the full $18 billion was included in the July balance and will be paid by year-end. The answer presents that assumption as NVIDIA's payment schedule.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F2\", \"F3\", \"F5\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"e2889 writes '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.' F2 preserves the quoted number, scale, sign and immediate qualifier. The defect definition is limited to those features. The unsupported step is treating the commitment as a scheduled payment from the July balance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F10\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"The e1919 quote says 'an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting' milestones. The format rule takes the qualifier from the words immediately before the number, with an exception for 'or more' or 'or less' immediately after it. The quote's 'up to' qualifies the $3 billion total. The passage summary uses 'up to' for the tranche, but the quoted tranche amount has no such qualifier, and F10 preserves its milestone condition.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}adjudication/R07.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"why\": \"The rung rests entirely on F12's 90% data-center share: share(745, 90) = 670.5, rung 3, but rung 3 holds only while the share is below 700 / 745 = 94.0%. The shown passages put the share near 100%, not 90%: e1947 says the four 'will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year', against the same four's 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion' of capital expenditures (e5014; e2079), which is at least 98%; NVIDIA's top-five figure is written both as total 'CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers ... nearly $800 billion in 2026' (e2759) and as 'nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026' (e5376), matching BofA's five-company total capex of 'roughly $795 billion' (e5055); e4082 puts 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year' and e1416 says tech giants 'will spend an estimated $750 billion this year'. The answer's own stated basis, Meta's '$130 billion of AI capex' (e3686) against its '$125 billion to $145 billion' guidance (e1549), gives 90% to 104%, about 96% at the midpoint, which is above the 94% cutoff. The cut to 90% to 'allow for other capital spending' has no passage behind it (e4404 says only that 'Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited'), so the forecast below $700 billion does not follow from the passages; with any share the passages support (94% or more), the same F11 of $745 billion gives rung 4. The arithmetic is correct and the share is written as its own figure as the format rule asks ('write the deduction or share as its own figure'), so no format defect arises; the failure is that the decisive assumption is not supported.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nadjudication/R07.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"why\": \"Zacks supports $745 billion in total spending, describing a “large portion” as directed toward AI infrastructure. Applying the stated 90% share gives $670.5 billion, which is in rung 3. But rung 3 requires a share below about 94%, and the cited passages give no reason to expect the four companies’ share to fall below that cutoff. Meta’s $130 billion in AI spending is about 96% of the midpoint of its total spending guidance, and the AWS growth passage gives no spending breakdown. The 90% assumption is explicit, but the evidence does not support the deduction that puts the forecast in rung 3.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}adjudication/R08.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"Every step checks against its passages and the stated assumptions. The reported first half is F2 $75.25 billion (e109, 'the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion') plus F3 $89.02 billion (e5079, 'Data Center alone at $89.02 billion'), giving F4 = 164.27, which matches e5309's '$164 billion'. Q3 company revenue F9 = 96.22 (e5078) x 1.16 = 111.62. F8's 16% is a stated estimate: guidance implies 12% at the midpoint (e4899, 'representing 12% sequential growth from the midpoint'), and Pradhan's beat extrapolation implies about 18% (e5056, 'roughly $113.6 billion'). The writer places the estimate between them, citing the prior beat (e3200), Rubin shipments (e2693) and supply limits (e5067). Q4 company revenue F11 = 111.62 x 1.15 = 128.36. F10 is a stated estimate that rests on e3406 ('growth in its hyperscale business ... should reaccelerate in the fiscal fourth quarter as Vera Rubin supply builds'). The Data Center shares of 93% and 93.5% are stated as modest rises from the 92.5% Q2 share, which 'rose ... then 92.5%' every quarter (e3399). F14 = 103.8, F15 = 120.01, F16 = 223.81 and F17 = 388.08, which falls in rung 2 ('at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion'). The Q4 growth step is generous next to analysts' 65% Q4 year-over-year growth (e5162), but it is a stated estimate and does not decide the rung. With Q3 at the guidance midpoint, the 92.5% share (99.9, e3401) and Q4 flat, the year is 164.27 + 99.9 + 99.9 = 364.07, still rung 2. Rung 1 would need about $236 billion in the second half, roughly 27% sequential company growth in Q4, which nothing shown supports. The reference F1 is Morningstar's own estimate (e2295, 'we estimate it will be $361 billion in fiscal 2027'). It is a quoted outside figure and sits in rung 2. The Q3 guidance (F6) and Pradhan's figure (F7) appear only in F8's rests_on. On the arithmetic path from F17, the first Q3 company figure reached is F9 and the first Q4 figure is F11, so each quarter has one forecast.\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\", \"figures\": [\"F14\", \"F15\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The format rule says: 'The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %).' F14 = share(F9 111.62, F12 93) = 111.62 x 93 / 100 = 103.8066. The listed 103.8 is 0.0066 off, far inside the allowed 0.52. F15 = share(F11 128.36, F13 93.5) = 120.0166. The listed 120.01 is 0.0066 off, inside the allowed 0.60. The rule allows these differences, so neither figure is wrong arithmetic. Downstream, F16 = 103.8 + 120.01 = 223.81 and F17 = 164.27 + 223.81 = 388.08 are both exact.\"}]}\nadjudication/R08.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"why\": \"The reported first half totals $164.27 billion (e109, e5079). NVIDIA's Q3 guidance, the previous guidance beat and the start of Rubin shipments support the assumed 16% sequential growth (e2652, e3200, e2693). The live Rubin deployment and expected pickup in hyperscaler growth support the assumed 15% Q4 growth, with supply constraints limiting the pace (e5118, e3406, e5067, e5205). Data Center's rising share and its role as the main growth driver support the assumed 93% and 93.5% shares (e3399, e5223). The resulting $388.08 billion forecast falls in rung 2, from $350 billion to under $400 billion. Morningstar's $361 billion estimate is the outside reference (e2295).\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F14\", \"F15\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"The format rule says, 'The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %).' The stated inputs give $103.8066 billion for F14 and $120.0166 billion for F15. Each listed value differs by $0.0066 billion, within the allowed tolerance.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}adjudication/R09.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"Every figure resolves and computes: F3 = 81.62 (e111 '$81.62 billion') + 96.2 (e2649 '$96.2 billion') = 177.82; F12 = apply_growth(108.0, 4.5) = 112.86; F14 = apply_growth(112.86, 14) = 128.66; F15 = 177.82 + 112.86 + 128.66 = 419.34, inside rung 2 (at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion). The two estimates are stated as the writer's own with their assumptions. F11's 4.5% beat rests on the computed Q2 beat of 5.71% (e3200 '$91.0 billion' vs e2649) and the Q4 FY2026 beat of 4.62% (e2452 '$65 billion' / '$68 billion'), plus e5054 'exceeded the high end of its revenue guidance in each of the past 12 quarters' and e5055 '3.1% above the high end', trimmed for e4911 'sold out of capacity' and e3733 'we have supply for 70%'. F13's 14% Q4 pace is benchmarked below the computed 17.86% Q2 growth (F4) and rests on e3406 'should reaccelerate in the fiscal fourth quarter as Vera Rubin supply builds', e2693, e2759 and e5067 'supply to remain constrained through fiscal 2028'. The Q3 shared quantity's first figure on the outcome path is the forecast F12, and guidance F5 sits beneath it ('outside figures beneath it ... are fine'); the 12% implied by Q3 guidance is named only as a benchmark in words, not an input. The writer's reading that the '70% growth' reports belong to FY2028 is argued from e2691 'Customers forecasts point to our growth, doubling next year ... we expect to grow approximately 70%' and the arithmetic (215.9 x 1.7 = 367.0, leaving Q4 near $81 billion, below Q2), which holds. Reference F16 = $411 billion is a quoted consensus (e4333 'Wall Street analysts expect $411 billion in revenue'), rung 2.\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F5\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The quote writes 'Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.' The format rule for quote_number says \\\"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value\\\", so value 108.0 with quote_number '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%' is a permitted form, not a misread. The qualifier rule covers 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or \\\"or more\\\" / \\\"or less\\\" just after); none when there is none': nothing stands before '$108.0 billion', and 'plus or minus 2%' belongs to the number expression copied in full, so qualifier 'none' is correct. Number, scale and sign match the quote; Reader 1 itself notes the midpoint is used correctly.\"}]}\nadjudication/R09.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v4\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"why\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 revenue totals $177.82 billion (e111, e2649). The assumed 4.5% beat on the $108.0 billion Q3 guidance midpoint gives $112.86 billion. Prior guidance beats support that assumption, while limited supply tempers it (e2452, e3200, e5054, e5055, e4911, e3733). The assumed 14% sequential growth gives Q4 revenue of $128.66 billion. Vera Rubin shipments and expected Q4 hyperscale reacceleration support continued growth, while supply constraints support a slower pace than Q2 (e2693, e3406, e5067). The resulting $419.34 billion falls within rung 2, from $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F5\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"F5 copies the full expression from e2652, \\\"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\\" The format rule explicitly permits a plus-or-minus expression to be \\\"given as low and high or its midpoint as value.\\\" F5 gives the midpoint, and the passage has no qualifier immediately before the number.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\np=Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\nfor rid,k in json.loads((p/'\\\\''reader-key.json'\\\\'').read_text()).items():\n a=p/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/(k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']+'\\\\''.json'\\\\'')\n d=json.loads(a.read_text().strip().removeprefix('\\\\''\"'```json'\"'\\\\'').removesuffix('\\\\''\"'```'\"'\\\\''))\n print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',rid,k)\n for a in d['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']: print('\\\\''Answer:'\\\\'', a)\n for f in d['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n  print(f['\\\\''id'\\\\''],f['\\\\''kind'\\\\''],f['\\\\''what'\\\\''],f.get('\\\\''value'\\\\''),f.get('\\\\''low'\\\\''),f.get('\\\\''high'\\\\''), '\\\\'' '\\\\''.join(f'\\\\''{key}={v}'\\\\'' for key,v in f.items() if key not in ('\\\\''id'\\\\'','\\\\''kind'\\\\'','\\\\''what'\\\\'','\\\\''unit'\\\\'','\\\\''value'\\\\'','\\\\''low'\\\\'','\\\\''high'\\\\'') and v not in ('\\\\'''\\\\'',None,[],'\\\\''none'\\\\'')))\n print('\\\\''Other:'\\\\'', {k:v for k,v in d.items() if k not in ('\\\\''figures'\\\\'','\\\\''answers'\\\\'')})\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\np=Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\nfor rid,k in json.loads((p/'\\\\''reader-key.json'\\\\'').read_text()).items():\n a=p/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/(k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']+'\\\\''.json'\\\\'')\n d=json.loads(a.read_text().strip().removeprefix('\\\\''\"'```json'\"'\\\\'').removesuffix('\\\\''\"'```'\"'\\\\''))\n print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',rid,k)\n for a in d['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']: print('\\\\''Answer:'\\\\'', a)\n for f in d['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n  print(f['\\\\''id'\\\\''],f['\\\\''kind'\\\\''],f['\\\\''what'\\\\''],f.get('\\\\''value'\\\\''),f.get('\\\\''low'\\\\''),f.get('\\\\''high'\\\\''), '\\\\'' '\\\\''.join(f'\\\\''{key}={v}'\\\\'' for key,v in f.items() if key not in ('\\\\''id'\\\\'','\\\\''kind'\\\\'','\\\\''what'\\\\'','\\\\''unit'\\\\'','\\\\''value'\\\\'','\\\\''low'\\\\'','\\\\''high'\\\\'') and v not in ('\\\\'''\\\\'',None,[],'\\\\''none'\\\\'')))\n print('\\\\''Other:'\\\\'', {k:v for k,v in d.items() if k not in ('\\\\''figures'\\\\'','\\\\''answers'\\\\'')})\n'\"","aggregated_output":"\n R01 {'case': 'S', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v6', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 4, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome in the upper part of the build but not near $800bn, since the $800bn figures cover five companies. Downward pressure: about $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a multi-year project value across all developers, not 2026 spending by these four, and the labour, grid and financing warnings (e1545, e5292, e2081, e4807) carry no number that would cut 2026 spending below $700bn. Repeats of the same claims (e4790, e1947, e2079 and the NVIDIA restatements) are counted once.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e5014', 'e1254', 'e1549', 'e1649', 'e922', 'e4405', 'e1416', 'e4082', 'e2759', 'e5376', 'e4567', 'e4566', 'e1856', 'e2458', 'e122', 'e1545', 'e5292', 'e2081', 'e4807'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e1254', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1549', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1649', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e922', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4405', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5014', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1856', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2458', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1416', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4082', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2759', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e122', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4567', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4790', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1947', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2079', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5376', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5081', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5105', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5114', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5358', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4766', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4514', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4564', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5055', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3686', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4566', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1545', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5292', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2081', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4807', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3142', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5150', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4417', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5170', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5200', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': []}\nF1 source {'population': 'Alphabet', 'measure': 'capital expenditure', 'scope': 'company total', 'period': 'CY2026'} None 195 205 basis=guidance evidence_id=e1254 quote_number=$195 billion to $205 billion\nF2 source {'population': 'Meta Platforms', 'measure': 'capital expenditure', 'scope': 'company total (fiscal year equals calendar year)', 'period': 'CY2026'} None 125 145 basis=guidance evidence_id=e1549 quote_number=$125 billion to $145 billion\nF3 source {'population': 'Amazon', 'measure': 'capital expenditure', 'scope': 'company total', 'period': 'CY2026'} 220 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e1649 quote_number=$220 billion\nF4 source {'population': 'Microsoft', 'measure': 'capital expenditure', 'scope': 'company total (fiscal Q3 FY2026, January-March 2026)', 'period': 'CY2026 Q1'} 30.88 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e922 quote_number=$30.88 billion\nF5 source {'population': 'Microsoft', 'measure': 'capital expenditure growth, year over year', 'scope': 'company total (fiscal Q3 FY2026, January-March 2026)', 'period': 'CY2026 Q1'} 84 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e922 quote_number=84%\nF6 estimate {'population': 'Microsoft', 'measure': 'capital expenditure', 'scope': 'company total', 'period': 'CY2026'} 175 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate. Microsoft states no calendar-2026 total in the evidence. Its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% year over year, and its $678bn Azure backlog supports a continuing ramp: carrying the growth pace through the year at roughly 15% sequential increases a quarter gives about $155bn on the reported quarter's basis (not the flat annualised $124bn). The outside tallies of the four companies' guided 2026 spending ($720-760bn) less the other three companies' guidance ($540-570bn) imply roughly $175-190bn at their centres, and those tallies include spending (such as leases) the single reported quarter may not. I take $175bn, between the two. rests_on=['F4', 'F5', 'F1', 'F2', 'F3', 'F8', 'F9', 'F10', 'e4405']\nF7 computed {'population': 'four largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'capital expenditure', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} None 715 745 basis=forecast inputs=['F1', 'F2', 'F3', 'F6'] operation=sum\nF8 source {'population': 'four largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'capital expenditure', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} None 720 745 basis=third_party evidence_id=e5014 quote_number=$720 billion to $745 billion qualifier=roughly reference_for=['v6']\nF9 source {'population': 'four largest hyperscalers', 'measure': 'capital expenditure', 'scope': 'combined, tracking', 'period': 'CY2026'} 745 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1856 quote_number=$745 billion qualifier=near\nF10 source {'population': 'top four hyperscalers', 'measure': 'capital expenditure', 'scope': 'combined, planned', 'period': 'CY2026'} 760 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2458 quote_number=$760 billion\nF11 source {'population': 'tech giants', 'measure': 'data-center spending', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} 750 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1416 quote_number=$750 billion\nF12 source {'population': 'five largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'data-center capex', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} 800 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5376 quote_number=$800 billion qualifier=nearly\nF13 estimate {'population': 'four largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'data-center share of capital expenditure', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} None 95 100 basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate data-center line, so reported capital expenditure is how the outcome will be measured in practice. rests_on=['F11', 'F12', 'e4082', 'e5014', 'e1649']\nF14 computed {'population': 'four largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'data-center capex', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} None 679.25 745 basis=forecast inputs=['F7', 'F13'] operation=share outcome_for=['v6']\nOther: {}\n\n R02 {'case': 'P', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v4', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 2, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"I forecast $420.88 billion, in rung 2. The $411 billion analyst consensus also sits in rung 2. NVIDIA reported $177.84 billion in the first half. The last quarter's guidance beat and Vera Rubin shipments support a Q3 forecast of $112 billion against the $108 billion guidance midpoint. I expect $131.04 billion in Q4 as more Vera Rubin systems reach hyperscalers. Current spending supports that ramp. The higher calendar 2027 spending view mainly supports later deliveries. Memory shortages and long equipment lead times limit the upside this fiscal year. The export-policy views reduce the risk of additional restrictions, and limited H200 authorization offers a small chance of late sales. The open Commerce investigation gives no direction for this year's shipments.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e5204', 'e111', 'e5078', 'e2652', 'e3200', 'e5056', 'e2693', 'e2759', 'e5061', 'e5138', 'e5067', 'e5205'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e5204', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e111', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5078', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2652', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3200', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5056', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2693', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2759', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5061', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5138', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5067', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5205', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v16', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v22', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v9', 'push': 'toward_first'}]}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v8', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 2, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"I forecast $390.95 billion, in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion estimate is the outside reference and also sits in rung 2. NVIDIA reported $164.27 billion in the first half. I allocate 93% of the company-wide Q3 forecast to Data Center, or $104.16 billion, as AI cloud, industrial and enterprise sales drive most of the segment's growth. I raise the share to 93.5% in Q4, or $122.52 billion, as Vera Rubin supply expands and hyperscaler sales pick up. Strong hyperscaler spending and the prospect of limited H200 authorization support the forecast, with most of the calendar 2027 spending impact falling later. The outlook for fewer new export restrictions eases downside risk, while the Commerce investigation remains open. Supply and deployment constraints make the top rung less likely.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e2295', 'e109', 'e5079', 'e3399', 'e2652', 'e3200', 'e5056', 'e2693', 'e5223', 'e5061', 'e5138', 'e5067', 'e5205'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e2295', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e109', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5079', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3399', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2652', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3200', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5056', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2693', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5223', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5061', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5138', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5067', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5205', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v16', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v22', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v9', 'push': 'toward_first'}]}\nF1 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q1'} 81.62 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e111 quote_number=$81.62 billion\nF2 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 96.22 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e5078 quote_number=$96.22 billion\nF3 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 H1'} 177.84 None None basis=reported inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=sum\nF4 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 108 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF5 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 113.6 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5056 quote_number=$113.6 billion qualifier=roughly\nF6 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'sequential revenue growth', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 16.4 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I expect a smaller beat than the outside estimate based on past guidance beats. The prior quarter's beat, Vera Rubin production shipments and management's outlook for AI cloud, industrial and enterprise demand support growth above the guidance midpoint. Supply constraints limit the beat. rests_on=['F2', 'F4', 'F5', 'e3200', 'e2693', 'e5061', 'e5067']\nF7 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 112 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F2', 'F6'] operation=apply_growth\nF8 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'sequential revenue growth', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 17 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I expect sequential growth to pick up modestly as Vera Rubin moves through its first full quarter of production shipments and more systems reach hyperscalers. Increased supply commitments support that pace. Memory constraints and long lead times at some distributors limit how much demand NVIDIA can turn into sales by year-end. rests_on=['F6', 'F7', 'e2693', 'e5138', 'e5061', 'e2759', 'e5067', 'e5205']\nF9 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 131.04 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F7', 'F8'] operation=apply_growth\nF10 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027'} 420.88 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F3', 'F7', 'F9'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v4']\nF11 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027'} 411 None None basis=consensus evidence_id=e5204 quote_number=$411 billion reference_for=['v4']\nF12 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q1'} 75.25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e109 quote_number=$75.25 billion\nF13 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 89.02 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e5079 quote_number=$89.02 billion\nF14 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 H1'} 164.27 None None basis=reported inputs=['F12', 'F13'] operation=sum\nF15 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'share of company revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 92.5 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e3399 quote_number=92.5%\nF16 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'share of company revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 93 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I expect the segment's share to rise modestly from Q2 because NVIDIA expects Data Center to drive most of the company's growth, led within the segment by AI cloud, industrial and enterprise customers. rests_on=['F15', 'e5223', 'e5061', 'e2693']\nF17 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'share of company revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 93.5 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I expect another modest rise in the segment's share as increased Vera Rubin supply brings through more hyperscaler sales. Supply and customer deployment limits keep the increase gradual. rests_on=['F16', 'e5061', 'e5138', 'e5067', 'e5205']\nF18 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 104.16 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F7', 'F16'] operation=share\nF19 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 122.52 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F17'] operation=share\nF20 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027'} 390.95 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F14', 'F18', 'F19'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v8']\nF21 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027'} 361 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2295 quote_number=$361 billion reference_for=['v8']\nOther: {}\n\n R03 {'case': 'E', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v41', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 3, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. Its more-than-double language points above that threshold without specifying by how much. My forecast is $34 billion, in rung 3. I estimate $17 billion of FY2027 Vera revenue from the $20 billion CPU outlook and expect Vera revenue to double as deployments broaden. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation is a concrete sign of interest. Neither statement gives committed purchase volumes. AMD's own tests suggest that Vera faces competition for CPU-intensive workloads, with an uncertain effect on sales. The hyperscaler spending outlook, especially the updated calendar 2027 assessment, supports the ramp, but gives no Vera allocation.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e3660', 'e4856', 'e4689', 'e4691', 'e5044', 'e5400', 'e5403'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e3660', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4687', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4689', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4691', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4692', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4764', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4856', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4857', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5044', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5399', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5400', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5401', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5402', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5403', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5404', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}]}\nF1 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'CPU', 'period': 'FY2027'} 20 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e3660 quote_number=$20 billion\nF2 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue multiple', 'scope': 'FY2028 Vera CPU revenue relative to the FY2027 CPU outlook', 'period': 'FY2028'} 2 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e4856 quote_number=double qualifier=more than\nF3 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Vera CPU', 'period': 'FY2028'} 40 None None basis=guidance inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=product reference_for=['v41']\nF4 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue realization share', 'scope': 'Vera CPU revenue as a share of the stated CPU opportunity', 'period': 'FY2027'} 85 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate that Vera realizes most of the stated CPU opportunity in its initial ramp. Expected deployment across major customers supports a high share, while the evidence gives no sales volumes to justify assigning the entire CPU opportunity to realized Vera revenue. rests_on=['F1', 'e4689', 'e5044']\nF5 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Vera CPU', 'period': 'FY2027'} 17 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F1', 'F4'] operation=share\nF6 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue multiple', 'scope': 'Vera CPU FY2028 versus FY2027', 'period': 'FY2028'} 2 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate that Vera revenue doubles from the discounted launch-year base as deployments broaden. Management's expansion outlook sets the benchmark for the pace. Anthropic is evaluating Vera, and AMD's platform and agentic-workload benchmarks suggest competition for additional deployments. rests_on=['F2', 'F5', 'e4689', 'e4691', 'e5044', 'e5400', 'e5403']\nF7 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Vera CPU', 'period': 'FY2028'} 34 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F5', 'F6'] operation=product outcome_for=['v41']\nOther: {}\n\n R04 {'case': 'O', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v29', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 1, 'reference_unavailable': \"The evidence supplies no consensus or company guidance for the outstanding FY2027 year-end balance. Bank of America's broader $70 billion estimate falls in rung 3 on size alone, but it gives no year-end date or split between funded and unfunded amounts.\", 'reasons': \"I forecast $16 billion of outstanding equity commitments at FY2027 year-end, below the $25 billion cutoff for rung 1. NVIDIA's July balance sat at the bottom of rung 2. Its stated funding schedule and first-half investment activity support an estimate that $17 billion of the July balance will be funded before year-end, leaving $8 billion. I estimate that another $8 billion of commitments added after July will still be outstanding. Announced infrastructure and equity deals and the GDT report support continued dealmaking. The Anthropic and Thinking Machines discussions add upside, with uncertain signing and funding dates. Poolside's license and MediaTek's convertible bonds are separate from the equity commitments forecast. The earlier OpenAI financing announcement lacks evidence of a fresh addition to the July balance, and Huang explicitly ruled out treating the earlier larger plan as a commitment. The AI capex themes support continued demand for ecosystem financing, with no direct read on how much NVIDIA will still owe at year-end.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e2620', 'e2889', 'e2080', 'e4729', 'e1957', 'e1919', 'e2404', 'e2207', 'e2305', 'e3455', 'e3880', 'e4648', 'e3934', 'e4956', 'e3474', 'e4486', 'e4487'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e2620', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2889', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2080', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4729', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1957', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1919', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2404', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2207', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2305', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3455', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3880', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4648', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3934', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4956', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3474', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4486', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4487', 'push': 'toward_first'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v5', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_last'}]}\nF1 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'equity investment commitments', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'end of FY2027 Q2'} 25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2620 quote_number=$25 billion\nF2 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'equity investment commitments scheduled for funding', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 H2'} 18 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2889 quote_number=$18 billion\nF3 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'direct equity investment commitments', 'scope': 'ecosystem partners', 'period': 'to date as of August 2026'} 70 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2080 quote_number=$70 billion qualifier=about\nF4 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'potential equity investment', 'scope': 'Lancium', 'period': 'August 2026 deal under discussion'} 3 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1919 quote_number=$3 billion qualifier=up to\nF5 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'announced equity investment', 'scope': 'SB Energy', 'period': 'August 2026 announcement'} 1.5 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e1957 quote_number=$1.5 billion\nF6 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'potential anchor equity investment', 'scope': 'Anthropic IPO', 'period': 'September 2026 discussions'} 10 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e4648 quote_number=$10 billion qualifier=as much as\nF7 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'potential equity investment', 'scope': 'Thinking Machines Lab', 'period': 'September 2026 discussions'} 2.5 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e3934 quote_number=$2.5 billion qualifier=approximately\nF8 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'funding of equity investment commitments outstanding at FY2027 Q2 end', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 H2'} 17 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I treat the company's remaining-year figure as the scheduled funding portion of the July balance. I expect nearly all of that portion to fund before fiscal year-end, with a small allowance for timing delays. rests_on=['F1', 'F2', 'e4729']\nF9 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'remaining equity investment commitments', 'scope': 'commitments outstanding at FY2027 Q2 end', 'period': 'end of FY2027'} 8 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F1', 'F8'] operation=difference\nF10 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'remaining equity investment commitments', 'scope': 'commitments added after FY2027 Q2 end', 'period': 'end of FY2027'} 8 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate that $8 billion of new commitments will remain unfunded at year-end. I allow for the announced SB Energy, Lancium and Poolside equity deals after discounting near-term closings and contingent amounts. I include a modest contribution from the named lab discussions and other ecosystem deals. The reported Anthropic and Thinking Machines amounts establish deal scale, while the uncertain signing and funding dates limit their contribution to the year-end balance. rests_on=['F4', 'F5', 'F6', 'F7', 'e2207', 'e2404', 'e2305', 'e3455', 'e3880', 'e4956']\nF11 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'equity investment commitments', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'end of FY2027'} 16 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F10'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v29']\nOther: {}\n\n R05 {'case': 'P-v4', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v4', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 2, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"I forecast FY2027 revenue of $416.34 billion, in rung 2. The $411 billion analyst consensus also sits in rung 2. The first two quarters are reported. For Q3, the previous guidance beat and CoreWeave's live Rubin deployment support $112.5 billion against NVIDIA's $108 billion midpoint. For Q4, the expected pickup in hyperscaler sales as Rubin supply expands supports $126 billion. Memory constraints and long channel lead times limit the volume that can ship before year-end and keep the forecast below rung 1. The calendar 2026 hyperscaler spending theme supports near-term demand, and the updated calendar 2027 theme adds support for orders entering Q4. Limited Chinese H200 authorization gives Q4 modest upside because shipments would have little time to land. The other export-control themes leave the near-term baseline broadly in place.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e5204', 'e111', 'e5078', 'e2652', 'e3200', 'e5056', 'e2693', 'e5118', 'e3406', 'e2686', 'e5067', 'e5205', 'e2776'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e5204', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e111', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5078', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2652', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3200', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5056', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2693', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5118', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3406', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2686', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5067', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5205', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2776', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v9', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v16', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v22', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'none'}]}\nF1 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027'} 411 None None basis=consensus evidence_id=e5204 quote_number=$411 billion reference_for=['v4']\nF2 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q1'} 81.62 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e111 quote_number=$81.62 billion\nF3 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 96.22 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e5078 quote_number=$96.22 billion\nF4 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 108 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF5 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 113.6 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5056 quote_number=$113.6 billion qualifier=roughly\nF6 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 112.5 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I forecast a beat against NVIDIA's guidance. The prior quarter beat its outlook even with China data-center compute excluded from guidance. Rubin production shipments and CoreWeave's live deployment support additional sales. Channel lead times temper the beat implied by repeating the historical pattern in full. rests_on=['F3', 'F4', 'F5', 'e3200', 'e2693', 'e5118', 'e5205']\nF7 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'sequential revenue growth', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 12 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I forecast 12% sequential growth on the higher Q3 base, close to the pace implied by the Q3 guide on reported Q2 sales. Management expects hyperscaler growth to reaccelerate as Rubin supply builds, and the AWS commitment supports demand over several fiscal years. Memory and system availability limit how much can ship this quarter. The pace includes a small allowance for late-quarter H200 sales if Chinese authorization translates into shipments within the fiscal year. rests_on=['F3', 'F4', 'F6', 'e3406', 'e2686', 'e5067', 'e5205', 'e2776']\nF8 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 126 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F6', 'F7'] operation=apply_growth\nF9 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027'} 416.34 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F2', 'F3', 'F6', 'F8'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v4']\nOther: {}\n\n R06 {'case': 'O', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v29', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 1, 'reference_unavailable': \"No consensus, company guidance or outside forecast of the year-end equity investment commitments balance is in the evidence. Bank of America's $70 billion counts money already invested as well as money still owed, a different measure. The last reported balance, $25 billion at July 26, 2026, sits at the lower edge of rung 2.\", 'reasons': \"Reference: there is no market forecast of the year-end balance. The status quo is NVIDIA's reported $25 billion of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026 (F1), exactly at rung 2's lower edge. Bank of America's '$70 billion committed in equity investments' (F6) does not place the year-end balance in rung 3: it counts funded and unfunded money together. NVIDIA bought more than $42 billion of stakes in the first half (F7) and still owed $25 billion, which together come close to BofA's figure. Derivation: NVIDIA said $18 billion (F2) of its commitments is to be funded in the rest of FY2027. That is the company's own schedule for the current half and the strongest input; it leaves about $7 billion (F3) of the July book still owed at year end. To that I add commitments signed after July that are still unfunded at year end (F4, my estimate $2-13 billion). Most new deals fund at closing (Poolside $1 billion, MediaTek $3.5 billion convertible bonds, SB Energy's first $1.5 billion). What stays on the books is staged or unclosed money: SB Energy's second $1.5 billion if its IPO slips, Lancium's milestone-linked $1 billion, and at most a $10 billion Anthropic IPO anchor if signed before year end but funded after. Huang said this year's large AI-lab investments will not repeat and that the $100 billion OpenAI plan was never a commitment, which argues against another large staged commitment. Outcome F5 = $9-20 billion, entirely below the $25 billion boundary: rung 1. What would move it to rung 2: a large part of the $18 billion slipping into next year, or a new multi-year staged commitment of more than $5 billion beyond the ones named.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e2620', 'e2889', 'e1732', 'e4729', 'e1957', 'e1970', 'e1919', 'e2207', 'e3474', 'e4648', 'e3934', 'e2280', 'e2643', 'e4487'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e2620', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3917', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4053', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2889', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1957', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1989', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2404', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1970', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1919', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2207', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3474', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3558', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2280', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2305', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3455', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3934', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4648', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4669', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4668', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2643', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4487', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4486', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1732', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2080', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2687', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4729', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4829', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3880', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4955', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4956', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': []}\nF1 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'equity investment commitments', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'end of FY2027 Q2'} 25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2620 quote_number=$25 billion\nF2 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'equity investment commitments to be funded', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 H2'} 18 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2889 quote_number=$18 billion\nF3 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'equity investment commitments outstanding at end of FY2027 Q2 still unfunded', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'end of FY2027'} 7 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=difference\nF6 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'committed equity investments', 'scope': 'ecosystem partners', 'period': 'cumulative to 2026-08-12, period not specified'} 70 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1732 quote_number=$70 billion\nF7 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'equity stake purchases', 'scope': 'other companies', 'period': 'FY2027 H1'} 42 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e4729 quote_number=$42 billion qualifier=more than\nF9 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'second-tranche investment', 'scope': 'SB Energy', 'period': 'at SB Energy IPO'} 1.5 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1970 quote_number=$1.5 billion\nF10 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'milestone-linked additional investment', 'scope': 'Lancium', 'period': 'after the initial investment'} 1 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1919 quote_number=$1 billion\nF11 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'anchor investment in IPO', 'scope': 'Anthropic', 'period': 'at Anthropic IPO'} 10 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e4648 quote_number=$10 billion qualifier=as much as\nF12 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'investment under discussion', 'scope': 'Thinking Machines Lab', 'period': 'not stated'} 2.5 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e3934 quote_number=$2.5 billion qualifier=approximately\nF4 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'equity investment commitments signed after FY2027 Q2 still unfunded', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'end of FY2027'} None 2 13 basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate. Most deals announced since quarter-end fund at closing (Poolside's $1 billion, MediaTek's $3.5 billion of convertible bonds, SB Energy's first $1.5 billion), so they leave no open commitment at year end. What can still be owed at year end is staged or not-yet-closed money: SB Energy's second $1.5 billion if its IPO slips past January, Lancium's milestone-linked $1 billion, a possible Anthropic IPO anchor of up to $10 billion only if signed before year end with the IPO after it, and a little from rounds still in talks (Thinking Machines about $2.5 billion, Perplexity unsized), which normally fund at closing. Low: Lancium's milestone tranche plus about $1 billion of other staged money. High: SB Energy's second tranche, Lancium's tranche and a full Anthropic anchor all signed and unfunded (about $12.5 billion), rounded up. Huang's statements that this year's large AI-lab investments will not repeat and that the $100 billion OpenAI plan was never a commitment argue against a further large staged commitment. rests_on=['F9', 'F10', 'F11', 'F12', 'e2207', 'e3474', 'e1957', 'e2280', 'e2643', 'e4487']\nF5 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'equity investment commitments', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'end of FY2027'} None 9 20 basis=forecast inputs=['F3', 'F4'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v29']\nOther: {}\n\n R07 {'case': 'S', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v6', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 3, 'reference_unavailable': \"The packet gives four-company estimates for all capital expenditure and for broadly defined AI infrastructure. NVIDIA's data-center figure includes a fifth company. The sources provide no quoted allocation that isolates data-center capex for the four companies.\", 'reasons': \"I forecast $670.5 billion in CY2026 data-center capex, which falls in rung 3. The closest market benchmark by company coverage is Zacks's $720 billion to $745 billion estimate for all capex, numerically in rung 4. The company spending revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support $745 billion of total capex. Project delays and grid constraints temper the pace, and the additional AWS GPU deployment is scheduled after CY2026. Applying my 90% data-center share to the total puts the forecast below $700 billion. The spending mix is the biggest uncertainty near that cutoff.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e5014', 'e2458', 'e1254', 'e1649', 'e1549', 'e3686', 'e922', 'e4404', 'e1947', 'e5376', 'e122', 'e5292', 'e5150'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e5014', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2458', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1254', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1649', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1549', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3686', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e922', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4404', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1947', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5376', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e122', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5292', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5150', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': []}\nF1 source {'population': 'four largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'total capital expenditure', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} None 720 745 basis=third_party evidence_id=e5014 quote_number=$720 billion to $745 billion qualifier=roughly\nF2 source {'population': 'four largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'total capital expenditure', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} 760 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2458 quote_number=$760 billion\nF3 source {'population': 'Alphabet', 'measure': 'total capital expenditure', 'scope': 'company-wide', 'period': 'CY2026'} None 195 205 basis=guidance evidence_id=e1254 quote_number=$195 billion to $205 billion\nF4 source {'population': 'Amazon', 'measure': 'total capital expenditure', 'scope': 'company-wide', 'period': 'CY2026'} 220 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e1649 quote_number=$220 billion\nF5 source {'population': 'Meta Platforms', 'measure': 'total capital expenditure', 'scope': 'company-wide', 'period': 'FY2026'} None 125 145 basis=guidance evidence_id=e1549 quote_number=$125 billion to $145 billion\nF6 source {'population': 'Meta Platforms', 'measure': 'AI capital expenditure', 'scope': 'company-wide', 'period': 'CY2026'} 130 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e3686 quote_number=$130 billion\nF7 source {'population': 'Microsoft', 'measure': 'total capital expenditure', 'scope': 'company-wide', 'period': 'FY2026 Q3'} 30.88 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e922 quote_number=$30.88 billion\nF8 source {'population': 'Microsoft', 'measure': 'year-over-year capital expenditure growth', 'scope': 'company-wide', 'period': 'FY2026 Q3'} 84 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e922 quote_number=84%\nF9 source {'population': 'four largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'AI infrastructure spending', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} 730 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e1947 quote_number=$730 billion qualifier=more than\nF10 source {'population': 'five largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'data-center capex', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} 800 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5376 quote_number=$800 billion qualifier=nearly\nF11 estimate {'population': 'four largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'total capital expenditure', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} 745 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I expect combined spending to land near the high end of the Zacks range. The Alphabet, Amazon and Meta revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support a strong pace. Amazon's higher memory costs also lift dollar spending. Project delays and grid connection constraints lead me to assume that some planned spending slips, keeping the forecast below the higher published plan. rests_on=['F1', 'F2', 'F3', 'F4', 'F5', 'F7', 'F8', 'e122', 'e5292']\nF12 estimate {'population': 'four largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'data-center share of total capital expenditure', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} 90 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate that data centers account for 90% of combined capital expenditure. Zacks identifies AI infrastructure as a large use of the group's spending, and the Meta AI capex estimate is close to its total capex guidance. I use that comparison as evidence of a high share and allow for other capital spending across all four companies. The packet gives no group-wide data-center split. rests_on=['F1', 'F5', 'F6', 'e4404']\nF13 computed {'population': 'four largest AI hyperscalers', 'measure': 'data-center capex', 'scope': 'combined', 'period': 'CY2026'} 670.5 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F11', 'F12'] operation=share outcome_for=['v6']\nOther: {}\n\n R08 {'case': 'P-v8', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v8', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 2, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"I forecast about $388 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue, in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion segment estimate is the direct outside reference and also falls in rung 2. The reported first half contributes $164.27 billion. I forecast about $104 billion in Q3 and $120 billion in Q4. NVIDIA's Q3 company guidance anchors the pace. The prior guidance beat and initial Rubin shipments support a modest beat, and the live CoreWeave deployment and management's expectation of faster hyperscaler growth support a further Q4 step-up. Memory constraints and long delivery lead times limit how much of that demand can ship by year-end, keeping the forecast below $400 billion. The calendar 2026 spending theme supports demand this fiscal year. The higher calendar 2027 spending theme offers some late-year upside, with most of the spending window falling after NVIDIA's fiscal close. Possible H200 authorization adds limited Q4 upside because authorization may come late and shipment timing is uncertain. The other export-policy themes leave near-term shipment assumptions unchanged.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e2295', 'e109', 'e5079', 'e5078', 'e2652', 'e3200', 'e5056', 'e2693', 'e5061', 'e5118', 'e3406', 'e3399', 'e5059', 'e5223', 'e5067', 'e5205'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e2295', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e109', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5079', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5078', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2652', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3200', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5056', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2693', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5061', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5118', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3406', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3399', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5059', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5223', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5067', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5205', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v16', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v22', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v9', 'push': 'toward_first'}]}\nF1 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027'} 361 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2295 quote_number=$361 billion reference_for=['v8']\nF2 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q1'} 75.25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e109 quote_number=$75.25 billion\nF3 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 89.02 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e5079 quote_number=$89.02 billion\nF4 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 H1'} 164.27 None None basis=reported inputs=['F2', 'F3'] operation=sum\nF5 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 96.22 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e5078 quote_number=$96.22 billion\nF6 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 108 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF7 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 113.6 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5056 quote_number=$113.6 billion qualifier=roughly\nF8 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'sequential revenue growth', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 16 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate 16% sequential growth from Q2. The Q2 guidance beat, the start of Rubin shipments and expected growth from AI cloud, industrial and enterprise customers support a Q3 beat. Supply constraints lead me to forecast a smaller beat than Pradhan's extrapolation. rests_on=['F5', 'F6', 'F7', 'e3200', 'e2693', 'e5061', 'e5067']\nF9 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 111.62 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F5', 'F8'] operation=apply_growth\nF10 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'sequential revenue growth', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 15 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate 15% sequential growth from my Q3 forecast. Management expects hyperscaler growth to pick up as Rubin supply builds, and CoreWeave already has a multi-rack Rubin deployment online. I expect a larger dollar gain on the higher Q3 base, with memory and long delivery lead times limiting the pace. rests_on=['F8', 'F9', 'e3406', 'e5118', 'e5067', 'e5205']\nF11 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 128.36 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F10'] operation=apply_growth\nF12 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'share of company revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 93 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate a modest rise from the reported Q2 mix because NVIDIA identifies Data Center as the main driver of Q3 growth. The estimate allows Edge Computing revenue to grow as well. rests_on=['F3', 'F5', 'e3399', 'e5059', 'e5223']\nF13 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'share of company revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 93.5 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate another modest mix gain as hyperscaler sales pick up with Rubin availability. This leaves room for sequential growth in the rest of the company. rests_on=['F12', 'e3399', 'e3406', 'e5059', 'e5118']\nF14 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 103.8 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F12'] operation=share\nF15 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 120.01 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F11', 'F13'] operation=share\nF16 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 H2'} 223.81 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F14', 'F15'] operation=sum\nF17 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027'} 388.08 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F4', 'F16'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v8']\nOther: {}\n\n R09 {'case': 'P-v4', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v4', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 2, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"Reference: the Wall Street consensus for FY2027 revenue is $411 billion (e4333), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion); the Zacks consensus of 87.4% growth on FY2026's $215.9 billion (about $405 billion, e4990) is also rung 2. Outcome: the first half is reported at $177.8 billion (Q1 $81.62 billion, Q2 $96.2 billion). Q3 is guided to $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%, above the $104.6 billion consensus for the quarter; NVIDIA beat its guidance midpoint by 5.7% in Q2 and 4.6% in Q4 FY2026 and has topped the high end of guidance in each of the past 12 quarters, so I forecast Q3 at about $112.9 billion (4.5% above the midpoint, trimmed because capacity is sold out and supply covers roughly 70% of demand). For Q4 I forecast about 14% sequential growth to about $128.7 billion: management says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds, Vera Rubin is in production shipments with purchase orders from every major hyperscaler and is guided to about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue at a higher revenue per gigawatt ($40 billion versus $25 billion for Blackwell), and supply commitments rose from $119 billion to $279 billion; the pace stays below the roughly 18% realized in Q2 because supply remains constrained through FY2028. FY2027 = 177.8 + 112.9 + 128.7, about $419 billion: rung 2, the same rung as the reference, a little above consensus. Rung 1 would need Q4 above about $140.5 billion, a 24.5% sequential jump against the supply limits management states. Rung 3 would need Q3 plus Q4 below about $214 billion, for example Q3 at the guidance low end and Q4 flat near $108 billion, which contradicts the beat record and the reacceleration management describes. The '70% full-year growth' reports (e3280, e3890, e4628) are not used for FY2027: the CFO's own words (e2691) put approximately 70% growth on next year, which e5227 identifies as FY2028; applied to FY2027 it would mean about $367 billion and a Q4 near $81 billion, below Q2, inconsistent with the $108 billion Q3 guidance. Competing accelerator commitments (AMD with OpenAI and Meta, Broadcom's $115 billion AI revenue for 2027) deploy mainly in calendar 2027 and NVIDIA is supply-bound now, so they do not lower FY2027. Themes in force: limited Chinese authorization of H200 purchases (judged likely) is upside the zero-China-compute guidance excludes, small and at most in Q4, so my Q4 pace does not assume zero China; hyperscaler 2026 data-center capital expenditure near $760 billion supports demand above supply; 2027 hyperscaler spending falls mainly in FY2028. None of these moves the outcome across $431.2 billion or $392 billion.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e111', 'e2649', 'e4992', 'e2652', 'e4963', 'e3200', 'e2452', 'e5054', 'e5055', 'e5056', 'e4911', 'e3733', 'e5067', 'e3406', 'e2693', 'e2721', 'e4202', 'e2759', 'e4333', 'e4990', 'e2691', 'e5227', 'e3280', 'e3890', 'e4628', 'e1040', 'e1041', 'e4982', 'e2681'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e111', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2649', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4992', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2652', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4963', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3200', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2452', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5054', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5055', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5056', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4911', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3733', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5067', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3406', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2693', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2721', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4202', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2759', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4333', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4990', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2691', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5227', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3280', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3890', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4628', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1040', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1041', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4982', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2681', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v9', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'none'}]}\nF1 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q1'} 81.62 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e111 quote_number=$81.62 billion\nF2 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 96.2 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2649 quote_number=$96.2 billion\nF3 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 H1'} 177.82 None None basis=reported inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=sum\nF4 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue sequential growth', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 17.86 None None basis=reported inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=growth\nF5 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 108.0 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF6 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 91.0 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e3200 quote_number=$91.0 billion\nF7 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue beat versus guidance midpoint', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 5.71 None None basis=reported inputs=['F6', 'F2'] operation=growth\nF8 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2026 Q4'} 65 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2452 quote_number=$65 billion\nF9 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2026 Q4'} 68 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2452 quote_number=$68 billion\nF10 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue beat versus guidance midpoint', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2026 Q4'} 4.62 None None basis=reported inputs=['F8', 'F9'] operation=growth\nF11 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue beat versus guidance midpoint', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 4.5 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate: Q3 FY2027 revenue lands about 4.5% above the guidance midpoint. NVIDIA beat its midpoint by 5.7% in Q2 FY2027 and by 4.6% in Q4 FY2026, and has exceeded the high end of guidance in each of the past 12 quarters, by 3.1% above the high end on average over the last four (about 5% above the midpoint). I trim slightly below that record because capacity is sold out and supply covers only about 70% of demand, which limits further upside surprises. rests_on=['F7', 'F10', 'e5054', 'e5055', 'e4911', 'e3733']\nF12 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 112.86 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F5', 'F11'] operation=apply_growth\nF13 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue sequential growth', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 14 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate: Q4 FY2027 revenue grows about 14% sequentially from the Q3 forecast. The benchmark is the roughly 18% sequential growth realized in Q2 FY2027; I set the pace below it because management expects supply to stay constrained through FY2028 and supply covers only about 70% of demand. The pace stays well above flat because management says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds, Vera Rubin is in production shipments with purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and OEM, and supply commitments more than doubled to $279 billion. The Q3 guidance itself implies about 12% sequential growth at its midpoint, a pace NVIDIA has typically exceeded. The pace does not assume zero China Data Center revenue. rests_on=['F4', 'e3406', 'e2693', 'e2759', 'e5067', 'e3733']\nF14 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 128.66 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F12', 'F13'] operation=apply_growth\nF15 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027'} 419.34 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F3', 'F12', 'F14'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v4']\nF16 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027'} 411 None None basis=consensus evidence_id=e4333 quote_number=$411 billion reference_for=['v4']\nOther: {}\n\n R10 {'case': 'P-v8', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v8', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 2, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"Reference: the only outside figure for this exact quantity is Morningstar's FY2027 Data Center estimate of $361 billion (F19, e2295), which sits in rung 2 ($350-400 billion); it was published before the Q2 report. For the whole company, analysts expect $411 billion of FY2027 revenue (F23); my company forecast is $418.2 billion (F22), slightly above, in line with NVIDIA's habit of beating guidance. Calculation: reported Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89.0 billion in Q2, so $164.25 billion in H1 (F3). Q3 company revenue is guided at $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%; NVIDIA beat the midpoint by 5.7% in Q2 and 4.6% in Q4 FY2026 and has beaten the high end for 12 straight quarters, so I forecast a 5% beat, $113.4 billion (F11, F12, my estimate). For Q4 I keep the guided 12% sequential pace (F13, my estimate): hyperscale growth is set to reaccelerate as Vera Rubin supply builds and supply commitments rose to $279 billion, but supply covers only about 70% of demand and stays tight through FY2028; that gives $127.0 billion (F14). Data Center was 92.5% of Q2 revenue and its share has risen every quarter; I assume 92.8% for H2 (F16), so H2 Data Center revenue is $223.1 billion (F17) and FY2027 Data Center revenue is $387.4 billion (F18), rung 2, the same rung as the reference but well above it, because Q2 and the Q3 guidance came in after the reference. Boundaries: falling below $350 billion would need H2 Data Center revenue under $185.8 billion, below the Q2 pace, although the Q3 guidance alone implies about $100 billion a quarter. Reaching $400 billion would need $235.8 billion, about 20% sequential Data Center growth in both quarters while supply is capped. So rung 1 is the upside risk (SemiAnalysis expects H2 Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates, e1135, but gives no base figure), not the most likely outcome. The reports of '70% growth for the full fiscal year' (e3280) refer to FY2028 per the earnings call (e2691); applied to FY2027 they would contradict H1 plus the Q3 guidance, so they move nothing. The H1 run-rate of just under $330 billion (e5310) annualises a past half and is not used. Competing accelerator deals (e1040, e4314) mostly deploy after FY2027 while NVIDIA sells all it can supply, so they do not move this year. Themes: China authorizing H200 purchases (judged likely) is an upside the Q3 guidance excludes; no evidence sizes it, and it is not large enough to lift the outcome past $400 billion on its own. Hyperscaler capex for 2026 sits about at the market reference, and the raised 2027 view supports the Q4 reacceleration, but supply, not demand, limits FY2027.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e109', 'e111', 'e2649', 'e2650', 'e2452', 'e3200', 'e2652', 'e5005', 'e5054', 'e5055', 'e4911', 'e3115', 'e3406', 'e5061', 'e4283', 'e2759', 'e5067', 'e3733', 'e4202', 'e2721', 'e3399', 'e5223', 'e2295', 'e4333', 'e1135', 'e2691', 'e3280', 'e2681', 'e1040', 'e4314', 'e5310', 'e4907'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e2650', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2652', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5054', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5055', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3406', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5061', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2759', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4202', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2721', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3399', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1135', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3733', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5067', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4911', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3115', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3280', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2691', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2681', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1040', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4314', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5310', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4907', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v9', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'none'}]}\nF1 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q1'} 75.25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e109 quote_number=$75.25 billion\nF2 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 89.0 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2650 quote_number=$89.0 billion\nF3 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 H1'} 164.25 None None basis=reported inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=sum\nF4 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 96.2 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2649 quote_number=$96.2 billion\nF5 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 91.0 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e3200 quote_number=$91.0 billion\nF6 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue beat over guidance midpoint', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 5.71 None None basis=reported inputs=['F5', 'F4'] operation=growth\nF7 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2026 Q4'} 65 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2452 quote_number=$65 billion\nF8 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2026 Q4'} 68 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2452 quote_number=$68 billion\nF9 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue beat over guidance midpoint', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2026 Q4'} 4.62 None None basis=reported inputs=['F7', 'F8'] operation=growth\nF10 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 108.0 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF11 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue beat over guidance midpoint', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 5.0 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate. NVIDIA beat its revenue guidance midpoint by 5.7% in FY2027 Q2 (F6) and by 4.6% in FY2026 Q4 (F9), and has beaten the high end of guidance in each of the last 12 quarters, by 3.1% on average over the last four (about 5% above the midpoint; e5054, e5055). Capacity is sold out, which limits further upside (e4911), so I assume a 5% beat on the $108.0 billion Q3 midpoint, not more than the recent record. rests_on=['F6', 'F9', 'e5054', 'e5055', 'e4911']\nF12 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 113.4 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F10', 'F11'] operation=apply_growth\nF13 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue growth quarter over quarter', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 12.0 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate of the Q4 sequential pace, benchmarked on the 12% sequential growth the Q3 guidance implies at its midpoint (e5005). Upward forces: NVIDIA says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406, e5061, e4283), supply and capacity commitments jumped to $279 billion (e2759), and Vera Rubin carries more revenue per gigawatt (e4202). Limits: supply covers only about 70% of demand and stays constrained through FY2028 (e3733, e5067), and my Q3 base already includes a beat. So I keep the guided 12% pace rather than the 17-18% delivered in recent quarters. The pace leaves room for limited H200 sales to China late in the year if purchases are authorized; no passage sizes such sales, so no separate figure is made. rests_on=['e5005', 'F10', 'F4', 'e3406', 'e5061', 'e4283', 'e2759', 'e4202', 'e3733', 'e5067']\nF14 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 127.01 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F12', 'F13'] operation=apply_growth\nF15 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 H2'} 240.41 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F12', 'F14'] operation=sum\nF16 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'Data Center share of revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 H2'} 92.8 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate. Data Center was 92.5% of revenue in FY2027 Q2 ($89.0 billion of $96.2 billion; F2, F4) and its share has risen every quarter, from 87.9% to 92.5%, with the latest step only 0.3 points (e3399). Growth in Q3 is driven primarily by the AI data center business (e5223), so I assume the share keeps edging up, to an average of 92.8% for H2. rests_on=['F2', 'F4', 'e3399', 'e5223']\nF17 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 H2'} 223.1 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F15', 'F16'] operation=share\nF18 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027'} 387.35 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F3', 'F17'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v8']\nF19 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027'} 361 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2295 quote_number=$361 billion reference_for=['v8']\nF20 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q1'} 81.62 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e111 quote_number=$81.62 billion\nF21 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 H1'} 177.82 None None basis=reported inputs=['F20', 'F4'] operation=sum\nF22 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027'} 418.23 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F21', 'F15'] operation=sum\nF23 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027'} 411 None None basis=consensus evidence_id=e4333 quote_number=$411 billion\nOther: {}\n\n R11 {'case': 'P', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v4', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 2, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"Reference: Wall Street consensus of $411 billion for FY2027 (F19) sits in rung 2 ($392-431.2 billion); the 87-88% consensus growth rates (e4990, e5129) on FY2026's $215.9 billion give about $405 billion, also rung 2. Outcome from the evidence: H1 is reported at $177.8 billion (Q1 $81.62 billion, Q2 $96.2 billion; F3). Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%, excluding China data center compute; given 12 straight beats of the high end (3.1% on average over the last four) and a $5.2 billion beat of the Q2 midpoint, trimmed for the stated memory, power and packaging caps, I forecast Q3 at $112.5 billion (F9). For Q4 I forecast 12% sequential growth, the pace guided for Q3, because hyperscale growth is said to reaccelerate in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds and supply commitments rose to $279 billion, while supply stays constrained (F11): Q4 about $126 billion, H2 $238.5 billion, FY2027 about $416 billion (F14), rung 2, above consensus but inside the same band. Sensitivity: Q3 at the guidance midpoint and only 5% Q4 growth still gives about $399 billion (rung 2); rung 1 ($431.2 billion) would need H2 of about $253 billion, e.g. Q4 near $138 billion (+20% sequential), which a supply-constrained company is not shown to deliver. The reports of '70% full-year growth' (e3280, e3890, e4628) conflict with the call transcript (e2691), which ties about 70% growth to next year (FY2028) because of supply limits; 70% on $215.9 billion ($367 billion) would also require Q4 to fall to about $81 billion, below Q1, so I read them as the FY2028 outlook. The analysts' quarterly path (90% Q3 growth, 65% Q4 growth: e5161, e5162) implies only about $398 billion and predates the Q3 guide's stated pace. Themes: China authorizing H200 purchases (v9) is judged more likely than not; Q3 guidance assumes no China data center compute, and I do not assume China at zero for Q4, but any such sales before the late-January year end are small and supply-limited, so they sit inside the Q4 pace rather than being added on top. Hyperscaler capex judged at about $760 billion for 2026 (v6) and above the market reference for 2027 (v7, updated) supports the Q4 hyperscale reacceleration. The export-control themes judged 'No' (v16, v22, v26, v27) keep the status quo already in guidance.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e111', 'e2649', 'e4992', 'e4273', 'e2652', 'e3346', 'e4963', 'e5054', 'e5055', 'e3200', 'e2452', 'e4911', 'e3115', 'e2870', 'e4899', 'e3406', 'e4283', 'e2759', 'e2691', 'e5067', 'e2681', 'e4333', 'e4990', 'e5129', 'e3280', 'e3890', 'e4628', 'e5161', 'e5162'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e2649', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3200', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2652', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4963', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5054', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5055', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3406', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4283', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2759', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2691', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2693', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2870', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3115', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5067', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4911', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2681', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4333', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4990', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5161', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5162', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3280', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4274', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4992', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v9', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v16', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v22', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'none'}]}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v8', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 2, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"Reference: Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 Data Center estimate (F20), the only quoted outside figure for the segment, sits in rung 2 ($350-400 billion). Applying a quarter's mix to the $411 billion consensus would be my own estimate, not the market's. Outcome from the family's figures: H1 Data Center revenue is reported at $164.25 billion (Q1 $75.25 billion, Q2 $89.0 billion; $164 billion per e5309). Data Center's share of revenue has risen every quarter, from 87.9% to 92.5% in Q2 (e3399). Management says Q3 growth comes primarily from the AI data center business (ACIE leading, Vera Rubin about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue), while Edge Computing, the remainder ($7.2 billion in Q2), grew a slower 13%. I therefore take Data Center at 93.0% of the $238.5 billion H2 revenue forecast (F16), giving $221.8 billion for H2 and about $386 billion for FY2027 (F18), rung 2. That stays below the total's $416 billion, so the part does not exceed the whole. It sits above Morningstar's pre-Q2 estimate, consistent with SemiAnalysis's view that H2 Data Center revenue will beat analyst estimates by 20% (e1135). The 'just under $330 billion' run rate (e5310) annualizes H1 flat and is not a forecast. Sensitivity: H2 at the guidance midpoint with 5% Q4 growth and a 92.5% share gives about $369 billion (rung 2). Rung 1 ($400 billion) needs about $236 billion of H2 Data Center revenue, i.e. H2 company revenue near $253 billion, the same acceleration the supply constraints (e2870, e3115, e5067) argue against. Rung 3 would need H2 Data Center below about $186 billion, under even the low end of Q3 guidance carried flat. e4907's $83.7 billion Q2 figure conflicts with the company's $89.0 billion release and is disregarded. Themes: possible China H200 authorization (v9, judged more likely than not) is Data Center upside that guidance excludes; it is not assumed to be zero but is small and supply-limited within FY2027. The hyperscaler capex themes (v6, v7) support Q4 hyperscale demand. The export-control themes judged 'No' keep the status quo.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e109', 'e2650', 'e5309', 'e3399', 'e5059', 'e5223', 'e2721', 'e4283', 'e3406', 'e1135', 'e2295', 'e5310', 'e4907', 'e2652', 'e5054', 'e5055', 'e2759', 'e2870', 'e3115', 'e5067', 'e2681'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e2650', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3399', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2721', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e1135', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4283', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5058', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2652', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5054', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4202', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2759', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2870', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3115', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5067', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e109', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e2295', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5310', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e3401', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4907', 'push': 'none'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v9', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v16', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v22', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v26', 'push': 'none'}, {'view_id': 'v27', 'push': 'none'}]}\nF1 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q1'} 81.62 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e111 quote_number=$81.62 billion\nF2 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 96.2 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2649 quote_number=$96.2 billion\nF3 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 H1'} 177.82 None None basis=reported inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=sum\nF4 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q1'} 75.25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e109 quote_number=$75.25 billion\nF5 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 89.0 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2650 quote_number=$89.0 billion\nF6 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 H1'} 164.25 None None basis=reported inputs=['F4', 'F5'] operation=sum\nF7 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} None 105.84 110.16 basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF8 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue above the high end of guidance, four-quarter average', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2026 Q3 to FY2027 Q2'} 3.1 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5055 quote_number=3.1%\nF9 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 112.5 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate: Q3 revenue lands about 4% above the $108.0 billion guidance midpoint and about 2% above its $110.16 billion high end. NVIDIA has beaten the high end in each of the past 12 quarters, by 3.1% on average over the last four (which would give about $113.6 billion), and beat the Q2 midpoint by $5.2 billion and the Q4 FY2026 guide by $3 billion; I take part of that pattern rather than all of it because management and outside analysts say memory, power and packaging bottlenecks cap near-term output and capacity is sold out. rests_on=['F7', 'F8', 'e5054', 'e3200', 'e2452', 'e4911', 'e3115']\nF10 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue growth quarter over quarter', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q3'} 12 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e4899 quote_number=12%\nF11 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue growth quarter over quarter', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 12 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate of Q4 sequential growth: 12%, the pace guided for Q3 (benchmark F10). It applies because management says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds, supply and capacity commitments rose from $119 billion to $279 billion, and the supply-capped outlook of about 70% growth in FY2028 needs low-to-mid-teens sequential growth from the Q4 exit; actual sequential growth has run 17-20% (Q1 to Q2, and Q2 to my Q3 estimate), but management expects supply to stay constrained through FY2028, so I do not carry that faster actual pace into Q4. rests_on=['F10', 'F2', 'F9', 'e3406', 'e4283', 'e2759', 'e2691', 'e5067']\nF12 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 Q4'} 126.0 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F11'] operation=apply_growth\nF13 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027 H2'} 238.5 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F12'] operation=sum\nF14 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027'} 416.32 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F3', 'F13'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v4']\nF15 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'share of revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 Q2'} 92.5 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e3399 quote_number=92.5%\nF16 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'share of revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 H2'} 93.0 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate: Data Center is 93.0% of H2 revenue, slightly above Q2's 92.5% (89.0 of 96.2). The share has risen every quarter (87.9%, 89.8%, 91.5%, 92.2%, 92.5%), with the gains narrowing; management says Q3 growth comes primarily from the AI data center business (ACIE, with Vera Rubin about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue), while Edge Computing, the remainder, grew a slower 13% sequentially in Q2. rests_on=['F15', 'F5', 'F2', 'e5059', 'e5223', 'e4283', 'e2721']\nF17 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027 H2'} 221.81 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F13', 'F16'] operation=share\nF18 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027'} 386.06 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F6', 'F17'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v8']\nF19 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'company', 'period': 'FY2027'} 411 None None basis=consensus evidence_id=e4333 quote_number=$411 billion reference_for=['v4']\nF20 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Data Center', 'period': 'FY2027'} 361 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2295 quote_number=$361 billion reference_for=['v8']\nOther: {}\n\n R12 {'case': 'E', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v41', 'shape': 'forecast', 'rung': 3, 'reference_unavailable': None, 'reasons': \"Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\", 'why': None, 'cites': ['e3660', 'e4691', 'e4764', 'e4856', 'e4687', 'e4857', 'e4689', 'e4692', 'e5044', 'e5399', 'e5400', 'e5401', 'e5402', 'e5404'], 'pushes': [{'evidence_id': 'e3660', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4691', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4764', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4856', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4687', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4857', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4689', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e4692', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5044', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5399', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5400', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5401', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5402', 'push': 'toward_last'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5403', 'push': 'none'}, {'evidence_id': 'e5404', 'push': 'toward_last'}], 'theme_pushes': [{'view_id': 'v7', 'push': 'toward_first'}, {'view_id': 'v6', 'push': 'none'}]}\nF1 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Vera CPU', 'period': 'FY2027'} 20 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e3660 quote_number=$20 billion\nF2 source {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue growth multiple', 'scope': 'Vera CPU', 'period': 'FY2028'} 2 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e4856 quote_number=double qualifier=more than\nF3 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Vera CPU', 'period': 'FY2028'} 40 None None basis=guidance inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=product reference_for=['v41']\nF4 source {'population': 'server CPU market', 'measure': 'revenue growth', 'scope': 'server CPU', 'period': 'annual rate through CY2030'} 50 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e4687 quote_number=50%\nF5 source {'population': 'CPU market', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'CPU', 'period': 'CY2026'} 35 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e4857 quote_number=$35 billion\nF6 source {'population': 'CPU market', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'CPU', 'period': 'CY2030'} 170 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e4857 quote_number=$170 billion\nF7 estimate {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue growth', 'scope': 'Vera CPU', 'period': 'FY2028'} 85 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth on the FY2027 base. Management's 'more than double' (F2, at least 100% growth) is the benchmark, but it is a target for a fiscal year that has not started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full. Outside views put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD, F4; Bank of America's fivefold path from F5 to F6), so a doubling needs large share gains. Broad announced adoption (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689; Anthropic evaluating Vera, e5044; Arm servers' large revenue share, e4692) supports a pace well above the market, while AMD's own benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) show standalone CPU share will be contested. Weighting the company target most heavily, tempered toward the market pace, gives 85%. rests_on=['F2', 'F4', 'F5', 'F6', 'e4689', 'e5044', 'e4692', 'e5399', 'e5400', 'e5401', 'e5402', 'e5404']\nF8 computed {'population': 'NVIDIA', 'measure': 'revenue', 'scope': 'Vera CPU', 'period': 'FY2028'} 37 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F1', 'F7'] operation=apply_growth outcome_for=['v41']\nOther: {}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\np=Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\nfor rid,k in json.loads((p/'\\\\''reader-key.json'\\\\'').read_text()).items():\n a=p/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/(k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']+'\\\\''.json'\\\\'')\n d=json.loads(a.read_text().strip().removeprefix('\\\\''\"'```json'\"'\\\\'').removesuffix('\\\\''\"'```'\"'\\\\''))\n print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',rid,k)\n for a in d['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']: print('\\\\''Answer:'\\\\'', str({k:a[k] for k in ['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'','\\\\''rung'\\\\'','\\\\''reasons'\\\\'','\\\\''reference_unavailable'\\\\'']}))\n for f in d['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n  print(f['\\\\''id'\\\\''],f['\\\\''kind'\\\\''],'\\\\''|'\\\\''.join(f['\\\\''what'\\\\''].values()),f.get('\\\\''value'\\\\''),f.get('\\\\''low'\\\\''),f.get('\\\\''high'\\\\''), '\\\\'' '\\\\''.join(f'\\\\''{key}={v}'\\\\'' for key,v in f.items() if key not in ('\\\\''id'\\\\'','\\\\''kind'\\\\'','\\\\''what'\\\\'','\\\\''unit'\\\\'','\\\\''value'\\\\'','\\\\''low'\\\\'','\\\\''high'\\\\'') and v not in ('\\\\'''\\\\'',None,[],'\\\\''none'\\\\'')))\n print('\\\\''Other:'\\\\'', {k:v for k,v in d.items() if k not in ('\\\\''figures'\\\\'','\\\\''answers'\\\\'')})\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\np=Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\nfor rid,k in json.loads((p/'\\\\''reader-key.json'\\\\'').read_text()).items():\n a=p/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/(k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']+'\\\\''.json'\\\\'')\n d=json.loads(a.read_text().strip().removeprefix('\\\\''\"'```json'\"'\\\\'').removesuffix('\\\\''\"'```'\"'\\\\''))\n print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',rid,k)\n for a in d['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']: print('\\\\''Answer:'\\\\'', str({k:a[k] for k in ['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'','\\\\''rung'\\\\'','\\\\''reasons'\\\\'','\\\\''reference_unavailable'\\\\'']}))\n for f in d['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n  print(f['\\\\''id'\\\\''],f['\\\\''kind'\\\\''],'\\\\''|'\\\\''.join(f['\\\\''what'\\\\''].values()),f.get('\\\\''value'\\\\''),f.get('\\\\''low'\\\\''),f.get('\\\\''high'\\\\''), '\\\\'' '\\\\''.join(f'\\\\''{key}={v}'\\\\'' for key,v in f.items() if key not in ('\\\\''id'\\\\'','\\\\''kind'\\\\'','\\\\''what'\\\\'','\\\\''unit'\\\\'','\\\\''value'\\\\'','\\\\''low'\\\\'','\\\\''high'\\\\'') and v not in ('\\\\'''\\\\'',None,[],'\\\\''none'\\\\'')))\n print('\\\\''Other:'\\\\'', {k:v for k,v in d.items() if k not in ('\\\\''figures'\\\\'','\\\\''answers'\\\\'')})\n'\"","aggregated_output":"\n R01 {'case': 'S', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v6', 'rung': 4, 'reasons': \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome in the upper part of the build but not near $800bn, since the $800bn figures cover five companies. Downward pressure: about $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a multi-year project value across all developers, not 2026 spending by these four, and the labour, grid and financing warnings (e1545, e5292, e2081, e4807) carry no number that would cut 2026 spending below $700bn. Repeats of the same claims (e4790, e1947, e2079 and the NVIDIA restatements) are counted once.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nF1 source Alphabet|capital expenditure|company total|CY2026 None 195 205 basis=guidance evidence_id=e1254 quote_number=$195 billion to $205 billion\nF2 source Meta Platforms|capital expenditure|company total (fiscal year equals calendar year)|CY2026 None 125 145 basis=guidance evidence_id=e1549 quote_number=$125 billion to $145 billion\nF3 source Amazon|capital expenditure|company total|CY2026 220 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e1649 quote_number=$220 billion\nF4 source Microsoft|capital expenditure|company total (fiscal Q3 FY2026, January-March 2026)|CY2026 Q1 30.88 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e922 quote_number=$30.88 billion\nF5 source Microsoft|capital expenditure growth, year over year|company total (fiscal Q3 FY2026, January-March 2026)|CY2026 Q1 84 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e922 quote_number=84%\nF6 estimate Microsoft|capital expenditure|company total|CY2026 175 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate. Microsoft states no calendar-2026 total in the evidence. Its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% year over year, and its $678bn Azure backlog supports a continuing ramp: carrying the growth pace through the year at roughly 15% sequential increases a quarter gives about $155bn on the reported quarter's basis (not the flat annualised $124bn). The outside tallies of the four companies' guided 2026 spending ($720-760bn) less the other three companies' guidance ($540-570bn) imply roughly $175-190bn at their centres, and those tallies include spending (such as leases) the single reported quarter may not. I take $175bn, between the two. rests_on=['F4', 'F5', 'F1', 'F2', 'F3', 'F8', 'F9', 'F10', 'e4405']\nF7 computed four largest AI hyperscalers|capital expenditure|combined|CY2026 None 715 745 basis=forecast inputs=['F1', 'F2', 'F3', 'F6'] operation=sum\nF8 source four largest AI hyperscalers|capital expenditure|combined|CY2026 None 720 745 basis=third_party evidence_id=e5014 quote_number=$720 billion to $745 billion qualifier=roughly reference_for=['v6']\nF9 source four largest hyperscalers|capital expenditure|combined, tracking|CY2026 745 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1856 quote_number=$745 billion qualifier=near\nF10 source top four hyperscalers|capital expenditure|combined, planned|CY2026 760 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2458 quote_number=$760 billion\nF11 source tech giants|data-center spending|combined|CY2026 750 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1416 quote_number=$750 billion\nF12 source five largest AI hyperscalers|data-center capex|combined|CY2026 800 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5376 quote_number=$800 billion qualifier=nearly\nF13 estimate four largest AI hyperscalers|data-center share of capital expenditure|combined|CY2026 None 95 100 basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate data-center line, so reported capital expenditure is how the outcome will be measured in practice. rests_on=['F11', 'F12', 'e4082', 'e5014', 'e1649']\nF14 computed four largest AI hyperscalers|data-center capex|combined|CY2026 None 679.25 745 basis=forecast inputs=['F7', 'F13'] operation=share outcome_for=['v6']\nOther: {}\n\n R02 {'case': 'P', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v4', 'rung': 2, 'reasons': \"I forecast $420.88 billion, in rung 2. The $411 billion analyst consensus also sits in rung 2. NVIDIA reported $177.84 billion in the first half. The last quarter's guidance beat and Vera Rubin shipments support a Q3 forecast of $112 billion against the $108 billion guidance midpoint. I expect $131.04 billion in Q4 as more Vera Rubin systems reach hyperscalers. Current spending supports that ramp. The higher calendar 2027 spending view mainly supports later deliveries. Memory shortages and long equipment lead times limit the upside this fiscal year. The export-policy views reduce the risk of additional restrictions, and limited H200 authorization offers a small chance of late sales. The open Commerce investigation gives no direction for this year's shipments.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v8', 'rung': 2, 'reasons': \"I forecast $390.95 billion, in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion estimate is the outside reference and also sits in rung 2. NVIDIA reported $164.27 billion in the first half. I allocate 93% of the company-wide Q3 forecast to Data Center, or $104.16 billion, as AI cloud, industrial and enterprise sales drive most of the segment's growth. I raise the share to 93.5% in Q4, or $122.52 billion, as Vera Rubin supply expands and hyperscaler sales pick up. Strong hyperscaler spending and the prospect of limited H200 authorization support the forecast, with most of the calendar 2027 spending impact falling later. The outlook for fewer new export restrictions eases downside risk, while the Commerce investigation remains open. Supply and deployment constraints make the top rung less likely.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nF1 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q1 81.62 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e111 quote_number=$81.62 billion\nF2 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q2 96.22 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e5078 quote_number=$96.22 billion\nF3 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 H1 177.84 None None basis=reported inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=sum\nF4 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 108 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF5 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 113.6 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5056 quote_number=$113.6 billion qualifier=roughly\nF6 estimate NVIDIA|sequential revenue growth|company|FY2027 Q3 16.4 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I expect a smaller beat than the outside estimate based on past guidance beats. The prior quarter's beat, Vera Rubin production shipments and management's outlook for AI cloud, industrial and enterprise demand support growth above the guidance midpoint. Supply constraints limit the beat. rests_on=['F2', 'F4', 'F5', 'e3200', 'e2693', 'e5061', 'e5067']\nF7 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 112 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F2', 'F6'] operation=apply_growth\nF8 estimate NVIDIA|sequential revenue growth|company|FY2027 Q4 17 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I expect sequential growth to pick up modestly as Vera Rubin moves through its first full quarter of production shipments and more systems reach hyperscalers. Increased supply commitments support that pace. Memory constraints and long lead times at some distributors limit how much demand NVIDIA can turn into sales by year-end. rests_on=['F6', 'F7', 'e2693', 'e5138', 'e5061', 'e2759', 'e5067', 'e5205']\nF9 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q4 131.04 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F7', 'F8'] operation=apply_growth\nF10 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 420.88 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F3', 'F7', 'F9'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v4']\nF11 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 411 None None basis=consensus evidence_id=e5204 quote_number=$411 billion reference_for=['v4']\nF12 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q1 75.25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e109 quote_number=$75.25 billion\nF13 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q2 89.02 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e5079 quote_number=$89.02 billion\nF14 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 H1 164.27 None None basis=reported inputs=['F12', 'F13'] operation=sum\nF15 source NVIDIA|share of company revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q2 92.5 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e3399 quote_number=92.5%\nF16 estimate NVIDIA|share of company revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q3 93 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I expect the segment's share to rise modestly from Q2 because NVIDIA expects Data Center to drive most of the company's growth, led within the segment by AI cloud, industrial and enterprise customers. rests_on=['F15', 'e5223', 'e5061', 'e2693']\nF17 estimate NVIDIA|share of company revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q4 93.5 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I expect another modest rise in the segment's share as increased Vera Rubin supply brings through more hyperscaler sales. Supply and customer deployment limits keep the increase gradual. rests_on=['F16', 'e5061', 'e5138', 'e5067', 'e5205']\nF18 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q3 104.16 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F7', 'F16'] operation=share\nF19 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q4 122.52 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F17'] operation=share\nF20 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 390.95 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F14', 'F18', 'F19'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v8']\nF21 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 361 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2295 quote_number=$361 billion reference_for=['v8']\nOther: {}\n\n R03 {'case': 'E', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v41', 'rung': 3, 'reasons': \"Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. Its more-than-double language points above that threshold without specifying by how much. My forecast is $34 billion, in rung 3. I estimate $17 billion of FY2027 Vera revenue from the $20 billion CPU outlook and expect Vera revenue to double as deployments broaden. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation is a concrete sign of interest. Neither statement gives committed purchase volumes. AMD's own tests suggest that Vera faces competition for CPU-intensive workloads, with an uncertain effect on sales. The hyperscaler spending outlook, especially the updated calendar 2027 assessment, supports the ramp, but gives no Vera allocation.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nF1 source NVIDIA|revenue|CPU|FY2027 20 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e3660 quote_number=$20 billion\nF2 source NVIDIA|revenue multiple|FY2028 Vera CPU revenue relative to the FY2027 CPU outlook|FY2028 2 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e4856 quote_number=double qualifier=more than\nF3 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Vera CPU|FY2028 40 None None basis=guidance inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=product reference_for=['v41']\nF4 estimate NVIDIA|revenue realization share|Vera CPU revenue as a share of the stated CPU opportunity|FY2027 85 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate that Vera realizes most of the stated CPU opportunity in its initial ramp. Expected deployment across major customers supports a high share, while the evidence gives no sales volumes to justify assigning the entire CPU opportunity to realized Vera revenue. rests_on=['F1', 'e4689', 'e5044']\nF5 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Vera CPU|FY2027 17 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F1', 'F4'] operation=share\nF6 estimate NVIDIA|revenue multiple|Vera CPU FY2028 versus FY2027|FY2028 2 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate that Vera revenue doubles from the discounted launch-year base as deployments broaden. Management's expansion outlook sets the benchmark for the pace. Anthropic is evaluating Vera, and AMD's platform and agentic-workload benchmarks suggest competition for additional deployments. rests_on=['F2', 'F5', 'e4689', 'e4691', 'e5044', 'e5400', 'e5403']\nF7 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Vera CPU|FY2028 34 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F5', 'F6'] operation=product outcome_for=['v41']\nOther: {}\n\n R04 {'case': 'O', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v29', 'rung': 1, 'reasons': \"I forecast $16 billion of outstanding equity commitments at FY2027 year-end, below the $25 billion cutoff for rung 1. NVIDIA's July balance sat at the bottom of rung 2. Its stated funding schedule and first-half investment activity support an estimate that $17 billion of the July balance will be funded before year-end, leaving $8 billion. I estimate that another $8 billion of commitments added after July will still be outstanding. Announced infrastructure and equity deals and the GDT report support continued dealmaking. The Anthropic and Thinking Machines discussions add upside, with uncertain signing and funding dates. Poolside's license and MediaTek's convertible bonds are separate from the equity commitments forecast. The earlier OpenAI financing announcement lacks evidence of a fresh addition to the July balance, and Huang explicitly ruled out treating the earlier larger plan as a commitment. The AI capex themes support continued demand for ecosystem financing, with no direct read on how much NVIDIA will still owe at year-end.\", 'reference_unavailable': \"The evidence supplies no consensus or company guidance for the outstanding FY2027 year-end balance. Bank of America's broader $70 billion estimate falls in rung 3 on size alone, but it gives no year-end date or split between funded and unfunded amounts.\"}\nF1 source NVIDIA|equity investment commitments|company|end of FY2027 Q2 25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2620 quote_number=$25 billion\nF2 source NVIDIA|equity investment commitments scheduled for funding|company|FY2027 H2 18 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2889 quote_number=$18 billion\nF3 source NVIDIA|direct equity investment commitments|ecosystem partners|to date as of August 2026 70 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2080 quote_number=$70 billion qualifier=about\nF4 source NVIDIA|potential equity investment|Lancium|August 2026 deal under discussion 3 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1919 quote_number=$3 billion qualifier=up to\nF5 source NVIDIA|announced equity investment|SB Energy|August 2026 announcement 1.5 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e1957 quote_number=$1.5 billion\nF6 source NVIDIA|potential anchor equity investment|Anthropic IPO|September 2026 discussions 10 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e4648 quote_number=$10 billion qualifier=as much as\nF7 source NVIDIA|potential equity investment|Thinking Machines Lab|September 2026 discussions 2.5 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e3934 quote_number=$2.5 billion qualifier=approximately\nF8 estimate NVIDIA|funding of equity investment commitments outstanding at FY2027 Q2 end|company|FY2027 H2 17 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I treat the company's remaining-year figure as the scheduled funding portion of the July balance. I expect nearly all of that portion to fund before fiscal year-end, with a small allowance for timing delays. rests_on=['F1', 'F2', 'e4729']\nF9 computed NVIDIA|remaining equity investment commitments|commitments outstanding at FY2027 Q2 end|end of FY2027 8 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F1', 'F8'] operation=difference\nF10 estimate NVIDIA|remaining equity investment commitments|commitments added after FY2027 Q2 end|end of FY2027 8 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate that $8 billion of new commitments will remain unfunded at year-end. I allow for the announced SB Energy, Lancium and Poolside equity deals after discounting near-term closings and contingent amounts. I include a modest contribution from the named lab discussions and other ecosystem deals. The reported Anthropic and Thinking Machines amounts establish deal scale, while the uncertain signing and funding dates limit their contribution to the year-end balance. rests_on=['F4', 'F5', 'F6', 'F7', 'e2207', 'e2404', 'e2305', 'e3455', 'e3880', 'e4956']\nF11 computed NVIDIA|equity investment commitments|company|end of FY2027 16 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F10'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v29']\nOther: {}\n\n R05 {'case': 'P-v4', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v4', 'rung': 2, 'reasons': \"I forecast FY2027 revenue of $416.34 billion, in rung 2. The $411 billion analyst consensus also sits in rung 2. The first two quarters are reported. For Q3, the previous guidance beat and CoreWeave's live Rubin deployment support $112.5 billion against NVIDIA's $108 billion midpoint. For Q4, the expected pickup in hyperscaler sales as Rubin supply expands supports $126 billion. Memory constraints and long channel lead times limit the volume that can ship before year-end and keep the forecast below rung 1. The calendar 2026 hyperscaler spending theme supports near-term demand, and the updated calendar 2027 theme adds support for orders entering Q4. Limited Chinese H200 authorization gives Q4 modest upside because shipments would have little time to land. The other export-control themes leave the near-term baseline broadly in place.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nF1 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 411 None None basis=consensus evidence_id=e5204 quote_number=$411 billion reference_for=['v4']\nF2 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q1 81.62 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e111 quote_number=$81.62 billion\nF3 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q2 96.22 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e5078 quote_number=$96.22 billion\nF4 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 108 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF5 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 113.6 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5056 quote_number=$113.6 billion qualifier=roughly\nF6 estimate NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 112.5 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I forecast a beat against NVIDIA's guidance. The prior quarter beat its outlook even with China data-center compute excluded from guidance. Rubin production shipments and CoreWeave's live deployment support additional sales. Channel lead times temper the beat implied by repeating the historical pattern in full. rests_on=['F3', 'F4', 'F5', 'e3200', 'e2693', 'e5118', 'e5205']\nF7 estimate NVIDIA|sequential revenue growth|company|FY2027 Q4 12 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I forecast 12% sequential growth on the higher Q3 base, close to the pace implied by the Q3 guide on reported Q2 sales. Management expects hyperscaler growth to reaccelerate as Rubin supply builds, and the AWS commitment supports demand over several fiscal years. Memory and system availability limit how much can ship this quarter. The pace includes a small allowance for late-quarter H200 sales if Chinese authorization translates into shipments within the fiscal year. rests_on=['F3', 'F4', 'F6', 'e3406', 'e2686', 'e5067', 'e5205', 'e2776']\nF8 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q4 126 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F6', 'F7'] operation=apply_growth\nF9 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 416.34 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F2', 'F3', 'F6', 'F8'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v4']\nOther: {}\n\n R06 {'case': 'O', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v29', 'rung': 1, 'reasons': \"Reference: there is no market forecast of the year-end balance. The status quo is NVIDIA's reported $25 billion of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026 (F1), exactly at rung 2's lower edge. Bank of America's '$70 billion committed in equity investments' (F6) does not place the year-end balance in rung 3: it counts funded and unfunded money together. NVIDIA bought more than $42 billion of stakes in the first half (F7) and still owed $25 billion, which together come close to BofA's figure. Derivation: NVIDIA said $18 billion (F2) of its commitments is to be funded in the rest of FY2027. That is the company's own schedule for the current half and the strongest input; it leaves about $7 billion (F3) of the July book still owed at year end. To that I add commitments signed after July that are still unfunded at year end (F4, my estimate $2-13 billion). Most new deals fund at closing (Poolside $1 billion, MediaTek $3.5 billion convertible bonds, SB Energy's first $1.5 billion). What stays on the books is staged or unclosed money: SB Energy's second $1.5 billion if its IPO slips, Lancium's milestone-linked $1 billion, and at most a $10 billion Anthropic IPO anchor if signed before year end but funded after. Huang said this year's large AI-lab investments will not repeat and that the $100 billion OpenAI plan was never a commitment, which argues against another large staged commitment. Outcome F5 = $9-20 billion, entirely below the $25 billion boundary: rung 1. What would move it to rung 2: a large part of the $18 billion slipping into next year, or a new multi-year staged commitment of more than $5 billion beyond the ones named.\", 'reference_unavailable': \"No consensus, company guidance or outside forecast of the year-end equity investment commitments balance is in the evidence. Bank of America's $70 billion counts money already invested as well as money still owed, a different measure. The last reported balance, $25 billion at July 26, 2026, sits at the lower edge of rung 2.\"}\nF1 source NVIDIA|equity investment commitments|company|end of FY2027 Q2 25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2620 quote_number=$25 billion\nF2 source NVIDIA|equity investment commitments to be funded|company|FY2027 H2 18 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2889 quote_number=$18 billion\nF3 computed NVIDIA|equity investment commitments outstanding at end of FY2027 Q2 still unfunded|company|end of FY2027 7 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=difference\nF6 source NVIDIA|committed equity investments|ecosystem partners|cumulative to 2026-08-12, period not specified 70 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1732 quote_number=$70 billion\nF7 source NVIDIA|equity stake purchases|other companies|FY2027 H1 42 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e4729 quote_number=$42 billion qualifier=more than\nF9 source NVIDIA|second-tranche investment|SB Energy|at SB Energy IPO 1.5 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1970 quote_number=$1.5 billion\nF10 source NVIDIA|milestone-linked additional investment|Lancium|after the initial investment 1 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e1919 quote_number=$1 billion\nF11 source NVIDIA|anchor investment in IPO|Anthropic|at Anthropic IPO 10 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e4648 quote_number=$10 billion qualifier=as much as\nF12 source NVIDIA|investment under discussion|Thinking Machines Lab|not stated 2.5 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e3934 quote_number=$2.5 billion qualifier=approximately\nF4 estimate NVIDIA|equity investment commitments signed after FY2027 Q2 still unfunded|company|end of FY2027 None 2 13 basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate. Most deals announced since quarter-end fund at closing (Poolside's $1 billion, MediaTek's $3.5 billion of convertible bonds, SB Energy's first $1.5 billion), so they leave no open commitment at year end. What can still be owed at year end is staged or not-yet-closed money: SB Energy's second $1.5 billion if its IPO slips past January, Lancium's milestone-linked $1 billion, a possible Anthropic IPO anchor of up to $10 billion only if signed before year end with the IPO after it, and a little from rounds still in talks (Thinking Machines about $2.5 billion, Perplexity unsized), which normally fund at closing. Low: Lancium's milestone tranche plus about $1 billion of other staged money. High: SB Energy's second tranche, Lancium's tranche and a full Anthropic anchor all signed and unfunded (about $12.5 billion), rounded up. Huang's statements that this year's large AI-lab investments will not repeat and that the $100 billion OpenAI plan was never a commitment argue against a further large staged commitment. rests_on=['F9', 'F10', 'F11', 'F12', 'e2207', 'e3474', 'e1957', 'e2280', 'e2643', 'e4487']\nF5 computed NVIDIA|equity investment commitments|company|end of FY2027 None 9 20 basis=forecast inputs=['F3', 'F4'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v29']\nOther: {}\n\n R07 {'case': 'S', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v6', 'rung': 3, 'reasons': \"I forecast $670.5 billion in CY2026 data-center capex, which falls in rung 3. The closest market benchmark by company coverage is Zacks's $720 billion to $745 billion estimate for all capex, numerically in rung 4. The company spending revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support $745 billion of total capex. Project delays and grid constraints temper the pace, and the additional AWS GPU deployment is scheduled after CY2026. Applying my 90% data-center share to the total puts the forecast below $700 billion. The spending mix is the biggest uncertainty near that cutoff.\", 'reference_unavailable': \"The packet gives four-company estimates for all capital expenditure and for broadly defined AI infrastructure. NVIDIA's data-center figure includes a fifth company. The sources provide no quoted allocation that isolates data-center capex for the four companies.\"}\nF1 source four largest AI hyperscalers|total capital expenditure|combined|CY2026 None 720 745 basis=third_party evidence_id=e5014 quote_number=$720 billion to $745 billion qualifier=roughly\nF2 source four largest AI hyperscalers|total capital expenditure|combined|CY2026 760 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2458 quote_number=$760 billion\nF3 source Alphabet|total capital expenditure|company-wide|CY2026 None 195 205 basis=guidance evidence_id=e1254 quote_number=$195 billion to $205 billion\nF4 source Amazon|total capital expenditure|company-wide|CY2026 220 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e1649 quote_number=$220 billion\nF5 source Meta Platforms|total capital expenditure|company-wide|FY2026 None 125 145 basis=guidance evidence_id=e1549 quote_number=$125 billion to $145 billion\nF6 source Meta Platforms|AI capital expenditure|company-wide|CY2026 130 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e3686 quote_number=$130 billion\nF7 source Microsoft|total capital expenditure|company-wide|FY2026 Q3 30.88 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e922 quote_number=$30.88 billion\nF8 source Microsoft|year-over-year capital expenditure growth|company-wide|FY2026 Q3 84 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e922 quote_number=84%\nF9 source four largest AI hyperscalers|AI infrastructure spending|combined|CY2026 730 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e1947 quote_number=$730 billion qualifier=more than\nF10 source five largest AI hyperscalers|data-center capex|combined|CY2026 800 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5376 quote_number=$800 billion qualifier=nearly\nF11 estimate four largest AI hyperscalers|total capital expenditure|combined|CY2026 745 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I expect combined spending to land near the high end of the Zacks range. The Alphabet, Amazon and Meta revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support a strong pace. Amazon's higher memory costs also lift dollar spending. Project delays and grid connection constraints lead me to assume that some planned spending slips, keeping the forecast below the higher published plan. rests_on=['F1', 'F2', 'F3', 'F4', 'F5', 'F7', 'F8', 'e122', 'e5292']\nF12 estimate four largest AI hyperscalers|data-center share of total capital expenditure|combined|CY2026 90 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate that data centers account for 90% of combined capital expenditure. Zacks identifies AI infrastructure as a large use of the group's spending, and the Meta AI capex estimate is close to its total capex guidance. I use that comparison as evidence of a high share and allow for other capital spending across all four companies. The packet gives no group-wide data-center split. rests_on=['F1', 'F5', 'F6', 'e4404']\nF13 computed four largest AI hyperscalers|data-center capex|combined|CY2026 670.5 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F11', 'F12'] operation=share outcome_for=['v6']\nOther: {}\n\n R08 {'case': 'P-v8', 'writer': 'codex'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v8', 'rung': 2, 'reasons': \"I forecast about $388 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue, in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion segment estimate is the direct outside reference and also falls in rung 2. The reported first half contributes $164.27 billion. I forecast about $104 billion in Q3 and $120 billion in Q4. NVIDIA's Q3 company guidance anchors the pace. The prior guidance beat and initial Rubin shipments support a modest beat, and the live CoreWeave deployment and management's expectation of faster hyperscaler growth support a further Q4 step-up. Memory constraints and long delivery lead times limit how much of that demand can ship by year-end, keeping the forecast below $400 billion. The calendar 2026 spending theme supports demand this fiscal year. The higher calendar 2027 spending theme offers some late-year upside, with most of the spending window falling after NVIDIA's fiscal close. Possible H200 authorization adds limited Q4 upside because authorization may come late and shipment timing is uncertain. The other export-policy themes leave near-term shipment assumptions unchanged.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nF1 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 361 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2295 quote_number=$361 billion reference_for=['v8']\nF2 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q1 75.25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e109 quote_number=$75.25 billion\nF3 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q2 89.02 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e5079 quote_number=$89.02 billion\nF4 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 H1 164.27 None None basis=reported inputs=['F2', 'F3'] operation=sum\nF5 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q2 96.22 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e5078 quote_number=$96.22 billion\nF6 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 108 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF7 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 113.6 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5056 quote_number=$113.6 billion qualifier=roughly\nF8 estimate NVIDIA|sequential revenue growth|company|FY2027 Q3 16 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate 16% sequential growth from Q2. The Q2 guidance beat, the start of Rubin shipments and expected growth from AI cloud, industrial and enterprise customers support a Q3 beat. Supply constraints lead me to forecast a smaller beat than Pradhan's extrapolation. rests_on=['F5', 'F6', 'F7', 'e3200', 'e2693', 'e5061', 'e5067']\nF9 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 111.62 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F5', 'F8'] operation=apply_growth\nF10 estimate NVIDIA|sequential revenue growth|company|FY2027 Q4 15 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate 15% sequential growth from my Q3 forecast. Management expects hyperscaler growth to pick up as Rubin supply builds, and CoreWeave already has a multi-rack Rubin deployment online. I expect a larger dollar gain on the higher Q3 base, with memory and long delivery lead times limiting the pace. rests_on=['F8', 'F9', 'e3406', 'e5118', 'e5067', 'e5205']\nF11 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q4 128.36 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F10'] operation=apply_growth\nF12 estimate NVIDIA|share of company revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q3 93 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate a modest rise from the reported Q2 mix because NVIDIA identifies Data Center as the main driver of Q3 growth. The estimate allows Edge Computing revenue to grow as well. rests_on=['F3', 'F5', 'e3399', 'e5059', 'e5223']\nF13 estimate NVIDIA|share of company revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q4 93.5 None None basis=forecast basis_text=I estimate another modest mix gain as hyperscaler sales pick up with Rubin availability. This leaves room for sequential growth in the rest of the company. rests_on=['F12', 'e3399', 'e3406', 'e5059', 'e5118']\nF14 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q3 103.8 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F12'] operation=share\nF15 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q4 120.01 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F11', 'F13'] operation=share\nF16 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 H2 223.81 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F14', 'F15'] operation=sum\nF17 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 388.08 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F4', 'F16'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v8']\nOther: {}\n\n R09 {'case': 'P-v4', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v4', 'rung': 2, 'reasons': \"Reference: the Wall Street consensus for FY2027 revenue is $411 billion (e4333), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion); the Zacks consensus of 87.4% growth on FY2026's $215.9 billion (about $405 billion, e4990) is also rung 2. Outcome: the first half is reported at $177.8 billion (Q1 $81.62 billion, Q2 $96.2 billion). Q3 is guided to $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%, above the $104.6 billion consensus for the quarter; NVIDIA beat its guidance midpoint by 5.7% in Q2 and 4.6% in Q4 FY2026 and has topped the high end of guidance in each of the past 12 quarters, so I forecast Q3 at about $112.9 billion (4.5% above the midpoint, trimmed because capacity is sold out and supply covers roughly 70% of demand). For Q4 I forecast about 14% sequential growth to about $128.7 billion: management says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds, Vera Rubin is in production shipments with purchase orders from every major hyperscaler and is guided to about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue at a higher revenue per gigawatt ($40 billion versus $25 billion for Blackwell), and supply commitments rose from $119 billion to $279 billion; the pace stays below the roughly 18% realized in Q2 because supply remains constrained through FY2028. FY2027 = 177.8 + 112.9 + 128.7, about $419 billion: rung 2, the same rung as the reference, a little above consensus. Rung 1 would need Q4 above about $140.5 billion, a 24.5% sequential jump against the supply limits management states. Rung 3 would need Q3 plus Q4 below about $214 billion, for example Q3 at the guidance low end and Q4 flat near $108 billion, which contradicts the beat record and the reacceleration management describes. The '70% full-year growth' reports (e3280, e3890, e4628) are not used for FY2027: the CFO's own words (e2691) put approximately 70% growth on next year, which e5227 identifies as FY2028; applied to FY2027 it would mean about $367 billion and a Q4 near $81 billion, below Q2, inconsistent with the $108 billion Q3 guidance. Competing accelerator commitments (AMD with OpenAI and Meta, Broadcom's $115 billion AI revenue for 2027) deploy mainly in calendar 2027 and NVIDIA is supply-bound now, so they do not lower FY2027. Themes in force: limited Chinese authorization of H200 purchases (judged likely) is upside the zero-China-compute guidance excludes, small and at most in Q4, so my Q4 pace does not assume zero China; hyperscaler 2026 data-center capital expenditure near $760 billion supports demand above supply; 2027 hyperscaler spending falls mainly in FY2028. None of these moves the outcome across $431.2 billion or $392 billion.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nF1 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q1 81.62 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e111 quote_number=$81.62 billion\nF2 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q2 96.2 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2649 quote_number=$96.2 billion\nF3 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 H1 177.82 None None basis=reported inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=sum\nF4 computed NVIDIA|revenue sequential growth|company|FY2027 Q2 17.86 None None basis=reported inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=growth\nF5 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 108.0 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF6 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q2 91.0 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e3200 quote_number=$91.0 billion\nF7 computed NVIDIA|revenue beat versus guidance midpoint|company|FY2027 Q2 5.71 None None basis=reported inputs=['F6', 'F2'] operation=growth\nF8 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2026 Q4 65 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2452 quote_number=$65 billion\nF9 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2026 Q4 68 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2452 quote_number=$68 billion\nF10 computed NVIDIA|revenue beat versus guidance midpoint|company|FY2026 Q4 4.62 None None basis=reported inputs=['F8', 'F9'] operation=growth\nF11 estimate NVIDIA|revenue beat versus guidance midpoint|company|FY2027 Q3 4.5 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate: Q3 FY2027 revenue lands about 4.5% above the guidance midpoint. NVIDIA beat its midpoint by 5.7% in Q2 FY2027 and by 4.6% in Q4 FY2026, and has exceeded the high end of guidance in each of the past 12 quarters, by 3.1% above the high end on average over the last four (about 5% above the midpoint). I trim slightly below that record because capacity is sold out and supply covers only about 70% of demand, which limits further upside surprises. rests_on=['F7', 'F10', 'e5054', 'e5055', 'e4911', 'e3733']\nF12 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 112.86 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F5', 'F11'] operation=apply_growth\nF13 estimate NVIDIA|revenue sequential growth|company|FY2027 Q4 14 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate: Q4 FY2027 revenue grows about 14% sequentially from the Q3 forecast. The benchmark is the roughly 18% sequential growth realized in Q2 FY2027; I set the pace below it because management expects supply to stay constrained through FY2028 and supply covers only about 70% of demand. The pace stays well above flat because management says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds, Vera Rubin is in production shipments with purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and OEM, and supply commitments more than doubled to $279 billion. The Q3 guidance itself implies about 12% sequential growth at its midpoint, a pace NVIDIA has typically exceeded. The pace does not assume zero China Data Center revenue. rests_on=['F4', 'e3406', 'e2693', 'e2759', 'e5067', 'e3733']\nF14 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q4 128.66 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F12', 'F13'] operation=apply_growth\nF15 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 419.34 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F3', 'F12', 'F14'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v4']\nF16 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 411 None None basis=consensus evidence_id=e4333 quote_number=$411 billion reference_for=['v4']\nOther: {}\n\n R10 {'case': 'P-v8', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v8', 'rung': 2, 'reasons': \"Reference: the only outside figure for this exact quantity is Morningstar's FY2027 Data Center estimate of $361 billion (F19, e2295), which sits in rung 2 ($350-400 billion); it was published before the Q2 report. For the whole company, analysts expect $411 billion of FY2027 revenue (F23); my company forecast is $418.2 billion (F22), slightly above, in line with NVIDIA's habit of beating guidance. Calculation: reported Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89.0 billion in Q2, so $164.25 billion in H1 (F3). Q3 company revenue is guided at $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%; NVIDIA beat the midpoint by 5.7% in Q2 and 4.6% in Q4 FY2026 and has beaten the high end for 12 straight quarters, so I forecast a 5% beat, $113.4 billion (F11, F12, my estimate). For Q4 I keep the guided 12% sequential pace (F13, my estimate): hyperscale growth is set to reaccelerate as Vera Rubin supply builds and supply commitments rose to $279 billion, but supply covers only about 70% of demand and stays tight through FY2028; that gives $127.0 billion (F14). Data Center was 92.5% of Q2 revenue and its share has risen every quarter; I assume 92.8% for H2 (F16), so H2 Data Center revenue is $223.1 billion (F17) and FY2027 Data Center revenue is $387.4 billion (F18), rung 2, the same rung as the reference but well above it, because Q2 and the Q3 guidance came in after the reference. Boundaries: falling below $350 billion would need H2 Data Center revenue under $185.8 billion, below the Q2 pace, although the Q3 guidance alone implies about $100 billion a quarter. Reaching $400 billion would need $235.8 billion, about 20% sequential Data Center growth in both quarters while supply is capped. So rung 1 is the upside risk (SemiAnalysis expects H2 Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates, e1135, but gives no base figure), not the most likely outcome. The reports of '70% growth for the full fiscal year' (e3280) refer to FY2028 per the earnings call (e2691); applied to FY2027 they would contradict H1 plus the Q3 guidance, so they move nothing. The H1 run-rate of just under $330 billion (e5310) annualises a past half and is not used. Competing accelerator deals (e1040, e4314) mostly deploy after FY2027 while NVIDIA sells all it can supply, so they do not move this year. Themes: China authorizing H200 purchases (judged likely) is an upside the Q3 guidance excludes; no evidence sizes it, and it is not large enough to lift the outcome past $400 billion on its own. Hyperscaler capex for 2026 sits about at the market reference, and the raised 2027 view supports the Q4 reacceleration, but supply, not demand, limits FY2027.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nF1 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q1 75.25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e109 quote_number=$75.25 billion\nF2 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q2 89.0 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2650 quote_number=$89.0 billion\nF3 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 H1 164.25 None None basis=reported inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=sum\nF4 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q2 96.2 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2649 quote_number=$96.2 billion\nF5 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q2 91.0 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e3200 quote_number=$91.0 billion\nF6 computed NVIDIA|revenue beat over guidance midpoint|company|FY2027 Q2 5.71 None None basis=reported inputs=['F5', 'F4'] operation=growth\nF7 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2026 Q4 65 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2452 quote_number=$65 billion\nF8 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2026 Q4 68 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2452 quote_number=$68 billion\nF9 computed NVIDIA|revenue beat over guidance midpoint|company|FY2026 Q4 4.62 None None basis=reported inputs=['F7', 'F8'] operation=growth\nF10 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 108.0 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF11 estimate NVIDIA|revenue beat over guidance midpoint|company|FY2027 Q3 5.0 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate. NVIDIA beat its revenue guidance midpoint by 5.7% in FY2027 Q2 (F6) and by 4.6% in FY2026 Q4 (F9), and has beaten the high end of guidance in each of the last 12 quarters, by 3.1% on average over the last four (about 5% above the midpoint; e5054, e5055). Capacity is sold out, which limits further upside (e4911), so I assume a 5% beat on the $108.0 billion Q3 midpoint, not more than the recent record. rests_on=['F6', 'F9', 'e5054', 'e5055', 'e4911']\nF12 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 113.4 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F10', 'F11'] operation=apply_growth\nF13 estimate NVIDIA|revenue growth quarter over quarter|company|FY2027 Q4 12.0 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate of the Q4 sequential pace, benchmarked on the 12% sequential growth the Q3 guidance implies at its midpoint (e5005). Upward forces: NVIDIA says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406, e5061, e4283), supply and capacity commitments jumped to $279 billion (e2759), and Vera Rubin carries more revenue per gigawatt (e4202). Limits: supply covers only about 70% of demand and stays constrained through FY2028 (e3733, e5067), and my Q3 base already includes a beat. So I keep the guided 12% pace rather than the 17-18% delivered in recent quarters. The pace leaves room for limited H200 sales to China late in the year if purchases are authorized; no passage sizes such sales, so no separate figure is made. rests_on=['e5005', 'F10', 'F4', 'e3406', 'e5061', 'e4283', 'e2759', 'e4202', 'e3733', 'e5067']\nF14 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q4 127.01 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F12', 'F13'] operation=apply_growth\nF15 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 H2 240.41 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F12', 'F14'] operation=sum\nF16 estimate NVIDIA|Data Center share of revenue|company|FY2027 H2 92.8 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate. Data Center was 92.5% of revenue in FY2027 Q2 ($89.0 billion of $96.2 billion; F2, F4) and its share has risen every quarter, from 87.9% to 92.5%, with the latest step only 0.3 points (e3399). Growth in Q3 is driven primarily by the AI data center business (e5223), so I assume the share keeps edging up, to an average of 92.8% for H2. rests_on=['F2', 'F4', 'e3399', 'e5223']\nF17 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 H2 223.1 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F15', 'F16'] operation=share\nF18 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 387.35 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F3', 'F17'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v8']\nF19 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 361 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2295 quote_number=$361 billion reference_for=['v8']\nF20 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q1 81.62 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e111 quote_number=$81.62 billion\nF21 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 H1 177.82 None None basis=reported inputs=['F20', 'F4'] operation=sum\nF22 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 418.23 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F21', 'F15'] operation=sum\nF23 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 411 None None basis=consensus evidence_id=e4333 quote_number=$411 billion\nOther: {}\n\n R11 {'case': 'P', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v4', 'rung': 2, 'reasons': \"Reference: Wall Street consensus of $411 billion for FY2027 (F19) sits in rung 2 ($392-431.2 billion); the 87-88% consensus growth rates (e4990, e5129) on FY2026's $215.9 billion give about $405 billion, also rung 2. Outcome from the evidence: H1 is reported at $177.8 billion (Q1 $81.62 billion, Q2 $96.2 billion; F3). Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%, excluding China data center compute; given 12 straight beats of the high end (3.1% on average over the last four) and a $5.2 billion beat of the Q2 midpoint, trimmed for the stated memory, power and packaging caps, I forecast Q3 at $112.5 billion (F9). For Q4 I forecast 12% sequential growth, the pace guided for Q3, because hyperscale growth is said to reaccelerate in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds and supply commitments rose to $279 billion, while supply stays constrained (F11): Q4 about $126 billion, H2 $238.5 billion, FY2027 about $416 billion (F14), rung 2, above consensus but inside the same band. Sensitivity: Q3 at the guidance midpoint and only 5% Q4 growth still gives about $399 billion (rung 2); rung 1 ($431.2 billion) would need H2 of about $253 billion, e.g. Q4 near $138 billion (+20% sequential), which a supply-constrained company is not shown to deliver. The reports of '70% full-year growth' (e3280, e3890, e4628) conflict with the call transcript (e2691), which ties about 70% growth to next year (FY2028) because of supply limits; 70% on $215.9 billion ($367 billion) would also require Q4 to fall to about $81 billion, below Q1, so I read them as the FY2028 outlook. The analysts' quarterly path (90% Q3 growth, 65% Q4 growth: e5161, e5162) implies only about $398 billion and predates the Q3 guide's stated pace. Themes: China authorizing H200 purchases (v9) is judged more likely than not; Q3 guidance assumes no China data center compute, and I do not assume China at zero for Q4, but any such sales before the late-January year end are small and supply-limited, so they sit inside the Q4 pace rather than being added on top. Hyperscaler capex judged at about $760 billion for 2026 (v6) and above the market reference for 2027 (v7, updated) supports the Q4 hyperscale reacceleration. The export-control themes judged 'No' (v16, v22, v26, v27) keep the status quo already in guidance.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v8', 'rung': 2, 'reasons': \"Reference: Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 Data Center estimate (F20), the only quoted outside figure for the segment, sits in rung 2 ($350-400 billion). Applying a quarter's mix to the $411 billion consensus would be my own estimate, not the market's. Outcome from the family's figures: H1 Data Center revenue is reported at $164.25 billion (Q1 $75.25 billion, Q2 $89.0 billion; $164 billion per e5309). Data Center's share of revenue has risen every quarter, from 87.9% to 92.5% in Q2 (e3399). Management says Q3 growth comes primarily from the AI data center business (ACIE leading, Vera Rubin about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue), while Edge Computing, the remainder ($7.2 billion in Q2), grew a slower 13%. I therefore take Data Center at 93.0% of the $238.5 billion H2 revenue forecast (F16), giving $221.8 billion for H2 and about $386 billion for FY2027 (F18), rung 2. That stays below the total's $416 billion, so the part does not exceed the whole. It sits above Morningstar's pre-Q2 estimate, consistent with SemiAnalysis's view that H2 Data Center revenue will beat analyst estimates by 20% (e1135). The 'just under $330 billion' run rate (e5310) annualizes H1 flat and is not a forecast. Sensitivity: H2 at the guidance midpoint with 5% Q4 growth and a 92.5% share gives about $369 billion (rung 2). Rung 1 ($400 billion) needs about $236 billion of H2 Data Center revenue, i.e. H2 company revenue near $253 billion, the same acceleration the supply constraints (e2870, e3115, e5067) argue against. Rung 3 would need H2 Data Center below about $186 billion, under even the low end of Q3 guidance carried flat. e4907's $83.7 billion Q2 figure conflicts with the company's $89.0 billion release and is disregarded. Themes: possible China H200 authorization (v9, judged more likely than not) is Data Center upside that guidance excludes; it is not assumed to be zero but is small and supply-limited within FY2027. The hyperscaler capex themes (v6, v7) support Q4 hyperscale demand. The export-control themes judged 'No' keep the status quo.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nF1 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q1 81.62 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e111 quote_number=$81.62 billion\nF2 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q2 96.2 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2649 quote_number=$96.2 billion\nF3 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 H1 177.82 None None basis=reported inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=sum\nF4 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q1 75.25 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e109 quote_number=$75.25 billion\nF5 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q2 89.0 None None basis=reported evidence_id=e2650 quote_number=$89.0 billion\nF6 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 H1 164.25 None None basis=reported inputs=['F4', 'F5'] operation=sum\nF7 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 None 105.84 110.16 basis=guidance evidence_id=e2652 quote_number=$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\nF8 source NVIDIA|revenue above the high end of guidance, four-quarter average|company|FY2026 Q3 to FY2027 Q2 3.1 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e5055 quote_number=3.1%\nF9 estimate NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q3 112.5 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate: Q3 revenue lands about 4% above the $108.0 billion guidance midpoint and about 2% above its $110.16 billion high end. NVIDIA has beaten the high end in each of the past 12 quarters, by 3.1% on average over the last four (which would give about $113.6 billion), and beat the Q2 midpoint by $5.2 billion and the Q4 FY2026 guide by $3 billion; I take part of that pattern rather than all of it because management and outside analysts say memory, power and packaging bottlenecks cap near-term output and capacity is sold out. rests_on=['F7', 'F8', 'e5054', 'e3200', 'e2452', 'e4911', 'e3115']\nF10 source NVIDIA|revenue growth quarter over quarter|company|FY2027 Q3 12 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e4899 quote_number=12%\nF11 estimate NVIDIA|revenue growth quarter over quarter|company|FY2027 Q4 12 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate of Q4 sequential growth: 12%, the pace guided for Q3 (benchmark F10). It applies because management says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds, supply and capacity commitments rose from $119 billion to $279 billion, and the supply-capped outlook of about 70% growth in FY2028 needs low-to-mid-teens sequential growth from the Q4 exit; actual sequential growth has run 17-20% (Q1 to Q2, and Q2 to my Q3 estimate), but management expects supply to stay constrained through FY2028, so I do not carry that faster actual pace into Q4. rests_on=['F10', 'F2', 'F9', 'e3406', 'e4283', 'e2759', 'e2691', 'e5067']\nF12 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 Q4 126.0 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F11'] operation=apply_growth\nF13 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 H2 238.5 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F9', 'F12'] operation=sum\nF14 computed NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 416.32 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F3', 'F13'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v4']\nF15 source NVIDIA|share of revenue|Data Center|FY2027 Q2 92.5 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e3399 quote_number=92.5%\nF16 estimate NVIDIA|share of revenue|Data Center|FY2027 H2 93.0 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate: Data Center is 93.0% of H2 revenue, slightly above Q2's 92.5% (89.0 of 96.2). The share has risen every quarter (87.9%, 89.8%, 91.5%, 92.2%, 92.5%), with the gains narrowing; management says Q3 growth comes primarily from the AI data center business (ACIE, with Vera Rubin about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue), while Edge Computing, the remainder, grew a slower 13% sequentially in Q2. rests_on=['F15', 'F5', 'F2', 'e5059', 'e5223', 'e4283', 'e2721']\nF17 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 H2 221.81 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F13', 'F16'] operation=share\nF18 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 386.06 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F6', 'F17'] operation=sum outcome_for=['v8']\nF19 source NVIDIA|revenue|company|FY2027 411 None None basis=consensus evidence_id=e4333 quote_number=$411 billion reference_for=['v4']\nF20 source NVIDIA|revenue|Data Center|FY2027 361 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e2295 quote_number=$361 billion reference_for=['v8']\nOther: {}\n\n R12 {'case': 'E', 'writer': 'claude'}\nAnswer: {'view_id': 'v41', 'rung': 3, 'reasons': \"Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\", 'reference_unavailable': None}\nF1 source NVIDIA|revenue|Vera CPU|FY2027 20 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e3660 quote_number=$20 billion\nF2 source NVIDIA|revenue growth multiple|Vera CPU|FY2028 2 None None basis=guidance evidence_id=e4856 quote_number=double qualifier=more than\nF3 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Vera CPU|FY2028 40 None None basis=guidance inputs=['F1', 'F2'] operation=product reference_for=['v41']\nF4 source server CPU market|revenue growth|server CPU|annual rate through CY2030 50 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e4687 quote_number=50%\nF5 source CPU market|revenue|CPU|CY2026 35 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e4857 quote_number=$35 billion\nF6 source CPU market|revenue|CPU|CY2030 170 None None basis=third_party evidence_id=e4857 quote_number=$170 billion\nF7 estimate NVIDIA|revenue growth|Vera CPU|FY2028 85 None None basis=forecast basis_text=My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth on the FY2027 base. Management's 'more than double' (F2, at least 100% growth) is the benchmark, but it is a target for a fiscal year that has not started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full. Outside views put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD, F4; Bank of America's fivefold path from F5 to F6), so a doubling needs large share gains. Broad announced adoption (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689; Anthropic evaluating Vera, e5044; Arm servers' large revenue share, e4692) supports a pace well above the market, while AMD's own benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) show standalone CPU share will be contested. Weighting the company target most heavily, tempered toward the market pace, gives 85%. rests_on=['F2', 'F4', 'F5', 'F6', 'e4689', 'e5044', 'e4692', 'e5399', 'e5400', 'e5401', 'e5402', 'e5404']\nF8 computed NVIDIA|revenue|Vera CPU|FY2028 37 None None basis=forecast inputs=['F1', 'F7'] operation=apply_growth outcome_for=['v41']\nOther: {}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba render_v13.py | sed -n '80,190p' && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '489,595p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba render_v13.py | sed -n '80,190p' && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '489,595p'\"","aggregated_output":"    80\t\n    81\tdef v13s_schema(member_ids, evidence_ids, theme_ids):\n    82\t    \"\"\"The small format (SMALL-DESIGN.md §3 [52], D737, D738): each rule the checks enforce stated beside its field.\"\"\"\n    83\t    members = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string', 'enum': member_ids}}\n    84\t    what = obj({'population': S,\n    85\t                'measure': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.'},\n    86\t                'scope': S,\n    87\t                'period': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.'}},\n    88\t               'What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.')\n    89\t    figure = obj({\n    90\t        'id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.'},\n    91\t        'what': what,\n    92\t        'kind': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['source', 'computed', 'estimate'], 'description': \"source: a number written in a shown passage's \"\n    93\t                 'quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.'},\n    94\t        'basis': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['reported', 'guidance', 'consensus', 'third_party', 'forecast'], 'description': \"reported: an \"\n    95\t                  \"actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this \"\n    96\t                  \"family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\"},\n    97\t        'value': nullable({'type': 'number', 'description': 'A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.'}),\n    98\t        'low': nullable({'type': 'number', 'description': 'A range, low at most high, with value null.'}),\n    99\t        'high': nullable({'type': 'number', 'description': 'A range, low at most high, with value null.'}),\n   100\t        'unit': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['USD bn', '%', 'x'], 'description': 'USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = '\n   101\t                 '1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).'},\n   102\t        'evidence_id': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids, 'description': 'Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.'}),\n   103\t        'quote_number': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a \"\n   104\t                                  \"piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent \"\n   105\t                                  \"sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', \"\n   106\t                                  \"'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written \"\n   107\t                                  \"range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change \"\n   108\t                                  \"written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, \"\n   109\t                                  \"plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\"}),\n   110\t        'qualifier': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': QUALIFIERS, 'description': \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before \"\n   111\t                               \"the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\"}),\n   112\t        'inputs': nullable({'type': 'array', 'items': S, 'description': \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\"}),\n   113\t        'operation': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': ['sum', 'difference', 'product', 'ratio', 'share', 'growth', 'apply_growth'],\n   114\t                               'description': 'Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); '\n   115\t                               'product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units '\n   116\t                               'give x; a ÷ x keeps a\\'s unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; '\n   117\t                               'growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and '\n   118\t                               'product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range '\n   119\t                               'input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the '\n   120\t                               'midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds '\n   121\t                               \"a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the \"\n   122\t                               'part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds '\n   123\t                               'one quantity for the same period twice. When '\n   124\t                               'a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 '\n   125\t                               \"Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\"}),\n   126\t        'basis_text': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Estimate only: the assumption, in words.'}),\n   127\t        'rests_on': nullable({'type': 'array', 'items': S, 'description': 'Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.'}),\n   128\t        'outcome_for': dict(members, description='The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure '\n   129\t                            'with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, '\n   130\t                            \"none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\"),\n   131\t        'reference_for': dict(members, description=\"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath \"\n   132\t                              'it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted '\n   133\t                              \"consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period \"\n   134\t                              \"(a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\"),\n   135\t    })\n   136\t    push = obj({'evidence_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['toward_first', 'toward_last', 'none']}})\n   137\t    theme_push = obj({'view_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': theme_ids or ['none']},\n   138\t                      'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['toward_first', 'toward_last', 'none']}})\n   139\t    answer = obj({\n   140\t        'view_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': member_ids},\n   141\t        'shape': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['forecast', 'unresolved'], 'description': 'forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and '\n   142\t                  'a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable '\n   143\t                  'null; no figure marked for it.'},\n   144\t        'rung': nullable({'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it '\n   145\t                          '(a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.'}),\n   146\t        'reference_unavailable': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.'}),\n   147\t        'reasons': S,\n   148\t        'why': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.'}),\n   149\t        'cites': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids}},\n   150\t        'pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': push}, 'theme_pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': theme_push},\n   151\t    })\n   152\t    return obj({'figures': {'type': 'array', 'items': figure, 'description': 'The family\\'s calculation. Use one forecast figure for each '\n   153\t                            'quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a '\n   154\t                            'point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why '\n   155\t                            'it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared '\n   156\t                            'quantity\\'s guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. '\n   157\t                            'To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the '\n   158\t                            'deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. '\n   159\t                            'Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.'},\n   160\t                'answers': {'type': 'array', 'items': answer}})\n   161\t\n   162\t\n   163\tdef v13s_header(family, shown):\n   164\t    \"\"\"[52] The family header's rule lines, rendered from the family's data.\"\"\"\n   165\t    lines = []\n   166\t    for r in family.get('relations', []):\n   167\t        if r['type'] == 'part_of' and {r['part'], r['whole']} <= shown:\n   168\t            periods = ', '.join(p for p in family.get('compare_periods', []) if p != family['members'][r['part']]['what']['period'])\n   169\t            lines.append(f'Relations: {r[\"part\"]} is part of {r[\"whole\"]}. For each of {periods} and for the outcomes, the part\\'s figure may '\n   170\t                         'not exceed the whole\\'s: for every value of anything both rest on, and otherwise the part\\'s low end may not exceed '\n   171\t                         'the whole\\'s high end.')\n   172\t    if not lines:\n   173\t        lines.append('Relations: none.')\n   174\t    for ps in family.get('part_scopes', []):\n   175\t        lines.append(f'Scopes: {ps[\"population\"]} {ps[\"measure\"]} {ps[\"part\"]} is part of {ps[\"population\"]} {ps[\"measure\"]} {ps[\"whole\"]}. A sum '\n   176\t                     'never adds the part into the whole for the same period; a difference may take the part from the whole of the same '\n   177\t                     'period only to give the rest.')\n   178\t    shared = '; '.join(f'{w[\"population\"]}, {w[\"measure\"]}, {w[\"scope\"]}, {w[\"period\"]}' for w in family.get('shared', [])) or 'none'\n   179\t    lines.append(f'Shared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, '\n   180\t                 f'the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: {shared}.')\n   181\t    keys = '; '.join(f'{v} = {m[\"what\"][\"population\"]} | {m[\"what\"][\"measure\"]} | {m[\"what\"][\"scope\"]} | {m[\"what\"][\"period\"]}'\n   182\t                     + (f' ({m[\"key_note\"]})' if m.get('key_note') else '') for v, m in family.get('members', {}).items() if v in shown)\n   183\t    lines.append(f'Outcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: {keys}.')\n   184\t    return '\\n'.join(lines) + '\\n'\n   185\t\n   186\t\n   187\tdef parts(user):\n   188\t    head, rest = user.split('\\n\\n[Evidence in view', 1)\n   189\t    evidence = '[Evidence in view' + rest.split('\\n\\n[', 1)[0]\n   190\t    tail = '\\n\\n[' + rest.split('\\n\\n[', 1)[1] if '\\n\\n[' in rest else ''\n   489\t        for start, end in starts:\n   490\t            before, after = text[:start], text[end:]\n   491\t            found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_BEFORE if re.search(r'(?:^|\\W)' + re.escape(w) + r'\\s*$', before, re.I)), None)\n   492\t            if found is None:\n   493\t                found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_AFTER if re.match(r'^\\s*,?\\s*' + re.escape(w) + r'\\b', after, re.I)), None)\n   494\t            written.append(found or 'none')\n   495\t        if q not in written:\n   496\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: the quote writes \"{written[0] if written else \"none\"}\" with \"{qn}\", but the qualifier is {q}'))\n   497\t    return errors\n   498\t\n   499\t\n   500\tdef _arithmetic(figs, calc):\n   501\t    errors = []\n   502\t    for f in figs.values():\n   503\t        if f['kind'] != 'computed':\n   504\t            continue\n   505\t        fid, op, ins = f['id'], f['operation'], f['inputs']\n   506\t        unit = _result_unit(op, [figs[i]['unit'] for i in ins])\n   507\t        if unit is None:\n   508\t            hint = ' (use share for a whole times a percentage)' if op == 'product' else ''\n   509\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: {op} cannot take inputs in {\", \".join(figs[i][\"unit\"] for i in ins)}{hint}'))\n   510\t            continue\n   511\t        if unit != f['unit']:\n   512\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: {op} of these inputs gives {unit}, not {f[\"unit\"]}'))\n   513\t            continue\n   514\t        got = {i: calc.interval(i) for i in ins}\n   515\t        if not all(got.values()):\n   516\t            continue                                       # an input that cannot be evaluated is refused at that input\n   517\t        if op in ('ratio', 'growth'):\n   518\t            denominator = got[ins[1]] if op == 'ratio' else got[ins[0]]\n   519\t            if denominator[0] <= 0 <= denominator[1]:\n   520\t                errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: divides by a figure whose value can be zero'))\n   521\t                continue\n   522\t        if op == 'share' and not (0 <= got[ins[1]][0] and got[ins[1]][1] <= 100):\n   523\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: a share takes a percentage from 0 to 100'))\n   524\t            continue\n   525\t        value = calc.interval(fid)\n   526\t        if value is None:\n   527\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: the value cannot be evaluated'))\n   528\t            continue\n   529\t        lo, hi = value\n   530\t        if _is_range(f):\n   531\t            ok = _close(f['low'], lo, unit) and _close(f['high'], hi, unit)\n   532\t        else:\n   533\t            ok = _close(f['value'], (lo + hi) / 2, unit)\n   534\t        if not ok:\n   535\t            declared = f'{f[\"low\"]:g}–{f[\"high\"]:g}' if _is_range(f) else f'{f[\"value\"]:g}'\n   536\t            got_text = f'{lo:.4g}' if lo == hi else f'{lo:.4g}–{hi:.4g}'\n   537\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: declared {declared} but {op} of {\", \".join(ins)} gives {got_text}'))\n   538\t    return errors\n   539\t\n   540\t\n   541\tdef _overlaps_band(interval, band):\n   542\t    lo, hi = interval\n   543\t    b_lo = band[0] if band[0] is not None else -math.inf\n   544\t    b_hi = band[1] if band[1] is not None else math.inf\n   545\t    if lo == hi:\n   546\t        return b_lo <= lo < b_hi\n   547\t    return lo < b_hi and hi >= b_lo\n   548\t\n   549\t\n   550\tdef _answers(family, figs, calc, answers):\n   551\t    errors, outcomes = [], {}\n   552\t    members = family['members']\n   553\t    for a in answers:\n   554\t        v = a.get('view_id')\n   555\t        member = members.get(v)\n   556\t        if member is None or a.get('shape') == 'unresolved':\n   557\t            continue\n   558\t        out = figs.get(a.get('outcome'))\n   559\t        if out is None:\n   560\t            continue                                       # reported by from_answer\n   561\t        if out['kind'] not in ('computed', 'estimate') or out['basis'] != 'forecast':\n   562\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: its outcome {out[\"id\"]} must be your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast)'))\n   563\t        if _key(out['what']) != _key(member['what']):\n   564\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} measures {\" | \".join(_key(out[\"what\"]))}, not the question\\'s {\" | \".join(_key(member[\"what\"]))}'))\n   565\t        if out['unit'] != member.get('unit', 'USD bn'):\n   566\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} is in {out[\"unit\"]}, not {member.get(\"unit\", \"USD bn\")}'))\n   567\t        outcomes[v] = out['id']\n   568\t        rung = a.get('rung')\n   569\t        if not isinstance(rung, int) or isinstance(rung, bool) or not 1 <= rung <= len(member['bands']):\n   570\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: rung {rung!r} is not one of 1..{len(member[\"bands\"])}'))\n   571\t            continue\n   572\t        interval = calc.interval(out['id'])\n   573\t        if interval is not None and not _overlaps_band(interval, member['bands'][rung - 1]):\n   574\t            shown = f'{interval[0]:g}' if interval[0] == interval[1] else f'{interval[0]:g}–{interval[1]:g}'\n   575\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: rung {rung} {member[\"bands\"][rung - 1]} does not hold its outcome {shown} as recomputed'))\n   576\t        ref = (a.get('reference') or {}).get('figure')\n   577\t        if ref in figs:\n   578\t            chain = {ref} | _beneath(ref, figs)\n   579\t            if any(figs[g]['kind'] == 'estimate' for g in chain):\n   580\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on an estimate'))\n   581\t            elif any(figs[g]['basis'] == 'forecast' for g in chain):\n   582\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on a forecast'))\n   583\t            elif not any(figs[g]['kind'] == 'source' and figs[g]['basis'] in EXTERNAL for g in chain):\n   584\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} has no quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure'))\n   585\t            else:                                          # [D741] a past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period\n   586\t                carried = sorted(g for g in chain if figs[g]['basis'] == 'reported' and figs[g]['unit'] in ('%', 'x')\n   587\t                                 and figs[g]['what']['period'] != figs[ref]['what']['period'])\n   588\t                if carried:\n   589\t                    errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} carries the reported {\", \".join(carried)} of another period to '\n   590\t                                         f'{figs[ref][\"what\"][\"period\"]}; that makes it our comparison estimate, not the market\\'s reference'))\n   591\t    return errors, outcomes\n   592\t\n   593\t\n   594\tdef _adopted(outcomes, figs):\n   595\t    \"\"\"[54] The figures the outcomes adopt: each reached from an outcome (inputs and rests_on) with no figure of its own quantity and\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
