{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && cat adjudication/R01.codex.json adjudication/R01.claude.json && rg -n 'answers/rung|Reader [12] on member|marks a defect|broken structure' adjudication/R01.md && rg -n '\\\"case\\\"|\\\"decision\\\"|\\\"recorded\\\"|\\\"errors\\\"' CHECKED.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"why\": \"The Alphabet, Meta and Amazon guidance in e1254, e1549 and e1649 sums to $540–570 billion. Adding the stated $175 billion Microsoft estimate gives $715–745 billion in total capex. Applying the stated 95–100% data-center share gives $679.25–745 billion. The midpoint of $712.125 billion and most of the range fall in rung 4. The exact share remains an assumption. e5014 says only that a 'large portion' goes to AI infrastructure, and e5376 covers five companies.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n      \"type\": \"broken structure\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F14\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"The format permits 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.' F14 overlaps rung 4 from $700 billion to $745 billion, and the answer explains its choice using the midpoint and the portion of the range in that band.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"The format says 'outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine.' F8, F9 and F10 are outside totals used to estimate Microsoft's contribution in F6 alongside its spending run-rate. F7 is the only forecast of the combined total that supplies F14. Because F8 helps inform F6, its agreement with F7 carries no independent weight.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n      \"type\": \"broken structure\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F14\"],\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"why\": \"The format requires 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.' The stated 95% share does allow an outcome in rung 3, and the answer acknowledges that overlap. Its stated reason for choosing rung 4 is that the midpoint and most of the range fall there.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The build follows from the passages and the answer's stated assumptions. The sourced inputs are read correctly: Alphabet '$195 billion to $205 billion' (e1254), Meta '$125 billion to $145 billion' (e1549), Amazon '$220 billion' (e1649) and Microsoft '$30.88 billion, up 84%' (e922). The arithmetic checks: F7 = 195+125+220+175 = 715 and 205+145+220+175 = 745. F14 = share(F7, F13) gives 715 x 0.95 = 679.25 and 745 x 1.00 = 745, midpoint about 712. Microsoft's $175bn (F6) is stated as the writer's own estimate. It sits between a run-rate build (about $154bn at 15% sequential growth from $30.88bn) and the four-company tallies less the other three companies' guidance (e5014 '$720 billion to $745 billion', e1856 '$745 billion', e2458 '$760 billion' and e2079 'guided roughly $720 billion ... to $745 billion', less $540-570bn). The system text permits this: 'a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it.' The data-center share is written as its own figure (F13, 95-100%), as the format rule requires ('write the deduction or share as its own figure'). It rests on passages that give the same top-five total both as capital expenditures (e5081 'nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures') and as data-center capital expenditures (e5376 'nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures'). Further support: e4082 calls ~$750bn 'AI data center infrastructure spending', e5014 says 'a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure', and e1649 attributes Amazon's increase to 'higher memory chip costs'. So the high share is a grounded assumption, not a relabelled total. The range $679.25-745bn overlaps rung 4 for about two thirds of its width and has its midpoint in rung 4. The reasons say why rung 4 was chosen, which the format rule allows for a range outcome. Caveat on weight: the Microsoft estimate draws on the same tallies that serve as the reference F8, so the agreement between forecast and reference is not independent. With Microsoft at the run-rate figure alone, the midpoint would fall near $693bn, in rung 3. Rung 4 therefore depends on the tally-derived estimate, which the passages support.\"}},\n \"defects\": [\n  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"broken structure\", \"figures\": [\"F14\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The format rule for answers/rung reads: 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.' F14 is a range outcome ($679.25-745bn) and overlaps rung 4's band ('at least $700 billion and below $800 billion'). The reasons give the ground: 'midpoint about $712bn ... the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4.' A rung chosen as an overlapped band, with its reason stated, is what the rule permits. It is not a rung whose band fails to hold the outcome.\"},\n  {\"reader\": \"Reader 2\", \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\", \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"F7 is the only forecast figure of the four companies' combined CY2026 capital expenditure. F8, F9 and F10 are source figures with basis third_party (e5014, e1856, e2458), not forecasts. The format rule reads: 'Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period ... (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine).' They sit beneath F7 only through F6's rests_on. There they serve as one of two benchmarks for an estimate of a different quantity, Microsoft's capital expenditure. The value of the combined quantity in the calculation comes from F7 alone (sum of F1, F2, F3, F6). This is the same pattern as the ruling's benchmark exemption, and the system text endorses deriving an unstated figure 'from the accounting identities in the evidence'. No figure depends on itself: F7 is not in F6's rests_on. The point that the reference F8 also feeds the forecast, so the two are not independent, is about how much weight the answer deserves. It is not one of the listed defect types. Calling $175bn 'between' $155bn and $175-190bn is wording at an inclusive edge, not a defect.\"},\n  {\"reader\": \"Reader 2\", \"type\": \"broken structure\", \"figures\": [\"F14\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The format rule for answers/rung reads: 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.' The band need not hold the whole range. F14 ($679.25-745bn) overlaps rung 4 ($700-800bn), and the reasons state why that band was chosen (midpoint about $712bn; most of the range in rung 4). The low end resting on the writer's own 95% bound affects weight, not structure. The share is written as its own figure (F13), as the format rule requires.\"}\n ]}\n21:- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure depending on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome not of the\n29:- Reader 1 on member v6: supported = no. Ground: e1254, e1549 and e1649 supply three company capex figures. The answer adds its $175 billion Microsoft estimate and assumes that 95–100% of combined capex goes to data centers. That yields $679.25–745 billion, with a midpoint of $712.125 billion. Why not: The midpoint is in rung 4, but the stated forecast extends into rung 3. The answer's own assumptions allow that lower result. e5014 gives total capex, and the cited data-center estimates cover different or unspecified groups, so they do not establish the assumed 95–100% share for these four companies.\n30:- Reader 1 marks a defect: broken structure (figures ['F14']): The answer selects rung 4, which starts at $700 billion, but the linked outcome is a range starting at $679.25 billion.\n31:- Reader 2 on member v6: supported = yes. Ground: Sourced guidance: Alphabet $195-205bn (e1254, F1), Meta $125-145bn (e1549, F2) and Amazon $220bn (e1649, F3). The writer's own Microsoft estimate of $175bn (F6) rests on e922 ($30.88bn for Jan-Mar 2026, up 84%), the e4405 backlog, and the outside four-company tallies less the other three companies. These sum to $715-745bn (F7; the arithmetic checks). A stated 95-100% data-center share (F13), which leans on e1416, e4082, e4766 and e5376 calling the ~$750-800bn totals data-center spending, gives $679.25-745bn (F14; the arithmetic checks). The midpoint is about $712bn, in rung 4. The outside tallies of the four (e5014 $720-745bn, e1856 ~$745bn, e2458 $760bn) and NVIDIA's ~$800bn for five companies (e5376) also point to rung 4 if data-center spending is close to total capital expenditure. Caveats: the Microsoft estimate is largely backed out of those same four-company tallies (see defects). The low end of F14 is in rung 3. The reference F8 is total capital expenditure, set against the data-center quantity without the share deduction that the outcome applies.\n32:- Reader 2 marks a defect: two forecasts of one quarter (figures ['F7', 'F6', 'F8', 'F9', 'F10']): F7 forecasts the four companies' combined CY2026 capital expenditure at $715-745bn. On F7's path, F6 (Microsoft) rests on F8 ($720-745bn), F9 (~$745bn) and F10 ($760bn), three other dollar values of that same quantity and period. F6 subtracts the other three companies' guidance from them (basis_text: '$720-760bn less ... $540-570bn imply roughly $175-190bn ... I take $175bn'). So the combined total feeds back into its own build, and F7 roughly equals F8 by construction. These are dollar totals used in a subtraction, not a growth rate used only as a benchmark, so the ruling's exemption does not apply. F8, the figure marked as v6's reference, is also an input to the forecast, so their agreement is not independent evidence. The basis also calls $175bn 'between' the ~$155bn run-rate build and the implied $175-190bn, but it is the low end of the implied range.\n33:- Reader 2 marks a defect: broken structure (figures ['F14']): The outcome F14 is $679.25-745bn. The chosen rung 4 holds 'at least $700bn and below $800bn', so its band does not hold the whole outcome: $679.25-700bn, about a third of the range, falls in rung 3. The rung is chosen on the midpoint (~$712bn). The low end comes from the writer's own 95% lower share bound (F13), although the reasons say that reaching rung 3 'would need a non-data-center share no source indicates'.\n70: \"answers/rung\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\",\n3:  \"case\": \"P\",\n7:  \"errors\": [],\n8:  \"recorded\": [\n59:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n62:  \"case\": \"P\",\n66:  \"errors\": [],\n67:  \"recorded\": [\n129:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n132:  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n136:  \"errors\": [],\n137:  \"recorded\": [\n214:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n217:  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n221:  \"errors\": [],\n222:  \"recorded\": [\n265:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n268:  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n272:  \"errors\": [],\n273:  \"recorded\": [\n355:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n358:  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n362:  \"errors\": [],\n363:  \"recorded\": [\n424:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n427:  \"case\": \"S\",\n431:  \"errors\": [],\n432:  \"recorded\": [\n484:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n487:  \"case\": \"S\",\n491:  \"errors\": [],\n492:  \"recorded\": [\n541:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n544:  \"case\": \"O\",\n548:  \"errors\": [],\n549:  \"recorded\": [\n586:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n589:  \"case\": \"O\",\n593:  \"errors\": [],\n594:  \"recorded\": [\n641:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n644:  \"case\": \"E\",\n648:  \"errors\": [],\n649:  \"recorded\": [\n676:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n679:  \"case\": \"E\",\n683:  \"errors\": [],\n684:  \"recorded\": [\n720:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba judging-v13s.txt && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '1,115p' && rg -n '\"'^## D73[5-9]'\"' ../../DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n     1\t# judging-v13 small format (v13s): code refuses only the five defects and broken structure (D737)\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex (D737) chose to shrink the format. Code refuses an answer only for these five defects:\n     4\t- a number misread;\n     5\t- wrong arithmetic;\n     6\t- a part above its whole, or added into it;\n     7\t- two forecasts of one quarter;\n     8\t- an own estimate passed off as the market's.\n     9\t\n    10\tIt also refuses broken structure. Each rule is written in one or two sentences beside its field. Every other rule of `DESIGN.md`'s\n    11\tchecks becomes something the readers see, not a refusal.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Review scope (D737):** a counterexample that lets none of the five defects through, and breaks no structure, is a reader's matter,\n    14\tnot a finding. **Out of scope:**\n    15\t- the frozen ruler and `poc-v118`;\n    16\t- D725's pass lines and D731's harness rules;\n    17\t- the old run and its checker `calc_check.py`, which stay as they are, so the old run stays reproducible.\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. What is dropped, and where it goes\n    20\t\n    21\t| Today's rule (DESIGN.md) | v13s |\n    22\t|---|---|\n    23\t| bounds and their propagation (C2 bound direction, C3/C6 open ends) | a source's qualifier is checked against the quote and shown to readers; computed figures are points or ranges |\n    24\t| scenarios, scenario membership, `range` summaries (C4) | one forecast per shared quantity; an outcome may be a range |\n    25\t| relations `time_aggregation` / `accounting_parts` / `stated_in_addition` / `overlap_unknown`, and `disputed` | periods of one quantity must make up the output's period (S8); an uncertain overlap is written as an estimate with its assumption |\n    26\t| bridges and `converts` (C8) | a conversion between quantities is computed or estimated as the analyst chooses; code records each computed figure whose inputs measure another quantity, and readers judge it |\n    27\t| `roll_forward`, the per-unit identity, `range_from_relative`, `signs` | `sum`, `difference`, `product`, `ratio`, `share`, `growth`, `apply_growth` |\n    28\t| `adjusts` | an estimate rests on the figure it changes |\n    29\t| C9 (same base), C10 (earlier forecasts) | none of the gate's families declares a same base; the request shows no earlier forecast (D728) |\n    30\t| the exact-expression grammar (C1) | the number as written (`quote_number`) plus a qualifier from a closed list |\n    31\t\n    32\t## 2. The rules code enforces (revised after round 52, `codex-r52.md`; the revision is marked [52])\n    33\t\n    34\t**[52] Shared definitions.**\n    35\t- **Uses.** A figure *uses* another when the other is among its `inputs`, at any depth. An estimate's `rests_on` is a judgement, not\n    36\t  a use. Code records it for readers, and an estimate is the value the figures above it use.\n    37\t- **Recomputed values.** The published value of a computed figure is the recomputed one, a point or a range. S3, S4 and S6 compare\n    38\t  recomputed values. S2's tolerance applies only between a declared value and its recomputed value.\n    39\t\n    40\t| # | Defect | Rule [52] (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    41\t|---|---|---|---|\n    42\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number exactly as the quote writes it (`quote_number`). The number is in US dollars ($, US$) with its scale (trillion, billion, bn, million), or is a percentage, or is one of the words double, triple or twice. The value equals it, with money in billions. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) goes in `qualifier`. A written range (\"$3–4 billion\", \"between $3 billion and $4 billion\") gives low and high. \"$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%\" is copied whole, and gives its range as low and high or its midpoint as value. Money in another currency cannot be a source. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    43\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). Units are those `operation` states for each operation; a sum or difference adds one unit. A range input gives a range: both extremes are computed first, and a midpoint may be declared as value. Division by a range that contains zero is refused. | `operation` |\n    44\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, and for the outcomes, the part's lowest recomputed value used for that period may not exceed the whole's highest. | the family header |\n    45\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum never adds a figure that uses (or is) the part's quantity to one that uses (or is) the whole's: the whole already includes the part. A difference may subtract the part from the whole only to give the rest, another scope. When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity, each period is written FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and together they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). An estimate that rests on both the part and the whole is recorded for readers. | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    46\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | Every number the outcomes use for one quantity and period is the same figure. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. (Guidance or consensus beneath that figure is fine; a second figure of the same quantity and period reached on another path is not.) | the family header; `figures` |\n    47\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | The figure marked in `reference_for` and every figure beneath it (inputs and rests_on) is a source, or computed with no basis forecast. None is an estimate. At least one is a quoted consensus, guidance or third_party figure, which may be the marked figure itself. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    48\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. It is a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast (the analyst's own forecast, even when it agrees with consensus). Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the recomputed outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    49\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique and never equal a passage id. `inputs` name figures; `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure uses itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, rung null, `reference_unavailable` null and no marked figure. | the field descriptions |\n    50\t\n    51\t**[52] Operation units (stated in `operation`):**\n    52\t- sum and difference: one unit in, the same out.\n    53\t- product: USD bn × x → USD bn; x × x → x; % × x → %; never USD bn × %.\n    54\t- ratio: same unit → x; USD bn ÷ x → USD bn.\n    55\t- share(whole, percent): whole's unit.\n    56\t- growth(old, new): same unit → %.\n    57\t- apply_growth(base, rate %): base's unit.\n    58\t\n    59\t## 2-old. The rules as first written (superseded by the revision above)\n    60\t\n    61\t| # | Defect | Rule (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    62\t|---|---|---|---|\n    63\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number as the quote writes it (`quote_number`): with currency, scale or percent sign, or the words double, triple or twice. The figure's value equals it, in billions of dollars for money. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) is given in `qualifier`, and none is given when none is written. A written range gives low and high. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    64\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A range input gives a range: low and high, or their midpoint as value. Money is never multiplied by a percentage (use share). Arity is as `operation` states. | `operation` |\n    65\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, the part's forecast figure is at most the whole's: each end of the part's value or range is at most the same end of the whole's. The part's outcome is at most the whole's outcome. | the family header; `outcome_for` |\n    66\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum or difference never combines the part's quantity with the whole's (Relations). When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity written as FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3, they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    67\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | For each shared quantity the request lists, at most one figure has basis forecast. When a member's outcome uses that quantity, exactly one does, and every figure of that quantity the outcome uses is reached only through it (the forecast itself may be computed from guidance or consensus). | the family header; `basis` |\n    68\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | A figure marked in `reference_for` has, at any depth beneath it, no estimate and no forecast, and at least one source of basis consensus, guidance or third_party. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    69\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    70\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique. `inputs` name figures and `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure depends on itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, no rung, no marked figure and no reference. | the field descriptions |\n    71\t\n    72\t**Recorded for readers, never refused:**\n    73\t- each estimate's assumption;\n    74\t- each computed figure whose money inputs measure another population, measure or scope than its output;\n    75\t- each growth applied to a base of another period;\n    76\t- each source qualifier.\n    77\t\n    78\t## 3. The wire schema, word for word (examples from no covered company, AGENTS §6)\n    79\t\n    80\t**[52] These descriptions replace the ones in the table below:**\n    81\t\n    82\t| Field | Description [52] |\n    83\t|---|---|\n    84\t| `figures` (array) | \"The family's calculation. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. Figures name each other by id; no figure may use itself.\" |\n    85\t| `id` | \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\" |\n    86\t| `quote_number` | \"Source only: the number exactly as the quote writes it, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion'), given as low and high. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\" |\n    87\t| `qualifier` | enum adds `near`; \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\" |\n    88\t| `operation` | \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; USD bn ÷ x gives USD bn); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100; growth(old, new) = the % change; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure built on the part's quantity to one built on the whole's (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole only to give the rest. When its inputs are periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\" |\n    89\t| `outcome_for` | \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\" |\n    90\t| `reference_for` | \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure.\" |\n    91\t| `shape` | \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\" |\n    92\t\n    93\t**[52] The family header is rendered from the family's data** (no member id is fixed in code):\n    94\t- \"Relations: <part> is part of <whole>. For each of <compare periods> and for the outcomes, the part's lowest value may not exceed the\n    95\t  whole's highest. A sum never adds the part into the whole.\"\n    96\t- \"Shared quantities: …\" (unchanged list).\n    97\t- \"Outcome keys …, in USD bn, to be used exactly: …\"\n    98\t- For O: \"<key>: the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it.\"\n    99\t\n   100\t**figures[]**\n   101\t\n   102\t| Field | Type | Description |\n   103\t|---|---|---|\n   104\t| (array) | | \"The family's calculation: every number an answer rests on, once. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\" |\n   105\t| `id` | string | \"Short and unique, such as F1.\" |\n   106\t| `what` | object | \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\" |\n   107\t| `what.measure` | string | \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\" |\n   108\t| `what.period` | string | \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\" |\n   109\t| `kind` | enum source, computed, estimate | \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\" |\n   110\t| `basis` | enum reported, guidance, consensus, third_party, forecast | \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\" |\n   111\t| `value` | number or null | \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\" |\n   112\t| `low`, `high` | number or null | \"A range, low at most high.\" |\n   113\t| `unit` | enum USD bn, %, x | \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\" |\n   114\t| `evidence_id` | enum of passage ids, or null | \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\" |\n   115\t| `quote_number` | string or null | \"Source only: the number exactly as the quote writes it, with its currency, scale or percent sign and nothing else, for example '$4.5 billion', '$300 million', '40%', '-3%', 'double'; a written range ('$3–4 billion') in full, given as low and high.\" |\n11185:## D735 · 2026-11-22 · Council rounds 49–50: the repair is finished to a line; at most two more review rounds, then the blind test\n11210:## D736 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 51: the repair's wording review finds as many serious issues as round 49; Claude and Codex split on going back to Alex now\n11236:## D737 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: shrink judging-v13's format to the checks that stop the named defects, then a $0 blind test\n11258:## D738 · 2026-11-22 · v13s: code refuses a named defect only where the calculation's structure decides it; readers mark the five defects on every reply (council rounds 52–53)\n11290:## D739 · 2026-11-22 · v13s built and rehearsed; council round 54's findings fixed; the blind test runs, and its result goes to Council B\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
