{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba S/system.txt && nl -ba S/user.txt | sed -n '1,12p;26,32p;82,85p;127,130p' && rg -n '\\\"reference_for\\\"|\\\"rung\\\"|holds the outcome|equals the result|plus or minus' S/schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11236,11333p'; nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '300,345p;375,400p'\"","aggregated_output":" 11236\t## D737 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: shrink judging-v13's format to the checks that stop the named defects, then a $0 blind test\n 11237\t\n 11238\t- **The question** (after D736): \"判数字新版本的修补连续三轮审查都在冒新规则（填写说明将从 1 万字涨到约 2 万字，每个答案约 16 个数、每个数要同时满足几十条规则）。Codex 主张修完再审最后一轮；我主张现在改结构。下一步怎么走？\"\n 11239\t  Options:\n 11240\t  - 砍成小格式再盲测 (recommended): keep only the checks that stop five defects (a number misread, wrong arithmetic, a part\n 11241\t    above its whole or added into it, two forecasts of one quarter, an own estimate passed off as the market's); the other rules\n 11242\t    become assumptions shown to readers; council, then the $0 blind test, then a request for the paid re-send;\n 11243\t  - 按原计划再修一轮 (Codex's position);\n 11244\t  - 关掉这条线.\n 11245\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22), the recommended option (\"…成小格式再盲测 (推荐)\").\n 11246\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11247\t  - The judging-v13 format shrinks. Code refuses an answer only for the five defects above and for broken structure (links that do\n 11248\t    not resolve, a cycle, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome not of the question's quantity, a rung that does not hold the\n 11249\t    outcome).\n 11250\t  - Each such rule is written in one or two sentences beside its field. Every other rule of DESIGN.md's checks becomes an assumption\n 11251\t    recorded and shown to the readers, never a refusal.\n 11252\t  - The council reviews within that scope. A counterexample that lets none of the five defects through is a reader's matter, not a\n 11253\t    finding.\n 11254\t  - Then the $0 blind test (REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5, with round 51's reader fix). A clean result returns to Alex for the paid re-send under\n 11255\t    D725's lines.\n 11256\t- **Superseded:** REPAIR-DESIGN.md v2's full restatement (D735's finish line), replaced by the small format.\n 11257\t\n 11258\t## D738 · 2026-11-22 · v13s: code refuses a named defect only where the calculation's structure decides it; readers mark the five defects on every reply (council rounds 52–53)\n 11259\t\n 11260\t- **Rounds 52 and 53** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r52.md`, `codex-r53.md`) reviewed the small format within D737's\n 11261\t  scope. Each closing rule written for an indirect form of a named defect produced new counterexamples in both directions:\n 11262\t  - \"uses the part\" lineage refuses a correct sum of two Data Center quarters, each computed as a share of company revenue;\n 11263\t  - \"one figure per quantity\" refuses two outside estimates averaged into one starting point;\n 11264\t  - multiplying by 1, relabelling a basis, or routing through an estimate's judgement still passes.\n 11265\t- **Settled (how D737's rule is built; D737 says code refuses only for the five defects, not that code catches every instance):**\n 11266\t  - Code refuses a named defect where the calculation's own structure decides it without reading intent:\n 11267\t    - **S8:** a sum whose inputs include the part's quantity and the whole's quantity themselves.\n 11268\t    - **S4:** two figures with basis forecast for one quantity and period, or a figure of a listed shared quantity used by an outcome\n 11269\t      on a path that bypasses its forecast figure.\n 11270\t    - **S3:** a part above its whole, compared with paired ends when the part is computed from the whole and lowest against highest\n 11271\t      otherwise, on recomputed values. A share takes 0 to 100 percent.\n 11272\t    - **S5:** an estimate or forecast at or beneath the marked reference.\n 11273\t    - **S1, S2, S6, S7** as written.\n 11274\t  - Indirect forms (a multiplier of one, a relabelled basis, an estimate's judgement) are recorded for readers. They are never\n 11275\t    refused.\n 11276\t  - **The readers** in the blind test and the gate mark the five defects on every reply. In the blind test, an accepted reply with\n 11277\t    a reader-marked defect is a stop, so whether real writers use these forms is measured, not argued.\n 11278\t  - **Round 53's false refusals and test conflicts are fixed in the design:**\n 11279\t    - DC Q3 + Q4;\n 11280\t    - averaged outside inputs;\n 11281\t    - cycles through `rests_on`;\n 11282\t    - a figure used twice taking one value in range arithmetic;\n 11283\t    - the \"to\", \"+/-\" and \"±\" spellings;\n 11284\t    - one-source \"±\";\n 11285\t    - the separate arm carrying the part-of relation for S8;\n 11286\t    - readers given the whole reply and every shown passage.\n 11287\t- **Next:** build `calc_small.py`, the v13s schema, header and plan, and the A1–A4 cases and mutants. Council round 54 reviews the code\n 11288\t  and the end-to-end rehearsal. Then comes the blind test.\n 11289\t\n 11290\t## D739 · 2026-11-22 · v13s built and rehearsed; council round 54's findings fixed; the blind test runs, and its result goes to Council B\n 11291\t\n 11292\t- **Round 54** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r54.md`) reviewed the code. It found real defects, each now fixed with a case\n 11293\t  and a mutant:\n 11294\t  - **S1:** \"5%\" was accepted out of \"85%\", and a fragment out of a range or a \"plus or minus\" expression. A quote_number must now be one\n 11295\t    whole expression the quote writes.\n 11296\t  - **S2:** one uncertain number took different values on different paths. A figure's value is now computed over the ranged figures\n 11297\t    it rests on, including zero when a range straddles it.\n 11298\t  - **S3:** linked ranges were compared end to end, and the comparison skipped with an unused alternative. The part must now fit the\n 11299\t    whole for every value of what both rest on, and the figures compared are the ones the outcomes adopt.\n 11300\t  - **S8:**\n 11301\t    - a correct sum of the part and the whole for different periods was refused;\n 11302\t    - a remainder across periods was accepted;\n 11303\t    - a period added twice was accepted.\n 11304\t  - **S4:** an unused or superseded figure was counted. Only the forecasts the outcomes adopt now count.\n 11305\t  - **S5/S7:** `rests_on` is enforced for estimates only and `inputs` for computed figures only, and numbers must be finite.\n 11306\t  - **The runner:** it now refuses a reply that is not exactly one JSON object, an incomplete inventory, and a ladder out of order.\n 11307\t    A checker error is recorded per reply.\n 11308\t- **Checks:**\n 11309\t  - `small_cases.py`: 100 of 100, each an exact rule set;\n 11310\t  - `small_mutants.py`: 38 mutants caught, and the control passes;\n 11311\t  - `small_rehearsal.py`: 19 of 19, including a defective and a correct answer for each named defect through the gate path, and the old\n 11312\t    run reproduced byte for byte;\n 11313\t  - the old harness unchanged: rehearsal 63 of 63, the $0 gate 87 of 87, the old plan's bytes identical.\n 11314\t- **Size:**\n 11315\t  - The v13s schema is about 14,300 characters against about 9,600, and a whole pair request about 140,000 characters against\n 11316\t    134,000. It is larger, not smaller as SMALL-DESIGN.md §4 expected.\n 11317\t  - The reason: the fields the old request left undefined now carry their rules (AGENTS §6).\n 11318\t- **Decided:**\n 11319\t  - The blind test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5) runs now, without a further design round. It costs $0 at the provider.\n 11320\t  - Its stop conditions measure the same classes empirically: readers mark the five defects on every reply, and an accepted reply with\n 11321\t    a marked defect stops the test.\n 11322\t  - Its result goes to a council round (Council B) before anything goes to Alex.\n 11323\t- **Output:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/`.\n 11324\t\n 11325\t## D740 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s blind test stops on a checker error and a reader-marked defect; council round 55 sends three reader rulings to Alex\n 11326\t\n 11327\t- **Result** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/`; $0 at the\n 11328\t  provider):\n 11329\t  - All 12 first answers (six cases, Claude and Codex writers) were schema-valid, and 11 passed every check.\n 11330\t  - **Stop 1:** S1 refused a right answer. \"From $35 billion to $170 billion\" was read as a range, through round 54's \"to\" range form\n 11331\t    (Claude's mistake). The old checker had excluded that form for exactly this ambiguity.\n 11332\t  - **Stop 2:** both readers and both adjudicators marked R06, a guidance-implied Q3 growth benchmark used while forecasting Q3 above\n 11333\t    guidance, as \"two forecasts of one quarter\". Both adjudicators rejected R12's claim, which has the same pattern.\n   300\t  fields the product stores (original day, original speaker, tier, the summary's hedges) and what the next step does with the record\n   301\t  (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"现在到底质量怎么样你深度的解析不要只是看尺子\").\n   302\t- Read the judges' reasons, not only the scores, and pick the next fix from the largest cause. Open the samples behind a metric's largest\n   303\t  values before reading it, and write down what a derived number reduces to.\n   304\t- **Report every number, regressions first.** For future evidence evaluation, combine the existing gold's core and supporting entries as helpful, use its not-kept entries as unhelpful, and leave optional entries neutral (D372). Count one research matter in one article (D250), not raw sentences. Retain the old labels and scores in historical results, and do not silently relabel the frozen gold. One quantity per table column. A comparison of\n   305\t  totals uses three draws; a count from one draw is labelled one draw and shows a mechanism, not a difference between arms. Lead with the\n   306\t  mechanism and the share of its class it explains, then the fix.\n   307\t- **Read what was dropped.** A statement the pipeline drops is thrown away with its reason, never parked on a side list; evaluation\n   308\t  reads the dropped statements by hand, classifies them by the mechanism that would have kept them, and fixes the largest class upstream\n   309\t  (D209: \"if it doesn't matter, just fucking throw it away … if there are false positives that got thrown away … you got to pick it up\").\n   310\t- **Measure end to end before a step's arm, and read the parked designs first.** A step's noise that the next step removes is not the\n   311\t  defect, and a step's recall that the next step throws away is. Before any arm on one step: the production replay through the whole path\n   312\t  on the gold window, the count at the step the product reads, and the decisions on the topic read for a parked fix (Alex, 2026-10-31:\n   313\t  \"make sure we are not going around in circles using wrong ruler and wrong prompts that don't work\"). Before a phase targets one step,\n   314\t  the upside of that step done perfectly is measured where the product is judged: the judge is given the gold's own evidence, built by\n   315\t  the production code, a few sends (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"我很不开心你的失败，为什么，深度多轮检讨记录，想思路突破\").\n   316\t- **Read what was kept against the gold, blind, before the next noise arm.** The not-kept gold entries a version keeps are judged by a\n   317\t  reader who wrote neither the gold nor the prompt, mixed with controls it cannot tell apart; where it parts from the gold, the standard\n   318\t  is unclear or the gold misreads it, and that goes to Alex before any arm counts it as noise (Alex, 2026-10-31: \"你再次重新检查尺子，看看是不是这些留下的还有歧义；然后再思考怎么降低noise\").\n   319\t- Pair every structural check with its semantic one. Run a data rule over its whole input range and on cases it was not tuned on. Weigh\n   320\t  a case by what it carries in the product, not by count.\n   321\t- A claim about a population (\"the data is clean\") rests on a code sweep of the whole population and an independent read of a fresh\n   322\t  random sample, never on the pages the author looked at.\n   323\t- Cost is priced from tokens at the published rates (`hivemind.model.PRICES`, cached input at `model.CACHED_INPUT_SHARE`), never from a\n   324\t  stored `cost_usd`; say which rates a projection uses.\n   325\t\n   326\t### 7.3 Iterating\n   327\t\n   328\t- **One class at a time** (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"). Count the misses by mechanism; take the largest\n   329\t  class; trace it to the sentence that causes it; change one thing aimed at that class; run the whole bench (a prompt change alters every\n   330\t  request) against the previous version; read every regression to its raw answer. The pages that showed the failure locate its\n   331\t  mechanism; only the whole bench (every set, every draw) accepts the change.\n   332\t- **Name the effect on the judgement first.** Every proposed change states the effect it is expected to have on a reader or on the final\n   333\t  judgement, and is tested there where possible; a step's own counts do not decide it alone. A fix that keeps moving its error to\n   334\t  neighbouring cases stops being tuned on that set (Alex, 2026-11-09: \"感觉现在一直在修一些mknor的原地打转，你是不是要思考下现在具体还差什么high priority big impact 的需要做？\";\n   335\t  he approved this rule the same day, D391).\n   336\t- **A model call is a sampler.** Re-send a stored request before calling its answer a defect. Totals and acceptance come from at least\n   337\t  three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A direct-read pass\n   338\t  (one send per article, every answer read beside its source; D238) locates mechanisms; its counts are one draw and are labelled so.\n   339\t- **A written log.** One row per arm: hypothesis, change, numbers against the control (regressions first), kept or not. Refuted arms and\n   340\t  your own mistakes stay in it; the next session starts from its last row.\n   341\t- **Own the loop, within what Alex agreed.** When the direction is agreed and no stop is in force (`CURRENT_STATE.md`), push your own\n   342\t  hypotheses through $0 checks and probes of a few cents on stored cases without asking. A registered test, an end-to-end run, or\n   343\t  anything that departs from the agreed plan starts on his word; a result that contradicts the hypothesis stops the line and goes to him\n   344\t  (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\"). While he is not active (D439), \"his word\" is the council's\n   345\t  recorded consensus, and a stopped line goes to the council for re-assessment.\n   375\t  A failed step of a plan is fixed and re-run at that same step: its failures are the work list, and nothing later in the plan and no\n   376\t  substitute plan starts before it passes (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"你为什么要drift? 你现在明明还有这么多的问题要fix,你到底知不知道你要fix什么？\").\n   377\t  Before a fix is proposed, write which goal the mechanism still serves and what the product already gives for it; a measurement that\n   378\t  removes the reason a mechanism exists retires the mechanism instead of starting its repair (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"I told you to think about\n   379\t  it and you're giving me suggestions that you reject yourself\").\n   380\t- **Before a version is rejected, the rejection is checked.** Every failed or refused answer behind a \"did not pass\" is read against its\n   381\t  source and put down as the model's error or the measuring rule's or check's error; a rule or check that refuses right answers is fixed,\n   382\t  and a version that already meets the goal (what it was built to fix is fixed, nothing that was right turned wrong) is not dropped for a\n   383\t  stricter rule a later review added. Alex, 2026-11-05: \"你到底是不是再搬石头砸自己的脚？各种reject自己，你确定是正确的rejection 吗？你又下去看trace吗？\";\n   384\t  \"你不能每次都这样判错然后浪费我时间，我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信下一步你要跑什么才能收敛\".\n   385\t- **Correct, then cheap.** Make the pipeline right and stable before any cost, speed or throughput work; report cost as a baseline only\n   386\t  (Alex, 2026-10-25).\n   387\t\n   388\t## 8. Product thinking\n   389\t\n   390\t- Think like a hedge-fund analyst. A thesis is an argued view about the business and the world; its stance comes from the argument\n   391\t  weighed against what is priced. Extract the few metrics or segments that carry it; financial-statement lines are its evidence, never its\n   392\t  definition.\n   393\t- **Lean cautious, catch the increment.** Most of what an item says is noise: not everything is evidence and not everything is a lead. A\n   394\t  typical claim or background line is common knowledge (what an analyst following the company knew before the item) and is not kept\n   395\t  without a number and a specific narrative; new information that changes an answer is never missed. Alex, 2026-10-29 (D243):\n   396\t  \"不是什么事情都是惊弓之鸟需要抽取，杂音还是很大的，我们整体应该倾向谨慎不要看什么都是线索的感觉，但是同时我们要敏感一些新的信息带来的增量不要错过\".\n   397\t- **A senior analyst's granularity.** A question is a bet an analyst would hold a card for: a big matter, never trivia (a deal, product or\n   398\t  project opens one only when its scale can change revenue, profit, cash or credit risk). Small matters open no question, and whether\n   399\t  their statements are kept follows the evidence rule; wordings, deadlines, milestones and periods of one bet are one question, and a\n   400\t  statement carried by several outlets is one statement (how code applies it: §3, Identity). Two questions stay apart only when their answers can move apart and an analyst\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba S/system.txt && nl -ba S/user.txt | sed -n '1,12p;26,32p;82,85p;127,130p' && rg -n '\\\"reference_for\\\"|\\\"rung\\\"|holds the outcome|equals the result|plus or minus' S/schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v6 = four largest AI hyperscalers | data-center capex | combined | CY2026.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 50 items]\n    10\t- e122 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com; original source Tom’s Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware’s running tally said more than 75 U.S. data-center projects worth around $130 billion had been blocked or delayed so far in 2026.\n    11\t    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .\n    12\t- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4567; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n    28\t    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n    29\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n    30\t    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n    31\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1738, e2079; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    32\t- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n    82\t    Quote: The Bank for International Settlements went further on September 10. In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n    83\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n    84\t    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n    85\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   127\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   128\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   129\t    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n   130\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n169:        \"description\": \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\"\n234:        \"description\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\"\n276:     \"reference_for\": {\n304:     \"reference_for\"\n329:     \"rung\": {\n333:        \"description\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\"\n530:     \"rung\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && for f in reader-packets/R{10,11,12}.*.json; do echo \"$f\"; cat \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && for f in reader-packets/R{10,11,12}.*.json; do echo \"$f\"; cat \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"reader-packets/R10.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 Data Center revenue from reported quarters e109 ($75.25bn) and e2650 ($89.0bn), summed to $164.25bn (F3, consistent with e5309's $164bn). Q3 company revenue = the $108.0bn guidance midpoint (e2652) times a stated 5% beat assumption (F11, resting on the computed Q2 beat of 5.71% over the $91.0bn midpoint from e3200/e2649, the Q4 FY2026 beat of 4.62% from e2452, and e5054/e5055's 3.1% above the high end, which is about 5% above the midpoint), giving $113.4bn. Q4 = $113.4bn times a stated 12% sequential pace benchmarked on the guided 12% (e5005), giving $127.01bn. H2 company revenue is $240.41bn; a stated H2 Data Center share of 92.8% (F16, from Q2's 92.5% = 89.0/96.2 and the rising share series in e3399) gives $223.1bn; the FY2027 total is $387.35bn, inside rung 2 ($350bn to $400bn). The arithmetic checks, and the rung holds with margin: falling below $350bn would need H2 Data Center revenue under $185.75bn, which is below the guided Q3 pace, and reaching $400bn would need about $235.75bn.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R10.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"Reported Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion in Q1 (e109) and $89.0 billion in Q2 (e2650) gives $164.25 billion for H1. The stated 5% beat on the $108.0 billion Q3 company guidance midpoint (e2652, e3200, e2452, e5054, e5055) gives $113.4 billion. Applying the assumed 12% sequential growth rate, benchmarked to e5005, gives $127.01 billion in Q4. Applying the assumed 92.8% Data Center share (e3399, e5223) to those two quarters gives $223.10 billion in H2 and $387.35 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reader-packets/R11.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 reported: F1 $81.62bn (e111) + F2 $96.2bn (e2649) = F3 $177.82bn. Q3: F9 $112.5bn, an estimate stated in its basis text from guidance F7 (e2652, $105.84-110.16bn) plus part of the beat pattern (e5054 twelve beats of the high end; e5055 3.1% average, i.e. about $113.6bn; e3200 $5.2bn over the Q2 midpoint; e2452 $68bn vs $65bn), trimmed for supply caps (e3115, e4911). Q4: F12 = F9 x 1.12 = $126.0bn, with F11's 12% the assumed pace guided for Q3 (F10, e4899), backed by hyperscale reacceleration (e3406, e4283), commitments of $279bn (e2759) and supply limits (e5067). FY2027 = 177.82 + 238.5 = $416.32bn (F14), inside rung 2 ($392-431.2bn). Every computed figure checks. The band holds even under the answer's own downside: guidance midpoint and 5% Q4 growth gives about $399bn (still rung 2). Reference F19 $411bn (e4333) is quoted consensus, also in rung 2. Reading the '70% full-year growth' reports as FY2028 is argued from e2691 and the arithmetic ($367bn would need Q4 near $81bn). Two small prose slips do not move the band: F11's 'actual sequential growth has run 17-20%' is really about 17-18% (96.2/81.62 = 17.9%; 112.5/96.2 = 16.9%), and the rung-1 example 'Q4 near $138bn (+20% sequential)' falls short of the H2 of about $253.4bn that rung 1 needs from a $112.5bn Q3 (it would need about $140.9bn, +25%).\", \"why_not\": \"\"}, \"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 reported: F4 $75.25bn (e109) + F5 $89.0bn (e2650) = F6 $164.25bn (in line with e5309's $164bn). H2: F17 = F13 company H2 $238.5bn x F16 93.0%, the stated share assumption, = $221.81bn. F16 rests on the share rising every quarter to 92.5% (F15, e3399; 89.0/96.2 = 92.5%), on growth led by the data center business (e5223, e4283, e2721), and on the remainder (Edge Computing, $7.2bn = 96.2 - 89.0) growing a slower 13% (e5059). FY2027 = 164.25 + 221.81 = $386.06bn (F18), inside rung 2 ($350-400bn). This is below the whole's $416.32bn, and the H2 part is a share of the H2 total, so the part cannot exceed the whole in Q3 or Q4. At Q2's 92.5% share the result would be about $384.9bn, still rung 2. The answer's downside (guidance midpoint, 5% Q4 growth, 92.5% share) gives about $369bn, also rung 2. Reference F20 $361bn is Morningstar's own estimate (e2295), not the writer's, and sits in rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R11.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v4\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"e111 and e2649 give $177.82 billion in first-half revenue. The answer assumes $112.5 billion for Q3, a smaller beat than the recent pattern in e5054 and e5055 would imply from e2652's guidance, given the supply constraints in e3115 and e4911. It uses the 12% pace in e4899 as a benchmark for Q4, supported by the expected pickup in hyperscale growth in e4283. That gives $126 billion for Q4 and $416.32 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    },\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"e109 and e2650 give $164.25 billion in first-half Data Center revenue. The answer assumes a 93% second-half share based on the rising share in e3399 and the expected Data Center-led growth in e5223 and e4283. Applying 93% to the same $238.5 billion second-half company forecast gives $221.81 billion for Data Center and $386.06 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reader-packets/R12.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F8 = F1 x (1 + F7) = 20 x 1.85 = 37, inside rung 3 (at least $30bn, below $40bn). F1 is the $20bn FY2027 CPU revenue guidance (e3660), tied to Vera CPU by e4856 and e4691. F7 is the stated 85% growth assumption: management's 'more than double' (F2, e4856) is the benchmark, discounted for time and execution toward the ~50% a year CPU-market pace (F4 from e4687; F5 to F6 from e4857, about 48% a year) and AMD's competing benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404), weighed against broad announced adoption (e4689, e5044, e4692). The reference F3 = 20 x 2 = 40 comes from guidance alone (F1, F2) and sits at the bottom of rung 2; F7 uses the doubling only as a benchmark, so the outcome path carries no second dollar value for FY2028.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreader-packets/R12.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"The FY2027 base is $20 billion (e3660, e4856). The writer assumes 85% FY2028 growth, discounting NVIDIA’s more-than-double outlook for execution risk and competition while allowing for broad Vera adoption (e4856, e4687, e4857, e4689, e5044, e5399–e5404). Applying 85% growth to $20 billion gives $37 billion, within rung 3.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && head -22 small-rehearsal.log && tail -7 small-cases.log && tail -5 small-mutants.log && rg -n 'overlap|rung.*edge' small_cases.py small_mutants.py && rg --files | rg 'tall|BLIND.*RESULT|calibrat'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && head -22 small-rehearsal.log && tail -7 small-cases.log && tail -5 small-mutants.log && rg -n 'overlap|rung.*edge' small_cases.py small_mutants.py && rg --files | rg 'tall|BLIND.*RESULT|calibrat'\"","aggregated_output":"PASS  every request is the planned v13s body, byte for byte\nPASS  36 first attempts sent, none refused\nPASS  every first attempt publishes\nPASS  every separate pair reconciles and publishes\nPASS  the reader card shows a recomputed outcome\nPASS  the reader card maps the recorded estimate to the card's figure numbers\n{\n \"damaged\": [],\n \"finals\": {\n  \"pair-old-1|joint|1\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-1|joint|2\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-1|joint|3\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-1|separate-v4|1\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-1|separate-v4|2\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-1|separate-v4|3\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-1|separate-v8|1\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-1|separate-v8|2\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-1|separate-v8|3\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-2|joint|1\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-2|joint|2\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-2|joint|3\": \"publish\",\n  \"pair-old-2|separate-v14|1\": \"publish\",\nPASS  A3  money × % through product  [expected S2; got S2]\nPASS  A3  \"FY2027 first half\" summed from quarters  [expected S8; got S8]\nPASS  A3  a passage id in inputs  [expected S7; got S7]\nPASS  A3  prose in inputs  [expected S7; got S7]\nPASS  A3  an outcome in \"USD trillion\"  [expected schema; got schema]\nPASS  A3  \"65% growth in Q4\" copied as the number  [expected S1; got S1]\n109 of 109 cases as registered\nPASS  S8 period spelling: caught: FAIL  A3  \"FY2027 first half\" summed from quarters  [expected S8; got pass]  []\nPASS  D740 from-to is a change: caught: FAIL  A1  [D740] \"from $35 billion to $170 billion\": each amount copied alone  [expected pass; got S1]  [[\"S1\", \"S1: \\\"$35 billion\\\" is part of the longer expre\nPASS  D741 carried share: caught: FAIL  A2  [D741] consensus total × a past quarter's reported share marked as the market reference (S5)  [expected S5; got pass]  []\nPASS  recorded conversion: caught: FAIL  A1  a conversion between quantities (recorded, not refused)  [expected pass + recorded conversion between quantities; got pass]  [\"missing recorded conver\n42 of 42 as expected (control + 41 mutants)\nsmall_mutants.py:34:    ('S6 rung holds outcome', \"        if interval is not None and not _overlaps_band(\", \"        if False and not _overlaps_band(\"),\nsmall_mutants.py:45:    ('S8 part into whole', \"                if any(_overlap(p['what']['period'], w['what']['period']) for p in parts for w in wholes):\", \"                if False:\"),\nsmall_mutants.py:46:    ('S8 overlap only', \"                if any(_overlap(p['what']['period'], w['what']['period']) for p in parts for w in wholes):\", \"                if True:\"),\nsmall_cases.py:215:@case('A1', 'a range outcome overlapping its rung', 'pass')\nBLIND3-RESULT.md\nBLIND-RESULT.md\nBLIND2-RESULT.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONPATH=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers python3 -B -c '\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport collections as c\nimport hashlib,json,sys\nfrom blind_test import first_json, READER_BRIEF, cs, model, CASES\nfrom blind_adjudicate import HEADER, descriptions, reading_lines\np=Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\nkey=json.loads((p/'\\\\''reader-key.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nrows=json.loads((p/'\\\\''CHECKED.json'\\\\'').read_text())\ntally=json.loads((p/'\\\\''TALLY.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nchecked={(r['\\\\''case'\\\\''],r['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']):r for r in rows}\nsupports=c.Counter(); authored=c.Counter(); rejected=0; n_disagree=0; n_adj=0; defect_unique=set(); forms=c.Counter()\nassert len(key)==len(rows)==12 and len(checked)==12 and set(checked)=={(case,w) for case in CASES for w in ('\\\\''codex'\\\\'','\\\\''claude'\\\\'')}\nfor rid,k in key.items():\n case,w=k['\\\\''case'\\\\''],k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']\n folder=p/case\n raw=(folder/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/f'\\\\''{w}.json'\\\\'').read_text()\n user=(folder/'\\\\''user.txt'\\\\'').read_text()\n sys_txt=(folder/'\\\\''system.txt'\\\\'').read_text()\n schema=json.loads((folder/'\\\\''schema.json'\\\\'').read_text())\n item=json.loads((folder/'\\\\''item.json'\\\\'').read_text())\n assert item['\\\\''user'\\\\'']==user and item['\\\\''system'\\\\'']==sys_txt and item['\\\\''schema'\\\\'']==schema\n assert hashlib.sha256(raw.encode()).hexdigest()==checked[case,w]['\\\\''sha256'\\\\'']\n assert (p/'\\\\''reader-packets'\\\\''/f'\\\\''{rid}.md'\\\\'').read_text()==READER_BRIEF+'\\\\''\\\\n# The request as the writer saw it\\\\n\\\\n'\\\\''+user+'\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n# The answer\\\\n\\\\n'\\\\''+raw+'\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\''\n ans=first_json(raw)\n assert not model.check_schema(ans,schema)\n calc=cs.from_answer(ans,item['\\\\''family'\\\\''])\n result=cs.check(item['\\\\''family'\\\\''],item['\\\\''passages'\\\\''],calc)\n assert not result['\\\\''errors'\\\\''] and cs.publish_decision(item['\\\\''family'\\\\''],calc,result)=='\\\\''publish'\\\\''\n for a in calc['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']:\n  # outcome ids are attached in from_answer\n  print(rid,a['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],a.get('\\\\''outcome'\\\\''),a.get('\\\\''rung'\\\\''),result['\\\\''published'\\\\''][a['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']] if isinstance(result['\\\\''published'\\\\''],dict) else result['\\\\''published'\\\\''])\n first=[json.loads((p/'\\\\''reader-packets'\\\\''/f'\\\\''{rid}.{r}.json'\\\\'').read_text()) for r in ('\\\\''codex'\\\\'','\\\\''claude'\\\\'')]\n defects=[{(m['\\\\''type'\\\\''],tuple(sorted(m['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']))) for m in a['\\\\''defects'\\\\'']} for a in first]\n mark_rows={(f'\\\\''Reader {i}'\\\\'',x['\\\\''type'\\\\''],tuple(sorted(x['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']))) for i,t in enumerate(first,1) for x in t['\\\\''defects'\\\\'']}\n for i,d in enumerate(defects):\n  authored[i]+=len(d)\n shared=defects[0]&defects[1]\n defect_unique.update((rid,d) for d in (defects[0]|defects[1]))\n members=[a['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''] for a in ans['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']]\n need=any(first[0]['\\\\''members'\\\\''][m]['\\\\''supported'\\\\'']\"'!=first[1]['\"'\\\\''members'\\\\''][m]['\\\\''supported'\\\\''] for m in members) or defects[0]\"'!=defects[1]\n if need:\n  n_disagree+=1\n  adjud=[json.loads((p/'\"'\\\\''adjudication'\\\\''/f'\\\\''{rid}.{r}.json'\\\\'').read_text()) for r in ('\\\\''codex'\\\\'','\\\\''claude'\\\\'')]\n  n_adj+=len(adjud)\n  for j in adjud:\n   assert {(x['\\\\''reader'\\\\''],x['\\\\''type'\\\\''],tuple(sorted(x['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']))) for x in j['\\\\''defects'\\\\'']}==mark_rows\n   assert all(x['\\\\''decision'\\\\'']=='\\\\''reject'\\\\'' for x in j['\\\\''defects'\\\\''])\n  rejected+=len(mark_rows)\n  assert (p/'\\\\''adjudication'\\\\''/f'\\\\''{rid}.md'\\\\'').read_text()=='\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\''.join([HEADER,'\\\\''# The two readings'\\\\'',*reading_lines('\\\\''Reader 1'\\\\'',first[0]),*reading_lines('\\\\''Reader 2'\\\\'',first[1]),'\\\\'''\\\\'', '\\\\''# The system text the writer was given'\\\\'',sys_txt,'\\\\'''\\\\'','\\\\''# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)'\\\\'',json.dumps(descriptions(schema),indent=1,ensure_ascii=False),'\\\\'''\\\\'','\\\\''# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)'\\\\'',user,'\\\\'''\\\\'','\\\\''# The answer'\\\\'',raw])\n else:\n  assert not (p/'\\\\''adjudication'\\\\''/f'\\\\''{rid}.md'\\\\'').exists()\n  adjud=first\n supporter=[tuple(j['\\\\''members'\\\\''][m]['\\\\''supported'\\\\''] for j in adjud) for m in members]\n for a in supporter: supports[a if a[0]==a[1] else '\\\\''split'\\\\'']+=1\n for f in ans['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n  if f['\\\\''kind'\\\\'']=='\\\\''source'\\\\'' and '\\\\''plus or minus'\\\\'' in f['\\\\''quote_number'\\\\'']: forms['\\\\''midpoint'\\\\'' if f['\\\\''value'\\\\''] is not None else '\\\\''range'\\\\'']+=1\n print('\\\\''  marks'\\\\'',len(mark_rows),'\\\\''shared'\\\\'',shared,'\\\\''supports'\\\\'',supporter)\nprint('\\\\''counts'\\\\'',n_disagree,n_adj,authored,rejected,len(defect_unique),supports,forms)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
