{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba S/user.txt | sed -n '10,150p'\"","aggregated_output":"    10\t- e122 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com; original source Tom’s Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware’s running tally said more than 75 U.S. data-center projects worth around $130 billion had been blocked or delayed so far in 2026.\n    11\t    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .\n    12\t- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n    13\t    Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n    14\t- e1254 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Alphabet 2026-07-22; from gnews:ac966c48ae5afea2a79acaf3 2026-07-27] Alphabet raised its 2026 capital expenditure guidance to $195 billion–$205 billion, from $180 billion–$190 billion previously.\n    15\t    Quote: Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\n    16\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1174, e1446, e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    17\t- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n    18\t    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\n    19\t- e1545 [party_statement; Larry Fink, as quoted by Preston Fore, Fortune; original source Larry Fink ; from gnews:79bbaae99cc8279528469e6e 2026-08-01] BlackRock CEO Larry Fink said the U.S. would run short of electricians needed to build AI data centers.\n    20\t    Quote: “I’ve even told members of the Trump team that we’re going to run out of electricians that we need to build out AI data centers,” Fink said to an audience at CERAWeek, an S&P Global energy conference hosted in Houston. “We just don’t have enough.”\n    21\t- e1549 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, as reported by Joel South; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n    22\t    Quote: Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion\n    23\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e414; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    24\t- e1649 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:275ccde6edc6d12657c32c3e 2026-08-06] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projection from $200 billion to $220 billion; the article says the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    25\t    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4567; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n    28\t    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n    29\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n    30\t    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n    31\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1738, e2079; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    32\t- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n    33\t    Quote: Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\n    34\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1947, e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    35\t- e2081 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Moody's ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Moody’s warned that hyperscaler-scale spending is reducing technology companies’ free cash flow and pushing them toward more borrowing.\n    36\t    Quote: Moody's has warned that spending on this scale is eating into free cash flow and pushing tech groups into heavier borrowing.\n    37\t- e2458 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The top four hyperscalers ; from gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23] The top four hyperscalers plan to spend $760 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    38\t    Quote: The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\n    39\t- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    40\t    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    41\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e592, e3353, e3556, e3557, e3621, e3647, e3651, e3729, e4224, e5377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    42\t- e3142 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from nvblog:vera-cpu-delivery 2026-08-27] AWS and NVIDIA announced plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and to bring Vera CPU-based infrastructure to AWS.\n    43\t    Quote: AWS and NVIDIA announced yesterday a major expansion of their 16-year collaboration, including plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and work to bring Vera CPU -based infrastructure to AWS.\n    44\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    45\t- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    46\t    Quote: Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.\n    47\t- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    48\t    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\n    49\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e175; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    50\t- e4404 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Amazon said AWS growth had reignited and the cloud platform had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    51\t    Quote: Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited, and it just had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    52\t- e4405 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Microsoft reported a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform.\n    53\t    Quote: Microsoft is sitting on a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform\n    54\t- e4406 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Google Cloud reported second-quarter revenue growth of 82% year over year.\n    55\t    Quote: Google Cloud delivered 82% year-over-year revenue growth in the second quarter.\n    56\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    57\t- e4417 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms ; from gnews:7578c01e5da9c31eff3455e3 2026-09-07] Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    58\t    Quote: Amazon, Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ), Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    59\t- e4514 [third_party_view; Jon Quast; original source Jon Quast 2026-08-27; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] Jon Quast estimated that the top five hyperscalers would spend roughly $800 billion on capex this year.\n    60\t    Quote: the top five hyperscalars spending roughly 800 billion in capex this year.\n    61\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    62\t- e4564 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-09; from gnews:33edf25ec336133783ceead0 2026-09-09] The article expects top hyperscalers to spend $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 billion in calendar 2027.\n    63\t    Quote: The top hyperscalers are expected to spend $800 billion this year and $1.3 billion next year.\n    64\t- e4566 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Elon Musk said a global memory shortage was the limiting factor for the AI build-out.\n    65\t    Quote: Elon Musk said that the global shortage of memory is \"the limiting factor\" to the AI build-out\n    66\t- e4567 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projections for 2026, citing rising memory costs.\n    67\t    Quote: Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) specifically cited rising memory costs when raising its capital expenditure projections for this year.\n    68\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1649; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    69\t- e4575 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Many technology companies ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] The author says many technology companies estimate NVIDIA GPUs’ useful life at six years.\n    70\t    Quote: Many technology companies estimate the useful life of Nvidia's graphics processing units (GPUs) at six years.\n    71\t- e4576 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] Michael Burry says AI chips should be expensed over two to three years, rather than over a longer useful life.\n    72\t    Quote: Burry believes the lifespan of these chips should be shorter, saying they should be expensed over two to three years.\n    73\t- e4680 [third_party_view; fool.com author Ryan Vanzo; original source Rich Privorotsky, Goldman Sachs analyst ; from gnews:b5313a0ca53ce1af48ab5c6c 2026-09-10] Goldman Sachs analyst Rich Privorotsky said a breakthrough in AI intelligence could shift demand and spur other AI labs to keep spending on development.\n    74\t    Quote: \"Price decline is one thing, but a real breakthrough in intelligence will once again change the demand curve,\" Privorotsky explains. \"It will force all other labs to catch up, keep the spending cycle energized, and reactivate the belief that 'there is something better worth building.'\"\n    75\t- e4766 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-11; from gnews:3f69a17dc0ba27583e1dbd92 2026-09-11] The author estimates that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    76\t    Quote: The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.\n    77\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    78\t- e4790 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c798576399fa62f56cf3145 2026-09-11] Alphabet increased its expected 2026 capital expenditures to $195 billion–$205 billion.\n    79\t    Quote: Alphabet has increased expected 2026 capital expenditures to between $195 billion and $205 billion.\n    80\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    81\t- e4807 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com, quoting Pablo Hernández de Cos in a BIS speech; original source Pablo Hernández de Cos 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said on September 10 that AI investment was increasingly financed by debt, particularly private credit, and warned disappointing AI returns could trigger an investment pullback.\n    82\t    Quote: The Bank for International Settlements went further on September 10. In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n    83\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n    84\t    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n    85\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    86\t- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend roughly $795 billion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    87\t    Quote: Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026\n    88\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4848; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    89\t- e5074 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Jacob Coxon ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    90\t    Quote: former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    91\t- e5075 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Evan Hubinger ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    92\t    Quote: Anthropic's alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger publicly said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    93\t- e5076 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei called for the industry to slow AI-model improvement until safety measures can catch up.\n    94\t    Quote: Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei responded by calling for the industry to \"pace the frontier\"—slowing the improvement of AI models until safety measures can catch up.\n    95\t- e5077 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Dario Amodei proposed independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    96\t    Quote: His proposal includes independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    97\t- e5078 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source Anthropic ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic committed to using the independent-evaluator approach.\n    98\t    Quote: Anthropic has committed to the evaluator approach.\n    99\t- e5079 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source Sam Altman ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said OpenAI would use independent evaluators and agreed that AI-model development should be paced.\n   100\t    Quote: OpenAI CEO Sam Altman backed the idea, saying OpenAI would also use independent evaluators and agreeing that the frontier needs to be paced.\n   101\t- e5081 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n   102\t    Quote: The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   103\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   104\t- e5105 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8cdef40aebf24cdc89f530ff 2026-09-16] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers’ annual capital spending is on track to reach $800 billion in 2026 and is expected to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   105\t    Quote: Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   106\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   107\t- e5114 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management pegged top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures at nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026.\n   108\t    Quote: top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026\n   109\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   110\t- e5150 [fact; Zacks Equity Research; original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] The expanded AWS partnership calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027–2028.\n   111\t    Quote: Its expanded AWS partnership, which calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027-28, further underscores the scale and longevity of AI infrastructure investment.\n   112\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4616; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   113\t- e5170 [third_party_view; marketbeat.com; original source marketbeat.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:b98d0a9596b1d1e0a9609645 2026-09-16] MarketBeat estimates that leading hyperscalers' capital-expenditure plans total more than $700 billion; the period is not specified.\n   114\t    Quote: CapEx plans among the leading hyperscalers total over $700 billion, underscoring the spend.\n   115\t- e5200 [third_party_view; Keith Noonan; original source Keith Noonan 2026-09-17; from gnews:d5a540df626501269df8ff8c 2026-09-17] Keith Noonan expects SpaceX, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and other AI-focused companies not to reduce capital expenditure plans for the coming year or investments in compute infrastructure further out.\n   116\t    Quote: Of those three companies, only SpaceX is public right now -- but keep an eye on quarterly reports from it and other public, AI-focused businesses such as Alphabet , Amazon , and Meta Platforms , and see if any of them announce a reduction in capital expenditure plans for the coming year or suggest that they expect to reduce their investments in compute infrastructure further out. It's probably not\n   117\t- e5291 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote flexible AI-data-center electricity use in exchange for faster grid connections.\n   118\t    Quote: NVIDIA, Google $GOOGL , and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance, a coalition aimed at getting AI data centers to dynamically manage their electricity consumption in response to grid conditions, with the goal of securing faster connections to the power grid in return.\n   119\t- e5292 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram, as quoted by Engadget ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram said grid connections for new data centers can take ten years or longer, while average grid utilization is about 50%, leaving potential headroom for flexible data-center loads.\n   120\t    Quote: Getting a new data center onto the grid can take ten years or longer, Sivaram noted, because utilities are obligated to guarantee sufficient capacity for peak demand periods. Yet average grid utilization hovers around 50%, which means data centers willing to flex their consumption could tap into substantial headroom on the existing network without forcing expensive new buildout.\n   121\t- e5293 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Data Center Watch, as cited by Fortune ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune said community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction in 2025.\n   122\t    Quote: Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\n   123\t- e5357 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said the roughly $1.5 trillion in annual AI infrastructure capital spending he described requires customers to pay for the capacity.\n   124\t    Quote: \"If you're going to build a trillion and a half dollars a year in capex, somebody has to pay for it,\" he said.\n   125\t- e5358 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA said the five largest U.S. hyperscalers were on track to spend $800 billion on capital expenditure in 2026.\n   126\t    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) noted on its August earnings call that the top five U.S. hyperscalers alone are on track to clock $800 billion in capital expenditure this year.\n   127\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   128\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   129\t    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n   130\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   131\t\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba O/user.txt | sed -n '1,110p' && nl -ba E/user.txt | sed -n '1,85p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v29 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Commitments are below $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Commitments are at least $100 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v29 = NVIDIA | equity investment commitments | company | end of FY2027 (the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it).\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 30 items]\n    10\t- e1732 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307 2026-08-12] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA had committed to provide $70 billion in equity investments to ecosystem partners; the period covered was not specified.\n    11\t    Quote: Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.\n    12\t- e1919 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source The Information 2026-08-07; from gnews:e2a48863c597e02d402f1551 2026-08-16] The Information reported on August 7, 2026, that NVIDIA planned to invest up to $3 billion in Lancium: an initial $2 billion for about 20% equity, plus up to $1 billion tied mainly to securing additional grid interconnections.\n    13\t    Quote: According to a report from The Information, published on August 7, NVIDIA will spend up to $3 billion in Lancium, a power infrastructure developer and the firm behind the Stargate data center project in Abilene, Texas. The deal is structured in two parts: an initial $2 billion for a roughly 20% equity share in Lancium, followed by an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting particular go\n    14\t- e1957 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-17; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000069:sbeoainvidia-portsrelease.htm 2026-08-17] NVIDIA said it will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n    15\t    Quote: NVIDIA will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy, joining existing investors SoftBank Group and OpenAI.\n    16\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2404; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    17\t- e1970 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey) 2026-08-17; from gnews:127ca150cda794237261a87c 2026-08-17] SB Energy is reportedly targeting an IPO as early as September 2026 to raise at least $5 billion, with NVIDIA's second $1.5 billion investment tranche expected to be made at the IPO.\n    18\t    Quote: SB Energy is reportedly targeting an IPO as early as next month, seeking to raise at least $5 billion — and Nvidia’s second $1.5 billion tranche is expected to land there, putting Nvidia, OpenAI, and SoftBank all on the cap table of a newly public AI power company.\n    19\t- e1989 [fact; CNBC; original source SB Energy, SoftBank, and NVIDIA ; from gnews:a95b9986d8697704ffa17843 2026-08-17] For the Ohio project, SB Energy and SoftBank plan power sources supporting 10 GW and at least $4.2 billion of regional grid investment; NVIDIA will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n    20\t    Quote: As part of the Ohio deal, SB Energy and SoftBank will build power sources supporting 10 gigawatts of energy and invest at least $4.2 billion into regional grid infrastructure. Nvidia will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1984, e1999, e2022, e2045; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e2080 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:a8855077e115780d8a3e8e06 2026-08-18] Bank of America estimated that NVIDIA had committed approximately $300 billion to ecosystem partners, comprising about $70 billion in equity investments and roughly $230 billion in residual-value guarantees or backstop commitments.\n    23\t    Quote: Bank of America estimates that NVIDIA has committed approximately $300 billion in capital to its ecosystem partners, comprising about $70 billion in equity investments and roughly $230 billion in residual value guarantees or backstop commitments.\n    24\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2095, e2170; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    25\t- e2207 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA and Poolside, as reported by Newcomer based on a Poolside investor letter 2026-08-20; from gnews:1e66644cc2e64e6295fd657a 2026-08-21] NVIDIA’s Poolside transaction includes a $6 billion non-exclusive Model Factory license, a $1 billion investment at a $12 billion pre-money valuation, and the transfer of 109 employees to NVIDIA.\n    26\t    Quote: The transaction, first reported by Newcomer on August 20 based on a Poolside investor letter and subsequently confirmed by Bloomberg and The Information, involves a $6 billion payment for a non-exclusive license to Poolside's Model Factory — the system the company uses to build its open-weight Laguna coding model family. In structural terms, a non-exclusive license grants Nvidia the right to utili\n    27\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2220, e3340; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    28\t- e2280 [rumour; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source The Information, citing people with knowledge of the discussions ; from gnews:80f2b541ac8595e762951bef 2026-08-23] Unnamed sources told The Information that NVIDIA was in talks to invest in Perplexity in a funding round valuing the startup at more than $30 billion.\n    29\t    Quote: Nvidia Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA, XETRA:NVD) , the US chipmaker, is in talks to invest in Perplexity as part of a funding round that would value the artificial intelligence startup at more than $30 billion, The Information reported on Sunday.\n    30\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2304, e2329; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    31\t- e2305 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source The Information, citing people familiar with the negotiations ; from gnews:117bd73fed732101b47f0049 2026-08-24] Perplexity is reportedly discussing a funding round potentially worth several billion dollars, with NVIDIA among the prospective investors; NVIDIA's potential investment amount was not reported.\n    32\t    Quote: The discussions centre on a funding round potentially worth several billion dollars, with Nvidia among the prospective investors, the report said, citing people familiar with the negotiations.\n    33\t- e2404 [fact; Latitude Media; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:74b684fc710dce162d8f4fe1 2026-08-24] NVIDIA announced a strategic investment in Lancium and a $1.5 billion investment in SB Energy in August 2026.\n    34\t    Quote: Also this month, the company announced both a strategic investment in Lancium , a Blackstone-backed AI data center developer integrating grid interconnection, behind-the-meter generation, and storage, and a $1.5-billion investment in SB Energy , which builds “power-first” AI infrastructure.\n    35\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1957; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    36\t- e2620 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported $99 billion of equity investments and $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of 2026-07-26.\n    37\t    Quote: These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.\n    38\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2929, e3258, e3477, e3889, e3917; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    39\t- e2643 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted in the CNBC interview transcript; original source Jensen Huang 2026-08-26; from extra:b23f73a08a535993119430f5 2026-08-26] Jensen Huang said NVIDIA’s large investments in AI labs in the current year were not expected to repeat next year.\n    40\t    Quote: And those large investments in AI labs are not going to repeat.\n    41\t- e2687 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Bank of America ; from gnews:0c8197cb92878f4b5dd1fe12 2026-08-26] Bank of America estimates NVIDIA has about $300 billion of committed capital to date, comprising about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n    42\t    Quote: The firm estimates roughly $300 billion of committed capital to date, including about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n    43\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2095; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    44\t- e2889 [party_statement; Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:aec1fae1b903de5c43ba7fdf 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said on Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of its fiscal year, in addition to $47.9 billion held in private companies.\n    45\t    Quote: The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year, on top of $47.9 billion it already holds in private companies.\n    46\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2960; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    47\t- e3455 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:7a5939361ddb9a65196dec06 2026-08-30] NVIDIA reportedly shifted from considering spending billions of dollars on Perplexity technology licences and employees to discussing an equity investment in Perplexity.\n    48\t    Quote: In early negotiations, Nvidia had planned to spend billions of dollars to purchase licenses for some of Perplexity's technology and absorb some of its employees. But the talks ultimately pivoted toward a more conventional equity investment path.\n    49\t- e3474 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:baa07a74334dc91adc7d85bc 2026-08-31] NVIDIA is investing $3.5 billion in MediaTek through convertible bonds.\n    50\t    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is investing $3.5 billion in Taiwan-based MediaTek through convertible bonds, taking an existing AI partnership a step further.\n    51\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3558; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    52\t- e3558 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0849c71e57d8603a7cbf08e0 2026-08-31] NVIDIA announced it would buy US$3.5 billion of MediaTek bonds convertible into shares; the deal was announced Monday.\n    53\t    Quote: The deal announced on Monday will see Nvidia buy bonds convertible into MediaTek shares.\n    54\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3474; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    55\t- e3880 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:08b1b514a1f4b7067e0dcda4 2026-09-04] NVIDIA said it committed over $40 billion to financing rounds across the AI stack in 2026.\n    56\t    Quote: The company has ramped up dealmaking for financing rounds across the AI stack in the previous 12 months, with over $40 billion committed in 2026.\n    57\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3890; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    58\t- e3917 [fact; Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's latest earnings report; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6d453f248e1068162655b045 2026-09-04] NVIDIA reported $25 billion in equity investment commitments as of July 26.\n    59\t    Quote: The company also reported a further $25 billion in equity investment commitments as of July 26.\n    60\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2620, e4053; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    61\t- e3934 [rumour; finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:67a905d722fae62095fd741e 2026-09-04] NVIDIA is reportedly discussing an approximately $2.5 billion investment in Thinking Machines Lab; no completion date is stated.\n    62\t    Quote: Nvidia is separately in discussions to invest approximately $2.5 billion\n    63\t- e4053 [fact; calcalistech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed 2026-09-06] NVIDIA reported about $99 billion invested in other companies’ shares and a further $25 billion of future investment commitments at FY2027 Q2 quarter-end.\n    64\t    Quote: The company reported that, as of the end of the quarter, it had about $99 billion invested in shares of other companies, as well as another $25 billion in commitments for future investments.\n    65\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3917; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    66\t- e4486 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] OpenAI announced $110 billion of new investment at a $730 billion pre-money valuation, including $30 billion each from NVIDIA and SoftBank and $50 billion from Amazon.\n    67\t    Quote: In late February, OpenAI announced $110 billion of new investment at a $730 billion pre-money valuation, including $30 billion each from Nvidia and SoftBank Group and $50 billion from Amazon.\n    68\t- e4487 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang said an earlier plan for NVIDIA to invest up to $100 billion in OpenAI was never a commitment.\n    69\t    Quote: Huang said that same month that an earlier, widely discussed plan for Nvidia to invest up to $100 billion in OpenAI \"was never a commitment.\"\n    70\t- e4648 [rumour; Calcalist Tech, citing Reuters; original source Unnamed people familiar with the discussions cited by Reuters ; from gnews:767d795fbdf40bc90ce11e15 2026-09-14] People familiar with discussions told Reuters that Nvidia could invest up to $10 billion in Anthropic’s potential IPO.\n    71\t    Quote: Nvidia could invest as much as $10 billion\n    72\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4669; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    73\t- e4668 [rumour; InvestingNews; original source Reuters ; from gnews:65814ac898f65c6b360be321 2026-09-14] Anthropic is negotiating for NVIDIA to become an anchor investor in its IPO, which could value Anthropic at roughly US$2 trillion; no amount or signing date is stated.\n    74\t    Quote: Anthropic is negotiating to secure NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA) as an anchor investor for a record-breaking initial public offering (IPO) that could value the startup at roughly US$2 trillion.\n    75\t- e4669 [rumour; InvestingNews; original source Reuters ; from gnews:65814ac898f65c6b360be321 2026-09-14] Reuters reported that NVIDIA is weighing a US$10 billion investment commitment in Anthropic's IPO; no signing date is stated.\n    76\t    Quote: According to a Reuters exclusive , the chipmaker is weighing a US$10 billion commitment to the offering, serving as an early backer that could signal strong institutional confidence to public markets preparing to absorb the astronomical valuations and capital demands of frontier artificial intelligence labs.\n    77\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    78\t- e4729 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6261d9e9a1fe8d22c05f5e82 2026-09-15] NVIDIA spent more than $42 billion buying equity stakes in other companies in the first half of FY2027, versus about $1.2 billion in the prior-year first half.\n    79\t    Quote: Nvidia spent more than $42 billion buying equity stakes in other companies in the first half of the fiscal year, up from about $1.2 billion a year earlier.\n    80\t- e4829 [third_party_view; trefis.com; original source trefis.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:106a61a1ef10fb3ba6adb1cd 2026-09-16] The author estimated that NVIDIA had put nearly $50 billion into Frontier AI labs.\n    81\t    Quote: NVIDIA has put nearly $50 billion into the Frontier AI labs\n    82\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2740; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    83\t- e4955 [third_party_view; CRN, quoting GDT executive Chris Kapusta; original source Chris Kapusta, GDT ; from gnews:35efa49dae24bb21af438112 2026-09-18] GDT executive Chris Kapusta said NVIDIA investments have helped some neoclouds access AI systems and enabled GDT to complete neocloud deals.\n    84\t    Quote: But, Kapusta said, Nvidia has also played a significant role in providing the necessary investments for some of these neoclouds to gain access to AI systems. This, in turn, has allowed GDT to get neocloud deals across the finish line.\n    85\t- e4956 [third_party_view; CRN, quoting GDT executive Chris Kapusta; original source Chris Kapusta, GDT ; from gnews:35efa49dae24bb21af438112 2026-09-18] GDT executive Chris Kapusta said about 25% of the roughly 20 neoclouds GDT works with have received NVIDIA equity-investment backing.\n    86\t    Quote: Out of the roughly 20 neoclouds GDT is working with, about 25 percent of them have received backing from Nvidia in the form of equity investments, according to Kapusta.\n    87\t\n    88\t[Themes in force, 8]\n    89\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-18; reasons: The reference is rung 2: NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 guidance assumes no China Data Center compute revenue, which signals no expected near-term sales but does not directly forecast whether Chinese authorities will authorize purchases by 2027-01-31. The evidence still makes selective authorization by the dead\n    90\t- v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: No explicit market consensus or Senate timetable is supplied. The observable reference is that RASA had not passed the Senate as of August 19, which sits at rung 2. The alleged remote-access cases could add pressure for legislation, but the recent evidence reports no Senate action or scheduled vote.\n    91\t- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan's Ministry of Economic Affairs introduce performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evi\n    92\t- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan introduce a criminal ban on AI chip exports to China by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The status-quo reference is rung 2, and the evidence still makes no ban by the deadline more likely than a ban. Taiwan is considering a criminal ban, and the alleged export case—along with lawmakers’ and experts’ stated view that the law needs to change—adds pressure toward legislation. But the supp\n    93\t- v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced U.S. chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is rung 2: the investigation is formally underway and described as likely to remain an ongoing point of contention, with no Commerce deadline or consensus supplied. The access allegations and Taiwanese smuggling enforcement show the issue remains active, but they do not report a Commer\n    94\t- v41 (fed-policy-rate, due 2026-09-30) Will the Federal Reserve raise its policy rate at its September 2026 meeting?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is the fed funds futures market's 86% probability of an increase, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The later fact says the Federal Reserve raised interest rates on September 17, so the outcome at the meeting is Yes; this is not merely a forecast based on the reference.\n    95\t- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-09-18; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the four-company estimate of $720–745 billion, which is rung 4. The newer $760 billion estimate for the four largest hyperscalers also falls in rung 4, so it lifts the implied total within the rung but does not cross the $800 billion boundary. The revised Alphabet and Amazon \n    96\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion; judged 2026-09-18; reasons: The market reference is the $1.08 trillion calendar-2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The implied estimate remains about $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the approximate 730/800 four-to-five share proxy to the $1.3 trillion top-five 2027 estimate. That clears rung 4’s $1.1 t\n    97\t\n    98\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n    35\t- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    36\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    38\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    39\t- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    40\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    41\t- e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n    42\t    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n    43\t- e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n    44\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n    45\t- e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n    46\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n    47\t\n    48\t[Themes in force, 10]\n    49\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities formally authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by Chinese AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or stated deadline for Chinese authorization is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no authorization by January 31, 2027. The evidence now makes rung 1 more likely: reports describe Chinese approval of H200 purchases for selec\n    50\t- v18 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Remote Access Security Act be enacted by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the latest supplied legislative status: the Act passed the House but had not passed the Senate as of August 19, 2026, placing the reference at rung 2 (No); no market-consensus estimate is supplied. With about six weeks remaining at the due date, the implied outcome remains non-enact\n    51\t- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt restrictions on AI chip sales to China by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is Taiwan's stated current policy status: no Taiwan-specific restrictions on AI chip sales to China, which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. The investigation and reports that officials are considering a criminal ban, followed by indictments and seizure of additional\n    52\t- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt performance-threshold controls on exports of advanced AI chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: No market consensus or formal target is supplied; the reference is the status-quo baseline of no adopted controls, rung 2. Consultations and active consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry confirmed that no timeline is set. With the deadline only five months away, the\n    53\t- v32 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Commerce Department announce or adopt restrictions on Chinese AI developers' access to advanced chips through overseas data centers by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the status quo—no stated market consensus or official timetable—and sits at rung 1. The active review and the reported use of overseas data centers make restrictions more plausible, but the evidence still describes examination and alleged access, not a proposed rule or a dated commi\n    54\t- v40 (ai-capex, due 2026-10-05) Will the relevant regulator approve CME Group’s planned futures contracts tied to H100 and B200 hourly rental costs by October 5, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-29; reasons: The stated plan points to rung 1: CME intends to launch on October 5, conditional on approval. But the evidence in force makes approval by that date less likely than not: the CFTC is still seeking comment on whether the market is sufficiently standardized and transparent, and that comment period run\n    55\t- v49 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-02-28) How much will global semiconductor sales total in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 High; judged 2026-09-16; reasons: The market reference is the WSTS and Gartner forecasts, both of which place 2026 sales in rung 4. Their quantified forecasts imply $1.51–$1.56 trillion, within rung 4's $1.50 trillion to below $1.60 trillion range; neither crosses a rung boundary. IDC's 52.8% growth forecast lacks a sales base, so i\n    56\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 (e5014). The evidence still implies about $750 billion: the four-company tracking estimate near $745 billion and the separate top-four estimate of $760 billion bracket that level; company capex revision\n    57\t- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is about $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capex, which is rung 3. My implied estimate is just above the $1.1 trillion rung-4 boundary: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast supports a higher spending path, while the four-company, data-center-only scope requires a discount;\n    58\t- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Congress enact legislation phasing out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is No (rung 2): no market consensus or enacted legislation is supplied, and the stated deadline remains the test. The House committee’s proposal is a concrete step toward enactment, but it is not enactment by Congress; with only this evidence, it raises the chance of Yes without making\n    59\t\n    60\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
