{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && for f in S/answers/{claude,codex}.json O/answers/{claude,codex}.json E/answers/codex.json P/answers/claude.json; do echo \"$f\"; wc -l \"$f\"; rg -n '\"'\\\"id\\\": \\\"F8\\\"|\\\"id\\\": \\\"F12\\\"|\\\"id\\\": \\\"F14\\\"|near \\\\\"'$138|data centers account for 90|scheduled funding portion'\"' \\\"\"'$f\" | cut -c1-220; done && nl -ba adjudication/R07.codex.json | head -13 && nl -ba adjudication/R06.codex.json | head -22 && nl -ba adjudication/R01.codex.json | head -30'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && for f in S/answers/{claude,codex}.json O/answers/{claude,codex}.json E/answers/codex.json P/answers/claude.json; do echo \"$f\"; wc -l \"$f\"; rg -n '\"'\\\"id\\\": \\\"F8\\\"|\\\"id\\\": \\\"F12\\\"|\\\"id\\\": \\\"F14\\\"|near \\\\\"'$138|data centers account for 90|scheduled funding portion'\"' \\\"\"'$f\" | cut -c1-220; done && nl -ba adjudication/R07.codex.json | head -13 && nl -ba adjudication/R06.codex.json | head -22 && nl -ba adjudication/R01.codex.json | head -30'","aggregated_output":"S/answers/claude.json\n     320 S/answers/claude.json\n137:      \"id\": \"F8\",\n213:      \"id\": \"F12\",\n251:      \"id\": \"F14\",\nS/answers/codex.json\n     277 S/answers/codex.json\n137:      \"id\": \"F8\",\n213:      \"id\": \"F12\",\n226:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that data centers account for 90% of combined capital expenditure. Zacks identifies AI infrastructure as a large use of the group's spending, and the Meta AI capex estimate is close to\nO/answers/claude.json\n     257 O/answers/claude.json\n156:      \"id\": \"F12\",\nO/answers/codex.json\n     301 O/answers/codex.json\n172:      \"id\": \"F8\",\n190:      \"basis_text\": \"I treat the company's remaining-year figure as the scheduled funding portion of the July balance. I expect nearly all of that portion to fund before fiscal year-end, with a small allowance for ti\nE/answers/codex.json\n     203 E/answers/codex.json\nP/answers/claude.json\n     467 P/answers/claude.json\n137:      \"id\": \"F8\",\n213:      \"id\": \"F12\",\n251:      \"id\": \"F14\",\n390:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: Wall Street consensus of $411 billion for FY2027 (F19) sits in rung 2 ($392-431.2 billion); the 87-88% consensus growth rates (e4990, e5129) on FY2026's $215.9 billion give about $405 bil\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"Zacks supports $745 billion in total spending, describing a “large portion” as directed toward AI infrastructure. Applying the stated 90% share gives $670.5 billion, which is in rung 3. But rung 3 requires a share below about 94%, and the cited passages give no reason to expect the four companies’ share to fall below that cutoff. Meta’s $130 billion in AI spending is about 96% of the midpoint of its total spending guidance, and the AWS growth passage gives no spending breakdown. The 90% assumption is explicit, but the evidence does not support the deduction that puts the forecast in rung 3.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": []\n     9\t}     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v29\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"The $9–20 billion range fits rung 1, but it relies on subtracting $18 billion from the July 26 balance. e2620 reports $25 billion of commitments as of July 26. e2889 says NVIDIA has '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.' Neither passage says the full $18 billion was included in the July balance and will be paid by year-end. The answer presents that assumption as NVIDIA's payment schedule.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F2\", \"F3\", \"F5\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"e2889 writes '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.' F2 preserves the quoted number, scale, sign and immediate qualifier. The defect definition is limited to those features. The unsupported step is treating the commitment as a scheduled payment from the July balance.\"\n    15\t    },\n    16\t    {\n    17\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    18\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    19\t      \"figures\": [\"F10\"],\n    20\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    21\t      \"why\": \"The e1919 quote says 'an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting' milestones. The format rule takes the qualifier from the words immediately before the number, with an exception for 'or more' or 'or less' immediately after it. The quote's 'up to' qualifies the $3 billion total. The passage summary uses 'up to' for the tranche, but the quoted tranche amount has no such qualifier, and F10 preserves its milestone condition.\"\n    22\t    }\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"The Alphabet, Meta and Amazon guidance in e1254, e1549 and e1649 sums to $540–570 billion. Adding the stated $175 billion Microsoft estimate gives $715–745 billion in total capex. Applying the stated 95–100% data-center share gives $679.25–745 billion. The midpoint of $712.125 billion and most of the range fall in rung 4. The exact share remains an assumption. e5014 says only that a 'large portion' goes to AI infrastructure, and e5376 covers five companies.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"broken structure\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F14\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"The format permits 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.' F14 overlaps rung 4 from $700 billion to $745 billion, and the answer explains its choice using the midpoint and the portion of the range in that band.\"\n    15\t    },\n    16\t    {\n    17\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    18\t      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    19\t      \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"],\n    20\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    21\t      \"why\": \"The format says 'outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine.' F8, F9 and F10 are outside totals used to estimate Microsoft's contribution in F6 alongside its spending run-rate. F7 is the only forecast of the combined total that supplies F14. Because F8 helps inform F6, its agreement with F7 carries no independent weight.\"\n    22\t    },\n    23\t    {\n    24\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    25\t      \"type\": \"broken structure\",\n    26\t      \"figures\": [\"F14\"],\n    27\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    28\t      \"why\": \"The format requires 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.' The stated 95% share does allow an outcome in rung 3, and the answer acknowledges that overlap. Its stated reason for choosing rung 4 is that the midpoint and most of the range fall there.\"\n    29\t    }\n    30\t  ]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
