,\n    57\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    58\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    59\t    },\n    60\t    {\n    61\t      \"id\": \"F6\",\n    62\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"committed equity investments\", \"scope\": \"ecosystem partners\", \"period\": \"cumulative to 2026-08-12, period not specified\"},\n    63\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n    59\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n    60\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n    61\t      \"value\": 40,\n    62\t      \"low\": null,\n    63\t      \"high\": null,\n    64\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    65\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n    66\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n    67\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n    68\t      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n    69\t      \"operation\": \"product\",\n    70\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    71\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    72\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    73\t      \"reference_for\": [\"v41\"]\n    74\t    },\n    75\t    {\n    76\t      \"id\": \"F4\",\n    77\t      \"what\": {\n    78\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    79\t        \"measure\": \"revenue realization share\",\n    80\t        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU revenue as a share of the stated CPU opportunity\",\n    81\t        \"period\": \"FY2027\"\n    82\t      },\n    83\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n    84\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n    85\t      \"value\": 85,\n    86\t      \"low\": null,\n    87\t      \"high\": null,\n    88\t      \"unit\": \"%\",\n    89\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n    90\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n    91\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n    92\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    93\t      \"operation\": null,\n    94\t      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that Vera realizes most of the stated CPU opportunity in its initial ramp. Expected deployment across major customers supports a high share, while the evidence gives no sales volumes to justify assigning the entire CPU opportunity to realized Vera revenue.\",\n    95\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\"],\n    96\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    97\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    98\t    },\n    99\t    {\n   100\t      \"id\": \"F5\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"agent_message","text":"1. **Serious — The third run stopped under the rule as registered.** R01 forecast $679.25–745 billion and chose rung 4, which starts at $700 billion. Both readers marked the same defect on F14. That independently triggers the stop at §5 line 252. D742 kept that line and assigned adjudication to disagreements. The adjudicators reviewed F14 because the packet also contained disagreements, and both correctly rejected the F14 mark. Their finding explains the stop without reversing it. The reader brief omitted the range exception. The writer’s schema allowed an overlapping band with a stated reason, and R01 gave one. I replayed the reply in memory. Rung 4 published, and changing only the selection to the disjoint rung 5 triggered S6. [Registered stop](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:244) · [D742 procedure](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:279) · [Reader brief](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:169) · [Writer’s schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/schema.json:329) · [S6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:541) · [R01 adjudications](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R01.codex.json) · [Claude’s adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R01.claude.json)\n\n2. **Wording — The counts reconcile with two corrections to the report.** All 12 replies passed the schema and published on replay, with zero refusals. There were 24 first readings and 12 adjudication readings across six packets. Both adjudicators rejected all eight individual defect marks, covering seven distinct allegations because F14 was marked twice. R02 and R09 account for **two** midpoint marks. Five replies used the permitted midpoint form. “No split remains” applies to defect marks only. Across 14 forecasts, 12 were supported, R07 was unsupported, and R06 stayed split. Every case had a usable reply on those readings, and both joint replies supported both forecasts. The 109 Part A checks also passed on replay. [Checker results](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json) · [Tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:382) · [Report](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:13) · [Part A results](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small-cases.log:110)\n\n3. **Serious — R07 exposes a forecast that can publish without enough evidence. It adds zero registered stops.** The arithmetic is right. At $745 billion of total spending, the 90% share gives $670.5 billion, and a share of about 94% reaches rung 4. Both adjudicators found the evidence too weak to justify the cut to 90%. I agree with that narrower conclusion. Meta’s figures are for one company, the nearly $800 billion estimate covers five, and the four-company $730 billion figure measures AI infrastructure. Those are reasons to question 90%, with no firm lower bound for the exact four-company data-center share. R01 supplied the usable reply that S needed. Carry R07 forward as a disclosed example of what the eventual paid measurement needs to reveal. [R07 calculation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json:213) · [Source passages](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:29) · [R07 adjudications](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json:3) · [Claude’s adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.claude.json:1) · [Usability rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:263)\n\n4. **Serious — R01 has a separate problem that the range ruling leaves open.** It labels Zacks’s $720–745 billion of **all** capital spending as the market reference for **data-center** spending. The writer’s instructions ask for a reference for the question itself. Claude’s first reading noticed the mismatch in its explanation, and neither reader filed it as a named defect. The same outside tally also helps set the Microsoft estimate, so agreement with the tally adds little confidence. The adjudicators addressed that dependence when they rejected the two-forecasts mark. The reference mismatch adds zero stops to the recorded vote, but it matters because R01 is S’s sole usable reply. Fresh readers need to judge its substance as well as its range. [R01 reference](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json:137) · [Zacks passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:83) · [Writer’s reference instruction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/system.txt:4) · [First reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R01.claude.json:1) · [Adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R01.codex.json:17)\n\n5. **Minor — Two disclosures deserve more precise wording.** Both O replies depend on reading the $18 billion as funding from the July balance. R04 states the overlap and payment timing as estimates. R06 presents the $18 billion as the company’s schedule, and the adjudicators split on whether that reading supports the forecast. R11 also contains a prose error that a reader did catch. A $138 billion fourth quarter gives $428.32 billion for the year, below the $431.2 billion cutoff it was offered to illustrate. Its actual forecast still sits in rung 2, and the reader filed no named defect. I found no other confirmed registered stop. [O source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/O/user.txt:36) · [R04 assumption](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/O/answers/codex.json:172) · [R06 adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R06.codex.json:3) · [R11 reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R11.claude.json:1)\n\n6. **Recommendation — Take the recorded STOP through Council B and ask Alex to authorize option (a).** Register the schema’s exact range rule in both reading instructions, then have fresh readers and adjudicators review all 12 original replies. Give them reshuffled IDs and access only to their packets. Keep prior votes, the answer key, and the expected result from them. Register the wording and scoring before release, with blinded controls for an explained overlap and a disjoint band. Reusing the replies can bias the people choosing the correction. Those controls and the full reread test whether the correction works beyond the case that prompted it. Option (b) would introduce fresh writer variation into a question about how the same answers were read. Option (c) is the right approval step, with the original STOP on the record. A clean reread can support a separate paid-run request to Alex. [Council B requirement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11318) · [Authority after a registered stop](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:339) · [Paid-run decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:267)\n\n   **Falsifier.** If the corrected reading rejects the valid overlap or accepts the disjoint control, the repair fails. If the reread hits any registered stop, including S losing its sole usable reply, the result stays with the council."}}
